Here's a basic function in Java that takes an array and an integer as input and returns a new array containing only the elements of the original array that are greater than or equal to K.

```Java
import java.util.*;

public class ArrayFilter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {4, 2, 10, 15, 8, 7}; 
        int K = 9;
        arr = filterArray(arr, K);

        for (int i : arr)
            System.out.println(i);
    }

    private static int[] filterArray(int[] arr, int K) {
        // edge cases of empty arrays or arrays with only one unique value
        if(arr == null || arr.length == 0 || containsOneUniqueValue(arr)) 
        return arr;

        ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int num: arr) {
            if (num >= K) 
            list.add(num);
        }

        // Convert ArrayList to array
        int[] filteredArr = new int[list.size()];
        for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) 
        filteredArr[i] = (int) list.get(i);

        return filteredArr;
    }

    private static boolean containsOneUniqueValue(int[] arr) {
        int uniqueValue = arr[0];
        for (int num : arr) {
            if (num != uniqueValue)
            return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

In this program:
- The main function initializes the array and K, then calls the filterArray method passing in the array and K.
- The filterArray function, if the array is empty or if all the elements in the array are same, it returns the original array itself.
- Else, it creates an ArrayList and adds elements that are greater than or equal to K.
- It then converts the ArrayList back into an array and returns this array.
- The additional function containsOneUniqueValue is used to check if all the values in the array are the same. If they are, it returns true, else false.

