class Node {
   int data;
   Node next;

   Node(int d) {
      data = d;
      next = null;
   }
}

class LinkedList {
   Node head;

   public void push(int new_data) {
      Node new_node = new Node(new_data);
      new_node.next = head;
      head = new_node;
   }

   void printList() {
      Node temp = head;
      while (temp != null) {
         System.out.print(temp.data + " ");
         temp = temp.next;
      }
      System.out.println();
   }

   public static Node sortedMerge(Node list1, Node list2) {
      if (list1 == null) {
         return list2;
      }
      if (list2 == null) {
         return list1;
      }

     if (list1.data <= list2.data) {
         list1.next = sortedMerge(list1.next, list2);
         return list1;
      } else {
         list2.next = sortedMerge(list1, list2.next);
         return list2;
      }
   }

   public static void main(String[] args) {
      LinkedList list1 = new LinkedList();
      LinkedList list2 = new LinkedList();

      list1.push(10);
      list1.push(8);
      list1.push(5);
      list1.push(4);
      list1.push(1);

      list2.push(9);
      list2.push(7);
      list2.push(6);
      list2.push(3);
      list2.push(2);

      list1.head = sortedMerge(list1.head, list2.head);

      list1.printList();
   }
}
```
In the code above, LinkedList is a class that represents a linked list. Each node in the list has an integer value and a link to the next node. The LinkedList class also provide methods to add data to the list (push), print the list (printList) and merge two sorted lists (sortedMerge).

