The problem arises because the shared `totalCost` variable is being accessed unsafely concurrently. Comprehensive Java code for the problem would use the synchronized method or block to handle the shared change of `totalCost` safely. Here is the corrected Java code:

```java
public class PurchaseCalculation extends Thread {

    private String client;
    final Object lock = new Object();

    /* Pair of Unit_cost and Items_count */
    private int[][] pairs;
    private static volatile int totalCost = 0;

    public PurchaseCalculation(String client, int[][] pairs) {
        this.client = client;
        this.pairs = pairs;
    }


    @Override
    public void run() {
        calculate();
    }

    public void calculate() {

        int clientCost = 0;

        for (int[] pair : pairs) {
            clientCost += pair[0] * pair[1];
        }

        /* Lock the block where 'totalCost` variable is changed */
        synchronized (lock) {
            totalCost += clientCost;
        }

         System.out.println("Client: " + client + ", Total Purchase Cost: $" + clientCost);
    }

    public static int getTotalCost() {
        return totalCost;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[][] pairs1 = {{30, 2}, {20, 4}, {15, 1}};
        int[][] pairs2 = {{25, 3}, {10, 5}, {5, 3}};

        Thread thread1 = new PurchaseCalculation("Client1", pairs1);
        Thread thread2 = new PurchaseCalculation("Client2", pairs2);

        /* Start Threads */
        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();

        /* Wait for threads to end */
        try {
            thread1.join();
            thread2.join();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Interrupted");
        }

        System.out.println("Total Purchase Cost (All Clients): $" + PurchaseCalculation.getTotalCost());
    }
}
```

In the above code, `PurchaseCalculation` extends `Thread` class. It takes 'client' and 'pairs' (array of 'Unit_cost' and 'Items_count') for each client. Inside `calculate` method, for each 'pair', clientCost is calculated and added to `totalCost` under the synchronized block. The added clientCost is made atomic and hence thread-safe using synchronized block which obtains lock on a shared object `lock`. The `main` method initializes two threads `thread1` and `thread2` that begin execution with `start()`. The `join` method is used to ensure that the main thread will wait for `thread1` and `thread2` to finish their execution before it continues. Total purchase cost for all clients is outputted at the end.

Algorithm Name: Sieve of Eratosthenes

The Sieve of Eratosthenes is an ancient algorithm used to find all primes less than N and is efficient when you need to find all primes in a given range. 

Here's a simple implementation of the Sieve of Eratosthenes:

1. Begin by creating a boolean array prime[ ] of size N+1 and initialize all entries as true. Entries in prime[] will eventually be false if they are not a prime, else remain true.

2. Now, iterate over each number starting from 2 to sqrt(N) and for every number check if it is unmarked.

```
for(int p=2; p*p<=n; p++) 
{ 
   // If prime[p] is not changed, then it is a prime 
   if(prime[p] == true) 
   { 
       // Update all multiples of p to be not prime
       for(int i=p*p; i<=n; i += p) 
           prime[i] = false; 
   } 
} 
```
3. At the end of this, array prime[] will hold the desired output. All i such that prime[i] is true will be prime numbers.

4. Print all prime numbers.
```
for(int i=2; i<=n; i++) 
   if(prime[i]) 
      printf("%d ", i); 
```

This algorithm can be further optimized, but this offers a starting point and a fair understanding of the process.

Keep in mind that this algorithm finds all primes less than N. If you need primes within a range, say, m to N, you can run this algorithm for the range, adjust the indices appropriately (subtract m to index into your boolean array), and only start marking multiples as composite when the composite number >= m. Another way is to run the algorithm from 2 to N and ignore the primes which are less than m.

