Below, I've written a simple C# class that can generate unique, thread-safe Base64 IDs of 20 characters. It uses a ConcurrentDictionary to guarantee thread-safety and uniqueness.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;

public class UniqueBase64IDGenerator
{
    private static readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, byte> _uniqueIds = 
						new ConcurrentDictionary<string, byte>();

    private static readonly int _bytesLength = 15; 
    // We select 15 because the Base64 string representation of 15 bytes 
    // is a 20 character long string

    public static string GenerateUniqueBase64ID()
    {
        string uniqueBase64ID;

        using (var rng = new RNGCryptoServiceProvider())
        {
            byte[] randomNumber = new byte[_bytesLength];

            do
            {
                rng.GetBytes(randomNumber);
                uniqueBase64ID = Convert.ToBase64String(randomNumber);
            }
            while (!_uniqueIds.TryAdd(uniqueBase64ID, byte.MinValue));
        }

        return uniqueBase64ID;
    }
}
```
This composition uses a ConcurrentDictionary to ensure uniqueness as well as provide lock-free thread safety. Cryptographically strong randomness is provided by RNGCryptoServiceProvider. Inside the loop, a new random identifier of the required length is generated and then it tries to add it to the ConcurrentDictionary. If the addition was successful, it means that the identifier was not in the dictionary before and it is unique.

Remember: 15 bytes provide 120 bits of security and converted to Base64 format it gives a 20 character string (every 3 bytes are represented by 4 Base64 characters). In many cases 120 bits is more than enough, but if you need more, you can increase the byte length. Be aware, increasing the byte length also makes the resulting Base64 string longer.

Also, in practical situations where it is possible that all unique IDs will be spent (it's unlikely with a high number of bits, but if you decrease them, it's more possible) you need to consider a mechanism of "renewing" used identifiers. In this implementation, the IDs will be stored until application is closed or while there is enough space in the memory. Make sure to validate the requirement and apply cleanup or storing used ids in a long-term storage if needed.

