Here is a simple solution. This function is implemented by recursively finding prime numbers and printing every third prime within a given range.

```c++
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

// Function to check if a number is prime
bool isPrime(int n, int i = 2)
{
    if (n <= 2)
        return (n == 2) ? true : false;
    if (n % i == 0)
        return false;
    if (i * i > n)
        return true;

    // Check for next divisor
    return isPrime(n, i + 1);
}

// Recursive function to print every 3rd prime number
void printThirdPrime(int n, int i = 2, int count = 0)
{
    if(i>n) return; // Base case: End recursion when reaching end of range

    // If current number is prime, increment the count
    if(isPrime(i)){
        count++;
    }

    // If current prime number is the third one, print it
    if(count % 3 == 0 && isPrime(i)){
        cout << i << " ";
    }

    // Call the recursive function for the next number in the range
    printThirdPrime(n, i + 1, count);
}

int main()
{
    printThirdPrime(100); // Call the function for range 1 to 100
    return 0;
}
```

The time complexity of this solution can be considered as O(N^2). For every number in the range (1 to N), we are checking if it is a prime number, which in worst case can take O(N) time. Hence time complexity becomes O(N*N). A more optimized approach can be using the Sieve of Eratosthenes but it's not applicable here because of recursive approach requirement.

The space complexity of the code is O(1), which means that the space required by the algorithm is constant, i.e., it does not change with the increase in the size of the input array, excluding space for the recursion stack. The recursion depth can go up to N in the worst case scenario in recursive call stack, resulting in O(N) space complexity considering the recursion call stack space.

