In Java, you can use the Collections.synchronizedList() method which returns a synchronized (thread-safe) list backed by the specified list.

Here is your Java code snippet:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> synchronizedArrayList = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<String>());

        Thread thread1 = new Thread() {
            public void run() {
                for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
                    synchronizedArrayList.add("Thread 1 Added Element " + i);
                    System.out.println("Thread 1 Added Element " + i);
                }
            }
        };

        Thread thread2 = new Thread() {
            public void run() {
                for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
                    synchronizedArrayList.add("Thread 2 Added Element " + i);
                    System.out.println("Thread 2 Added Element " + i);
                }
            }
        };

        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();

        // wait for threads to end
        try {
            thread1.join();
            thread2.join();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Interrupted");
        }

        System.out.println("\nArrayList Contents:");
        // iterator should be used in a synchronized block
        synchronized(synchronizedArrayList) {
            Iterator<String> iterator = synchronizedArrayList.iterator(); 
            while (iterator.hasNext())
                System.out.println(iterator.next());
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we have a synchronized ArrayList which is populated by two threads (`thread1` and `thread2`). Each thread adds 5 elements to the ArrayList. After both threads finish their execution, we print the contents of the ArrayList. The synchronized block is needed when we iterate over the ArrayList to avoid concurrent modification exceptions.

Here is a simple implementation of a Student class in Java. The setId and setEmail methods include some basic input validation.

```java
public class Student {
    private String studentId;
    private String course;
    private String email;

    public Student(String studentId, String course, String email) {
        setId(studentId);
        setCourse(course);
        setEmail(email);
    }

    public String getStudentId() {
        return studentId;
    }

    public void setId(String studentId) {
        if (studentId == null || studentId.isEmpty() || !studentId.matches("[0-9]+")) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid student ID. It must not be null or empty and should only contain digits.");
        }
        this.studentId = studentId;
    }

    public String getCourse() {
        return course;
    }

    public void setCourse(String course) {
        this.course = course;
    }

    public String getEmail() {
        return email;
    }

    public void setEmail(String email) {
        if(email == null || email.isEmpty() || !email.matches("^[A-Za-z0-9+_.-]+@(.+)$")) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid email format.");
        }
        this.email=email;
    }
}
```
Please consider the following when integrating this into your project:

- This implementation assumes that a valid student ID is non-empty and only consists of digits. You may need to adjust the regular expression in the setter if your student IDs follow a different format.
- The email validator used here is very basic and can be improved to more accurately check the format of email addresses. It might also be desirable to use a library for this to account for the complexity of the rules.

