Approach:
We can use the depth-first search algorithm to detect cycles in a directed graph. The base idea is to use DFS traversal to mark each visited node and also track the recursion stack. If a path of a node leads back to itself, there is a cycle. 

If we find a node which is already in the recursion stack, it means there is a cycle (as DFS visited nodes by diving into recursion, the node is in the recursive call stack before we finish all recursion). However, if there is a node that has already been visited but it is not present in the recursion stack, it means we have visited the node, but we have finished all the branches of the tree rooted at this node. It is not part of the current path, thus we ignore this node.

For displaying individual cycles, we can keep a parent array and as soon as we detect a cycle, we backtrack using the parent array and print the cycle. But two cycles with some common vertices will not be printed distinctly as soon as we find a back edge we print the cycle which might include other cycles as well.

In cases of disconnected graphs (graphs with isolated nodes or separate components), DFS naturally handles these cases by implicitly implementing a loop over all the nodes of the graph for the DFS subroutine call. This ensures that every node will be visited, whether it's connected with the main graph or not.

For complicated network structures with multiple disconnected cycles, our above approach still holds true.

Java Code:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Stack;

public class Graph {
    static class Node {
        int dest;
        Node(int dest) {
            this.dest = dest;
        }
    }

    List<List<Node>> adjList;
    int nodes;

    Graph(int nodes) {
        adjList = new ArrayList<>();
        this.nodes = nodes;
        for(int i = 0; i < nodes; i++) {
            adjList.add(i, new ArrayList<>());
        }
     }

     public void addEdge(int source, int dest) {
        adjList.get(source).add(new Node(dest));
     }

     public void detectAndPrintAllCycles() {
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[nodes];
        boolean[] dfsVisited = new boolean[nodes];
        for(int i = 0; i < nodes; i++) {
            if(!visited[i]) {
                if(detectAllCyclesUtil(i, visited, dfsVisited)) {
                    return; // stop as cycle is found
                }
            }
        }
     }

     public boolean detectAllCyclesUtil(int index,boolean[] visited, boolean[] dfsVisited){
        visited[index] = true;
        dfsVisited[index] = true;

        List<Node> childNodes = adjList.get(index);
        for(Node child : childNodes) {
            if(!visited[child.dest]) {
                if(detectAllCyclesUtil(child.dest, visited, dfsVisited)) {
                    System.out.println("Cycle present at node: " + child.dest);
                    return true; // cycle detected
                }
            } else if (dfsVisited[child.dest]) {
                System.out.println("Cycle present at node: " + child.dest);
                return true; // cycle detected
            }
        }
        dfsVisited[index] = false;
        return false;
     }
}
```

This code will handle and print the node where cycle is detected while DFS traversal. We have to specify here that the explicit removal of cycles is not possible without making changes to the actual graph connections. As is, this code will only identify these cycles. If you want to remove a cycle, you will have to decide how to break the structure to eliminate loops. This depends on the specific rules and tolerance levels of the application.

