The problem in the provided code is typically known as a race condition. Race conditions occur in multithreaded applications when two threads access shared data simultaneously and at least one thread modifies the data. As a result, the final value of the shared data (in this case `sharedNumber`) will depend on scheduling and timing of thread execution that leads to unpredictable output.

To resolve this issue, we can use the `synchronized` keyword in Java which only allows one thread to enter the portion of the code that has been synchronized. However calling synchronized methods will have a performance impact so it's best to try to minimize the code inside the synchronized block.

Here is the code:

```java
public class Multiplier {
    private float sharedNumber = 1.0f;

    public synchronized float MultiplyFirstPair(float a, float b) {
            // Local variable to store the result temporarily
            float multResult = a * b;

            // Updating the shared number
            sharedNumber *= multResult;
            return multResult;
    }

    public synchronized float MultiplySecondPair(float a, float b) {
            // Local variable to store the result temporarily
            float multResult = a * b;

            // Updating the shared number
            sharedNumber *= multResult;
            return multResult;
    }

    public synchronized float FinalMultiplication(float a){
        return sharedNumber * a;
    }
}
```

This solution ensures that any thread that is performing any multiplication operation completes its task before any other thread can start a new multiplication. However, this solution may not entirely optimal, if the first pair and the second pair of numbers to multiply are independent (i.e., the second pair doesn't rely on the result of the first pair), the operations could theoretically be performed simultaneously. The optimal solution will ultimately depend on the specific requirements of your application.

