```java
public class SharedResource {
    private int counter = 0;

    public synchronized void increment(String threadName) {
        counter++;
        System.out.println("Thread " + threadName + " incremented the counter to " + counter);

        if (counter > 10) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Too many increments");
        }
    }
}

public class IncrementThread extends Thread {
    private SharedResource sharedResource;

    public IncrementThread(SharedResource sharedResource) {
        this.sharedResource = sharedResource;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        while (true) {
            try {
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(1000);
                    sharedResource.increment(Thread.currentThread().getName());
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
                System.out.println("Exception caught: " + e.getMessage());
                break;
            } finally {
                System.out.println("Executing finally block");
            }
        }
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SharedResource sharedResource = new SharedResource();

        new IncrementThread(sharedResource).start();
        new IncrementThread(sharedResource).start();
    }
}
```
The `SharedResource` is a shared resource shared among multiple threads. The increment method is `synchronized` to prevent race conditions, because it modifies the counter property which is shared by multiple threads.

In `IncrementThread`, we start a new thread that loops infinitely and increments the `SharedResource`'s counter every second. Exception handling is incorporated in this loop.

The outer `try` block is used to catch `IllegalStateException`. This is a custom exception that is thrown when the counter is greater than 10. If this exception is caught, the thread stops its execution (`break;`)

Inside the `try` block, there is a nested `try-catch` block. It catches `InterruptedException` which can be thrown by `Thread.sleep(1000);`. This is thrown when another thread has interrupted the current thread.

Finally, blocks are always executed regardless of the success of the `try` and `catch` blocks. In this case, it's used to print "Executing finally block".

This design ensures that exceptions are caught as soon as possible and handled properly. Keep in mind, however, that the granularity and complexity of exception handling should be appropriate to your program's specific needs. Overly complex exception handling can negatively impact your program's performance and readability.

