To create a sequence of seven random ASCII alphanumeric characters, you need to generate random values within the range that corresponds to ASCII alphanumeric characters. Here is the correct JavaScript code that only generates a sequence of seven random alphanumeric characters:

```JavaScript
function generateSequence() {
    let asciiStarts = [48, 65, 97];
    let asciiLengths = [10, 26, 26];
    let sequence = "";
    for(let i = 0; i < 7; i++) {
        let setIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * asciiStarts.length);
        sequence += String.fromCharCode(Math.floor(Math.random() * asciiLengths[setIndex]) + asciiStarts[setIndex]);
    }
    return sequence;
}

console.log(generateSequence());
```

In this code, `asciiStarts` and `asciiLengths` are used to store the starting ASCII values and lengths of the number, uppercase letter, and lowercase letter groups respectively. A random start point is chosen (`setIndex`), and a random character from that set is appended to `sequence`.

Once a string is created in JavaScript, it is by default immutable, meaning it cannot be changed. If you try to change the string, what actually happens is a new string is created with the changes.

As for each time the program runs it should generate a new random sequence, this is automatically guaranteed by `Math.random()`. Every time you call it, it will generate a new random number.

The last requirement, to validate the generated sequence, could be accomplished using a regular expression, as shown in the code below:

```JavaScript
function validateSequence(sequence) {
    const regex = /^[a-z0-9]{7}$/i;
    return regex.test(sequence);
}

// example usage:
