Here is a simple Java program to solve the problem using a helper function `isFibonacci(int n)` that checks if a number is a Fibonacci number. Another helper function, `isEven(int n)`, checks if a number is even. If sum exceeds maximum integer, a custom `ExceedMaxIntException` is thrown.

```java
class ExceedMaxIntException extends Exception {
    ExceedMaxIntException(String s) {
        super(s);
    }
}

public class Main {

    static boolean isPerfectSquare(int x) {
        int s = (int) Math.sqrt(x);
        return (s*s == x);
    }

    static boolean isFibonacci(int n) {
        return isPerfectSquare(5*n*n + 4) || isPerfectSquare(5*n*n - 4);
    }

    static boolean isEven(int n) {
        return n % 2 == 0;
    }

    static int sumFibonacciEven(int[] array) throws ExceedMaxIntException {
        int sum = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
            if (isFibonacci(array[i]) && isEven(array[i])) {
                if(Integer.MAX_VALUE - sum < array[i]){
                     throw new ExceedMaxIntException("Sum exceeded maximum integer value") ;
                }
                sum += array[i];
            }
        }

        return sum;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {8, -7, 4, -3, 19, 22};
        try {
            System.out.println("Sum: " + sumFibonacciEven(array));
        }
        catch (ExceedMaxIntException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }

}
```

This assumes that by "Fibonacci numbers" we mean non-negative integers in the Fibonacci sequence, so negative numbers and non-integers are excluded. It's worth noting that this will be quite inefficient for large array sizes or large numbers, because an integer n is in the Fibonacci sequence if and only if 5n^2 + 4 or 5n^2 - 4 is a perfect square, so this approach involves checking many numbers to see if they are perfect squares. A more efficient approach would be to instead iteratively generate the Fibonacci sequence up to the maximum value in the array, and check each array element against the generated sequence.

