Here's a simple implementation of a class that performs circular shift on the given array.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm> 

template <typename T>
class CircularShift {
public:
    std::vector<T> array;
    int shift_count;

    CircularShift(const std::vector<T>& array, int shift_count) {
        if(shift_count < 0) {
            throw std::out_of_range("Negative shift count is not allowed");
        }
        this->array = array;
        this->shift_count = shift_count;
    }

    void performShift() {
        std::rotate(this->array.begin(), this->array.begin()+this->shift_count, this->array.end()); 
    }

    void display() {
        for (const auto &i: this->array) {
            std::cout << i << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << std::endl;
    }
};

int main() {

    int n, k;
    std::cout << "Enter the size of the array: ";
    std::cin >> n;

    std::cout << "Enter the shift count: ";
    std::cin >> k;

    std::vector<int> array(n);

    std::cout << "Enter elements of array: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        std::cin >> array[i];
    }

    CircularShift<int> cs(array, k);

    try {
        cs.performShift();
        cs.display();
    } catch(const std::exception& e) {
        std::cerr << e.what() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;

}
```
This program creates a templated class (`CircularShift`) that takes the array and the shift count as input. The `performShift` function uses the STL `rotate` algorithm to perform the circular shift operation. The `display` function displays the elements of the shifted array. The shift count is checked for validity in the constructor of the class - if a negative shift count is given, an exception is thrown.

Please note:
- This implementation assumes that 'shift_count' is counted from the beginning of the array. If you want to count from the end, you'll have to adjust the `std::rotate` call accordingly.
- Exceptions need to be handled in a `try...catch` block where these functions are used. In this case, it is done in the `main` function. In real-world code, you may want to handle these exceptions where it makes the most sense.

