Here is a Java code sample that implements a multithreaded Singleton class which performs lazy initialization, serialization, ensures thread safety and incorporates synchronized methods with double-checked locking. 

```java
import java.io.Serializable;

public class SingletonClass implements Serializable {

    // Volatile keyword ensures that changes made in one thread are 
    // immediately reflect in other threads
    private static volatile SingletonClass singletonInstance;

    // private constructor to restrict instantiation of the class from other classes.
    private SingletonClass() {
        // Prevents formation of another instance by reflection
        if (singletonInstance != null) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Use getInstance() method to get the single instance of this class.");
        }
    }

    // Synchronized Method with double checked locking principle
    public static SingletonClass getInstance() {
        if (singletonInstance == null) {
            // Double checked locking principle
            // If there is no instance then create one
            synchronized (SingletonClass.class) {
                if (singletonInstance == null) { 
                    singletonInstance = new SingletonClass();
                }
            }
        }

        return singletonInstance;
    }

    // Method to prevent multiple objects creation during deserialization.
    protected SingletonClass readResolve() {
        return getInstance();
    }
}
```

In this code:

- The volatile keyword guarantees that multiple threads handle the `singletonInstance` variable correctly when it is being initialized.
- Double-checked locking principle is used in the `getInstance()` method to reduce the overhead of acquiring a lock by testing the locking criterion before acquiring the lock.
- The `readResolve()` method is used to ensure that a new object is not created when the singleton class is deserialized.

IMPORTANT: The Singleton design pattern is a very useful software design pattern where you need to use only a single instance of a class. However, it is also considered an anti-pattern by many designers as it can introduce global state into an application and hide dependencies. Use it judiciously.

