Below is a small glimpse of how such a Svelte store could be architected, using the JavaScript programming language. I've commented on the code for clarity.

```javascript
import { writable } from 'svelte/store';

// Create a raw 'notifications' store
const notifications = writable([]);

// Define a new store that wraps around the raw notifications store
// and provides specialized behavior.
const greetingsStore = {
  subscribe: notifications.subscribe, 

  // Method to add a notification.
  // The new notification is simply appended to the existing
  // notifications array.
  notify: (name) => {
    const now = new Date();
    const timestamp = `${now.getHours()}:${now.getMinutes()}:${now.getSeconds()}`;

    notifications.update(n => {
      return [...n, { name, timestamp }];
    });
  },

  // Often the app needs to display notifications in reverse order.
  // Instead of constantly reversing the notifications array in the
  // components, it's more efficient to do it once here.
  reversed: {
    subscribe: (run, invalidate) => notifications.subscribe(n => {
      // To get the notifications in reverse order, we just reverse the array.
      run(n.slice().reverse());
    }, invalidate),
  },

  // Likewise, we can provide a specialized subscription for,
  // e.g., only showing notifications with certain names.
  withName: (nameFilter) => ({
    subscribe: (run, invalidate) => notifications.subscribe(n => {
      // Filter out notifications without the given name.
      run(n.filter(({ name }) => name === nameFilter));
    }, invalidate),
  })
};

export default greetingsStore;
```

