Coloring text in the console using C++ isn't straightforward, as console output color control is not part of the language standard. However, operating systems provide certain ways to achieve this. A popular approach is to use console ANSI escape code for Unix-based systems, and the SetConsoleTextAttribute() function for Windows.

You can find below a couple of examples:

1. Unix-based Systems (macOS, Linux, etc.)

```cpp
#include <iostream>

#define RESET   "\033[0m"
#define BLACK   "\033[30m"
#define RED     "\033[31m"

int main() {
    std::cout << RED << "This text will be red" << RESET << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In this code, we define several color control codes using ANSI escape sequences, and then use them to change the color of the text in the stdout.

2. Windows OS:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <windows.h>

int main() {
    HANDLE hConsole = GetStdHandle(STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE);

    SetConsoleTextAttribute(hConsole, FOREGROUND_GREEN);
    std::cout << "This text will be green" << std::endl;

    // Reset to white color
    SetConsoleTextAttribute(hConsole, FOREGROUND_RED | FOREGROUND_GREEN | FOREGROUND_BLUE);
}
```

In Windows, we use the SetConsoleTextAttribute function to set the color. We first get the console output handle and then pass it with the color code to the function.

Note that these are operating system-specific solutions. Your program won't be portable i.e., a program written for Unix-based systems will not run on Windows without modification and vice-versa.

To make code multi-platform, you'd need to use preprocessors to determine which OS your code is being compiled on and then use the corresponding method.

```cpp
#include <iostream>

#ifdef _WIN32
    #include <windows.h>
    #define FOREGROUND_YELLOW 14
    #define FOREGROUND_WHITE 15
#else
    #define FOREGROUND_YELLOW "\033[33m"
    #define FOREGROUND_WHITE "\033[0m"
#endif

int main(){
    #ifdef _WIN32
        HANDLE hConsole = GetStdHandle(STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE);
        SetConsoleTextAttribute(hConsole, FOREGROUND_YELLOW);
        std::cout << "This text will be yellow in Windows" << std::endl;
        SetConsoleTextAttribute(hConsole, FOREGROUND_WHITE);
    #else
        std::cout << FOREGROUND_YELLOW << "This text will be yellow in Unix-based OS" << FOREGROUND_WHITE << std::endl;
    #endif

    return 0;
}
```

In this code, we use `_WIN32` macro to check if we are on a Windows system. If we are, we include `windows.h` and use Windows-specific colors and functions. If we are not (else), then we use Unix-specific colors and functions.

