Here is a Python-based algorithm that includes the use of a dictionary for synonyms and a hash map (another dictionary in Python) for counting word frequencies. Note that this solution assumes the function get_synonyms(word) is already defined and that it returns a list of synonyms for the input word. 

The function "get_normal_form(word)" is used to normalize the synonyms to the common form.

```python
from collections import defaultdict
from collections import Counter

# Assume this is our dictionary of synonyms
synonyms_dict = {
    "adore": ["love","cherish"],
    "love": ["adore","cherish"],
    "cherish": ["adore","love"],
}

def get_synonyms(word):
    return synonyms_dict[word]

def get_normal_form(word):
    if word in synonyms_dict:
        return min(synonyms_dict[word])
    else:
        return word

def count_synonyms(text):
    words = text.split()
    # Normalize all words to their common form
    words = [get_normal_form(word) for word in words]
    word_counts = Counter(words)
    return dict(word_counts)

# Test the function
text = "i adore to love but love doesn't cherish to love me back"
print(count_synonyms(text))
```

In the output, "love", "adore", and "cherish" are all counted as "adore" (since "adore" is alphabetically the smallest among them), which appears 4 times.

This solution is efficient in terms of both time and space complexity. Both getting the normal form of a word and counting the frequencies have a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of words in the text. The space complexity is also linear in the number of unique words in the text. Note that the complexity of the algorithm will increase if getting synonyms becomes more complex. As it assumes synonyms can be retrieved in constant time from a predefined dictionary, if synonyms need to be fetched over the network or computed with a more complex algorithm, it would affect the overall efficiency of the solution.

