```jsx
import React from 'react';

class App extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { items: [], text: '' };
    this.deleteItem = this.deleteItem.bind(this);
    this.updateItem = this.updateItem.bind(this);
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <h3>Todo List</h3>
        <input onChange={this.handleChange} value={this.state.text} />
        <button onClick={this.handleSubmit}>Add</button>
        <List items={this.state.items}
              deleteItem={this.deleteItem}
              updateItem={this.updateItem}/>
      </div>
    );
  }

  handleChange = (e) => {
    this.setState({ text: e.target.value });
  }

  handleSubmit = () => {
    if(!this.state.text.length) {
      return;
    }
    const newItem = {
      text: this.state.text,
      id: Date.now()
    };
    this.setState(prevState => ({
      items: prevState.items.concat(newItem),
      text: ''
    }));
  }

  deleteItem(id){
    const remainingItems = this.state.items.filter(item => id !== item.id);
    this.setState({items: remainingItems})
  }

  updateItem(id, value){
    const updatedItems = this.state.items.map(item => {
      if(id === item.id) item.text = value;
      return item;
    });

    this.setState({items: updatedItems})
  }
}

class List extends React.Component {
  render() {
    return (
      <ul>
        {this.props.items.map(item => (
          <li key={item.id}>
            <input type="text" defaultValue={item.text} 
            onBlur={e => {
              this.props.updateItem(item.id, e.target.value)
            }}/>
            <button onClick={() => this.props.deleteItem(item.id)}>Delete</button>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    );
  }
}

export default App;
```
This app maintains an application-level state (within `App`) of a list of items and an input box for adding new items. The `App` component handles all changes to the application state.

In the `List` component, each item displays as a list (`<li>`) with an input box and a delete button. When the input box loses focus (`onBlur`), the update function from `App` is called to update the state with the new value for the item text. If the delete button is clicked, the delete function from `App` is called to remove the item from the state.

Keep in mind that this is a very basic implementation and might not cover all specific use-cases, it does not handle edge cases or input errors like entering an empty item. For a production application, you would include error handling and other optimizations.
