Here's a Java program that sorts an integer array in ascending order using the Bubble Sort algorithm. Bubble sort has an average time complexity of O(n^2), making it suitable for small-sized arrays. For large arrays, you may want to use other more efficient sorting algorithms like Merge Sort or Quick Sort.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {2, 5, 3, 1, 9, 4};
        bubbleSort(arr);

        // Print the sorted array
        for (int i : arr) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
    }

    public static void bubbleSort(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        boolean swapped;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            swapped = false;
            for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
                if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                    // Swap arr[j] and arr[j + 1]
                    int temp = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                    arr[j + 1] = temp;
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }
            // If the inner loop did not swap any elements, the array is already sorted
            if (!swapped) {
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

When you run this code, it will output the sorted array:

```
1 2 3 4 5 9
```

