output:
Here are the issues in the code:

1. Attempting to reverse a 2D array but only swapping rows. The columns within the rows also need to be reversed.
2. The method doesn't actually output or return the reversed array. We won't be able to see the result of the operation.

Here is the corrected and optimized code:

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

         int a[][] = { {1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7 ,8, 9} };
         a = reverseArray(a);
         for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < a[0].length; j++) {
                System.out.print(a[i][j] + " ");
            }
            System.out.println();
         }
    }

    static int[][] reverseArray(int arr[][]){
        int rows = arr.length;
        int cols = arr[0].length;

        // Reverse columns in each row
        for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j < cols/2; j++) {
                int temp = arr[i][j];
                arr[i][j] = arr[i][cols-j-1];
                arr[i][cols-j-1] = temp;
            }
        }

        // Reverse rows
        for (int i = 0; i < rows/2; i++){
            int[] temp = arr[i];
            arr[i] = arr[rows-i-1];
            arr[rows-i-1] = temp;
        }

        return arr;
    }
}
```
This code first reverses the columns within each row of the array and then reverses the rows. The result is a fully reversed 2D array. The reversed array is returned and then printed to the console for verification.

