Here is a JavaScript function that validates an email address based on regex pattern and checks the domain against an array of acceptable domains:

```javascript
function validateEmail(email) {
  // Predefined list of acceptable domains
  var acceptableDomains = ["google.com", "yahoo.com", "hotmail.com", "outlook.com", "gmail.com"];

  try {
    // Use regular expressions to validate email format
    var regex = /^\w+([\.-]?\w+)*@\w+([\.-]?\w+)*(\.\w{2,3})+$/;
    if (regex.test(email)) {
      // Split the email address at the @ sign to get the domain
      var domain = email.split('@')[1];
      // Check if the domain is part of the acceptable domains
      if (acceptableDomains.includes(domain)) {
        console.log("Email is valid and the domain is acceptable.");
      } else {
        console.log("Email is valid but the domain is not acceptable.");
      }
    } else {
      console.log("Email address is invalid.");
    }
  }
  catch(err) {
    // Handle exceptions
    console.log(`An error occurred: ${err.message}`);
  }
}
```

In this function, we first check the validity of the email format using a regular expression. If the format is correct, we proceed to check the domain against a predefined array of acceptable domains. To handle a potential exception such as the email parameter not being a string, we use a `try...catch` statement. If an exception is thrown within the `try` block, the `catch` block will be executed to handle the exception. 

Please note that JavaScript regular expressions for email validation can get extremely complicated due to the sheer variety of valid email address formats according to the official standard, so this regex is a simple version that should work for most common email formats. If you need to handle all valid email formats according to the specification, you might want to consider using a well-maintained email validation library instead.

