print(calculatePostfix(convertToPostfix(infix)))  # output: 20.0
```

The convertToPostfix function goes through the infix notation from left to right. If it encounters a number, it directly adds it to the output. If it encounters an operator, it will check the stack. If the stack is empty or contains a '(' on top, it will push the operator onto the stack. If the incoming operator has higher precedence than the top of the stack, the operator is pushed on top of the stack. Else, operators are popped from the stack and added to the output list until we see an operator with a lower precedence or the stack is emptied. If it encounters a '(', it pushes it onto the stack. If it encounters a ')', it pops the operators from the stack and adds them to the output until it encounters a '('.

The calculatePostfix function also goes through the postfix notation from left to right. If it encounters a number, it pushes it to the stack. If it encounters an operator, it pops two numbers from the stack, does the operation, and then pushes the result back to the stack. At the end of the expression, the stack will hold the result. 

