Here is the Java code for mergesort algorithm that sorts elements in an array. This code also incorporates a counter that counts the number of comparison operations during the process.

```java
public class MergeSort {
    private static int comparisonCount = 0;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example usage:
        int[] sampleArray = {12, 11, 13, 5, 6, 7};
        System.out.println("Given Array");
        printArray(sampleArray);

        MergeSort ob = new MergeSort();
        ob.sort(sampleArray, 0, sampleArray.length - 1);

        System.out.println("\nSorted array");
        printArray(sampleArray);
        System.out.println("\nNumber of comparison operations: " + comparisonCount);
    }

    void sort(int arr[], int l, int r) {
        if (l < r) {
            int m = (l + r) / 2;

            sort(arr, l, m);
            sort(arr, m + 1, r);

            merge(arr, l, m, r);
        }
    }

    void merge(int arr[], int l, int m, int r) {
        int n1 = m - l + 1;
        int n2 = r - m;

        int L[] = new int[n1];
        int R[] = new int[n2];

        for (int i = 0; i < n1; ++i)
            L[i] = arr[l + i];
        for (int j = 0; j < n2; ++j)
            R[j] = arr[m + 1 + j];

        int i = 0, j = 0;
        int k = l;

        while (i < n1 && j < n2) {
            comparisonCount++; // Count comparison operation
            if (L[i] <= R[j]) {
                arr[k] = L[i];
                i++;
            } else {
                arr[k] = R[j];
                j++;
            }
            k++;
        }

        while (i < n1) {
            arr[k] = L[i];
            i++;
            k++;
        }

        while (j < n2) {
            arr[k] = R[j];
            j++;
            k++;
        }
    }

    static void printArray(int arr[]) {
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
            System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
        System.out.println();
    }
}

```

In this example, the `sort()` method is the core of the MergeSort algorithm, it splits the array and recursively sorts each side. The `merge()` function then combines the sorted halves into a sorted whole.

The variable `comparisonCount` is incremented each time a comparison is made in the `merge()` function, tracking the number of comparison operations made throughout the program.

Python has a built-in module called "urlparse" that is useful to break down a URL into its component parts. Moreover, the TLDExtract module can be used to extract parts from the domain name. Here is a solution in Python using these libraries:

```python
import collections
from urllib.parse import urlparse, urlsplit
import tldextract

def valid_url(url):
    try:
        result = urlparse(url)
        return all([result.scheme, result.netloc])
    except ValueError:
        return False

def extract_subdomain(url):
    try:
        return urlsplit(url).hostname
    except ValueError:
        return ''

def subdomain_counter(urls):
    count = collections.defaultdict(int)
    for url in urls:
        if valid_url(url):
            ext = tldextract.extract(url)
            subdomain = "{}.{}.{}".format(ext.subdomain, ext.domain, ext.suffix)
            count[subdomain] += 1
        else:
            print(f'Invalid URL: {url}')
    return count

urls = ["https://subdomain.example.com/path", "https://anothersub.example.com/path", "https://subdomain.example.com/anotherpath"]
print(subdomain_counter(urls))
