Stack and queue are two types of data structures widely used in Computer Science Theory. The fundamental differences are:

1. A stack is a linear data structure that follows a Last In, First Out (LIFO) order for accessing data. Meaning, the data added last in the stack is the first one to be removed. Because of its LIFO property, a stack is also called a LIFO data structure.

2. A queue, on the other hand, is also a linear data structure but follows a First In, First Out (FIFO) order for accessing data. Meaning, the data added first in the queue is the first one to be removed. Due to its FIFO property, a queue is also categorized as a FIFO data structure. 

Now, here is how you can encapsulate Stack and Queue in Java:

**Stack:**

Stack in Java is a class that falls under the Collection framework. It extends the Vector class. It encompasses five operations: push, pop, peek, isEmply, and search. Here is a very basic example to illustrate how a stack can be created:

```
Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
stack.push(10);
stack.push(20);
stack.push(30);
System.out.println(stack.pop());
System.out.println(stack.peek());
```

**Queue:**

Queue in Java is an interface under Java’s collection framework which includes insertion, removal among other operations that can be performed on basic data structures. Below is a basic enqueue and dequeue example:

```
Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
q.add(10);
q.add(20);
q.add(30);
System.out.println(q.remove());
System.out.println(q.peek());
```

Stacks are mainly used for balancing symbols, reverse a word, postfix notation, etc., while Queues are used when things don’t have to be processed immediately, but have to be processed in First In First Out order like Breadth-First Search or first come first serve scheduling algorithm.

In order to create a generic class to define a fruit in TypeScript, we will use the class syntax. This class will hold variables for common characteristics all fruits have such as name, color, etc. This class would adhere to SOLID principles:

1. Single Responsibility Principle: The Class has a single job i.e., to hold properties common to all fruits.
2. Open-Closed Principle: The class is open for extension, but closed for modification.
3. Liskov's Substitution Principle: Derived classes must be substitutable for their base classes which is done here.
4. Interface Segregation Principle: The class doesn't depend on any interfaces it doesn't use.
5. Dependency Inversion Principle: The class doesn't depend on specific characteristics, it's generic to be used with all fruits.

Here is the code in TypeScript:

```typescript
class Fruit<T> {
  name: T;
  color: T;
  taste: T;

  constructor(name: T, color: T, taste: T) {
    this.name = name;
    this.color = color;
    this.taste = taste;
  }

  getName(): T {
    return this.name;
  }

  getColor(): T {
    return this.color;
  }

  getTaste(): T {
    return this.taste;
  }
}

const apple = new Fruit<string>('Apple', 'Red', 'Sweet');
console.log(apple.getName());
console.log(apple.getColor());
console.log(apple.getTaste());

const orange = new Fruit<string>('Orange', 'Orange', 'Sour');
console.log(orange.getName());
console.log(orange.getColor());
console.log(orange.getTaste());
```

In the above code, we have created a generic class named 'Fruit'. We have passed a generic placeholder 'T' which can be of any type. Then, we have created properties 'name', 'color', and 'taste' which will be common for all fruits. We have implemented an encapsulation principle where we don't directly modify or access an object's properties, instead, we have 'getter' methods to get these properties. Then, we have created objects 'apple' and 'orange' of class 'Fruit'.

