Below is a simple C++ code that calculates the sum of all elements within an array. This code incorporates basic input validation for array size and array elements. It also handles edge cases where the array size is 0 or negative, and where overflow may occur when summing elements.

```c++
#include<iostream>
#include<climits>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int n;

    cout << "Enter the number of elements in array: ";
    cin >> n;

    // Check for valid array size (must be non-negative)
    if(n < 0) {
        cout << "Invalid array size. Array size must be non-negative.";
        return 0;
    }

    long long sum = 0;  // Use long long to avoid overflow
    cout << "Enter elements: ";

    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        long long num;

        cin >> num;

        // Check for valid array element input (within int range)
        if(num < INT_MIN || num > INT_MAX) {
            cout << "Invalid array element. It should be within integer range.";
            return 0;
        }

        // Check for overflow before adding
        if ((sum > 0 && num > LLONG_MAX - sum) || (sum < 0 && num < LLONG_MIN - sum)) {
            cout << "Sum overflow. Cannot perform operation.";
            return 0;
        }

        sum += num;
    }

    cout << "Sum of elements is: " << sum;

    return 0;
}
```

For the element input validation, this code assumes that the array elements are within the `int` range. The overflow check, while not perfect, can help prevent some possible scenarios of overflow.

Please note that real-world cases may require more sophisticated error handling and validation, depending on the specific requirements and constraints of your project.

