Here's a simple implementation using concurrent programming in Java. This code consists of two threads, one for capitalizing the string and the other one for reversing the string. All thread safety matters and exception handling tactics have been implemented in this code.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class ThreadSafeStringTransform {

    private String string;
    private Lock lock;

    public ThreadSafeStringTransform(String string) {
        this.string = string;
        this.lock = new ReentrantLock();
    }

    public void toUpperCase() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            this.string = this.string.toUpperCase();
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public void reverse() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            this.string = new StringBuilder(this.string).reverse().toString();
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public String getString() {
        return this.string;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ThreadSafeStringTransform transformer = new ThreadSafeStringTransform("Hello, world!");

        Thread thread1 = new Thread(transformer::toUpperCase);
        Thread thread2 = new Thread(transformer::reverse);

        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();

        try {
            thread1.join();
            thread2.join();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        System.out.println(transformer.getString());
    }
}
```

This program creates a `ThreadSafeStringTransform` object with the initial string "Hello, world!" as input. It then starts two threads, one to make the string upper case and another to reverse it. The `lock()` and `unlock()` methods of the `ReentrantLock` object are used to ensure that the threads are executed one at a time. This guarantees that any reading or writing we do with `this.string` is thread-safe.

Note: The order in which the transformations are done is not guaranteed. It can happen either the string is capitalized first and then reversed or vice versa. If a specific order is needed, we have to apply some different strategy e.g., executing threads sequentially.
Also, keep in mind that this is a contrived example. Using independent threads for such small operations could be over-engineering in real applications. But it serves its purpose for teaching thread safety and concurrent programming.

Heap Sort is a popular yet slow sorting algorithm that utilizes the property of heaps - data structures similar to binary trees. Let's break down this problem and provide a code solution for heap sort in Java. 

Here's the step-by-step procedure to achieve this:

Step 1: Convert the unsorted list into a max heap data structure, where the parent node is always greater than or equal to its child nodes.

Step 2: The greatest element, the root of the max heap, is swapped with the last element of the unsorted list.

Step 3: Now the last element of the list is the maximum/ largest element. We exclude the last element from the heap and repeat the above process with the remaining elements. 

Here's how you can implement Heap Sort in Java:

```java
public class HeapSort {
    public void sort(int arr[]) {
        int n = arr.length;

        // Build heap (rearrange array)
        for (int i = n / 2 - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            heapify(arr, n, i);
        }

        // One by one extract an element from heap
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            // Move current root to end
            int temp = arr[0];
            arr[0] = arr[i];
            arr[i] = temp;

            // Call max heapify on the reduced heap
            heapify(arr, i, 0);
        }
    }

    // To heapify a subtree rooted with node i which is an index in arr[]. n is size of heap
    void heapify(int arr[], int n, int i) {
        int largest = i; // Initialize largest as root
        int leftChild = 2 * i + 1; 
        int rightChild = 2 * i + 2;

        // If left child is larger than root
        if (leftChild < n && arr[leftChild] > arr[largest]) {
            largest = leftChild;
        }

        // If right child is larger than largest so far
        if (rightChild < n && arr[rightChild] > arr[largest]) {
            largest = rightChild;
        }

        // If largest is not root
        if (largest != i) {
            int swap = arr[i];
            arr[i] = arr[largest];
            arr[largest] = swap;

            // Recursively heapify the affected sub-tree
            heapify(arr, n, largest);
        }
    }

    // Function to print array of size n
    static void printArray(int arr[]) {
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    // Driver program
    public static void main(String args[]) {
        int arr[] = {12, 11, 13, 5, 6, 7};
        int n = arr.length;

        HeapSort hs = new HeapSort();
        hs.sort(arr);

        System.out.println("Sorted array is:");
        printArray(arr);
    }
}
```

The `heapify` function makes sure that the array is a max heap. After that, we exchange the root of the max heap with last node and reduce the size of heap by one. We then continue this process until all nodes are swapped.

So, that’s how you can implement Heap Sort method in Java. This will sort numbers in increasing order.

