Here is a basic implementation of the Shell sort algorithm in C++. We use the "greater than" comparison operator to make the sort in descending order. To handle precision errors, we round to a specific decimal place (3 in this case) during comparison in the sort:

```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>        
using namespace std;

void shellSort(float array[], int n) {

    // Start with a big gap, then reduce the gap
    for (int gap = n / 2; gap > 0; gap /= 2) {
        // Do a gapped "insertion sort" for this gap size.
        // The first gap elements a[0..gap-1] are already in gapped order
        for (int i = gap; i < n; i += 1) {
            float temp = array[i];

            // add a[i] to the elements that have been gap sorted
            int j;
            for (j = i; j >= gap && round(array[j - gap]*1000)/1000 < round(temp*1000)/1000; j -= gap)
                array[j] = array[j - gap];

            // put temp (the original a[i]) in its correct location
            array[j] = temp;
        }
    }
}

void printArray(float array[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        cout << array[i] << " ";
    cout << "\n";
}

int main() {
    float array[] = {1.12345, 3.98765, 2.76452, 5.10293, 4.85321};
    int n = sizeof(array)/sizeof(array[0]);
    cout << "Original array: \n";
    printArray(array, n);
    shellSort(array, n);
    cout << "\nSorted array in descending order: \n";
    printArray(array, n);
}
```
In this code, the variable `temp` holds the current element while `j` serves as the comparison counter. At each step, `temp` is compared to the previously sorted elements, and if `temp` is greater, elements are shifted until the correct position for `temp` is found.

The precision handling is done by rounding the numbers to the nearest thousandth (3 decimal places) during the comparison operation (`round(array[j - gap]*1000)/1000 < round(temp*1000)/1000`). Rounding to the nearest thousandth is arbitrary and may be adjusted according to the specific needs of your task.

Please note, due to the nature of floating-point representation, some precision errors may still persist, but this provides a reasonable solution for most typical cases.

Lastly, the `printArray` function is used to print the array in a pretty format.

