The reference code is erroneous because the order of swap is not correct. In your code, when you do `*x = *y;` the original value of `x` is lost. During `*z = *x;`, `z` is set to `y`, not the original `x`, because `x` is no longer the original `x`, it is now `y`.

Here's the corrected function:

```C
#include<stdio.h>

void cyclic_swap(int* x, int* y, int* z){
    int tempX = *x;
    *x = *z;
    *z = *y;
    *y = tempX;
}

int main(){
    int x = 2;
    int y = 4;
    int z = 6;

    printf("Before swap: x = %d, y = %d, z = %d\n", x, y, z);

    cyclic_swap(&x, &y, &z);

    printf("After first swap: x = %d, y = %d, z = %d\n", x, y, z);

    cyclic_swap(&x, &y, &z);

    printf("After second swap: x = %d, y = %d, z = %d\n", x, y, z);

    return 0;
}
```

