```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <algorithm>

double findMedian(std::vector<double> & nums, int size) {
    // Check if vector is empty
    if (size == 0) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("Vector is empty");
    }

    // Sort the vector using QuickSort
    std::sort(nums.begin(),nums.end());

    // If size of the vector is even
    if (size % 2 == 0) {
        return (nums[size/2 - 1] + nums[size/2]) / 2.0;
    }
    // If size of the vector is odd
    else {
        return nums[size/2];
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<double> nums = {1.5, 3.0, 4.5, 2.5, 5.0};
    int size = nums.size();
    try {
        double median = findMedian(nums, size);
        std::cout << "Median of the vector is: " << median << std::endl;
    }
    catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) {
        std::cerr << e.what() << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This program starts by checking if vector is empty or not. If it's not empty, it sorts the vector using the built-in sort function in C++, which utilizes QuickSort algorithm. Once the vector is sorted, it finds the median. If the size of the vector is even, the median is the average of the two middle elements. If the size of the vector is odd, the median is the middle element. If the vector is empty, it throws an exception that can be handled in a try-catch block. This program prints the median of the vector if it's not empty. If the vector is empty, it prints an error message.

