The problem can be solved by using a combination of a HashSet and a HashMap. The HashSet is used to keep track of the IDs of the products that have been added. The HashMap is used to store the product ID as the key and the product object as its value.

In order to efficiently find the top k highest-priced products within a specific category, an additional HashMap can be used, where the key is the category, and the value is a max heap/PriorityQueue of product objects. This heap is constructed such that it maintains the top k priced elements at any point in time.

To support the search by name and category, we will use another two HashMaps storing Name and Category as keys and use a HashSet of product IDs, associated with them.

Here is an example implementation in Java:

```java
class Product {
    int id;
    String name;
    String category;
    double price;

    Product(int id, String name, String category, double price) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.category = category;
        this.price = price;
    }
}

class Inventory {
    Map<Integer, Product> productMap;
    Map<String, Set<Integer>> nameMap, categoryMap;
    Map<String, PriorityQueue<Product>> maxHeapForCategory;

    Inventory() {
        productMap = new HashMap<>();
        nameMap = new HashMap<>();
        categoryMap = new HashMap<>();
        maxHeapForCategory = new HashMap<>();
    }

    void addProduct(int id, String name, String category, double price) {
        if (productMap.containsKey(id)) return;

        Product p = new Product(id, name, category, price);
        productMap.put(id, p);

        nameMap.computeIfAbsent(name.toLowerCase(), s -> new HashSet<>()).add(id);
        categoryMap.computeIfAbsent(category.toLowerCase(), s -> new HashSet<>()).add(id);

        maxHeapForCategory.computeIfAbsent(category, k -> new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Double.compare(b.price, a.price))).add(p);
    }

    void removeProduct(int id) {
        if(!productMap.containsKey(id)) return;

        Product p = productMap.get(id);
        nameMap.get(p.name.toLowerCase()).remove(id);
        categoryMap.get(p.category.toLowerCase()).remove(id);
        maxHeapForCategory.get(p.category).remove(p);
        productMap.remove(id);
    }

    void updateProduct(int id, String name, String category, double price) {
        if(!productMap.containsKey(id)) return;

        removeProduct(id);
        addProduct(id, name, category, price);
    }

    Set<Product> searchByName(String name) {
        Set<Product> products = new HashSet<>();
        if(nameMap.containsKey(name.toLowerCase())) {
            for (int id: nameMap.get(name.toLowerCase())) {  
                products.add(productMap.get(id));
            }
        }
        return products;
    }

    Set<Product> getByCategory(String category) {
        Set<Product> products = new HashSet<>();
        if(categoryMap.containsKey(category.toLowerCase())) {
            for (int id: categoryMap.get(category.toLowerCase())) {
                products.add(productMap.get(id));
            }
        }
        return products;
    }

    List<Product> getTopKInCategory (String category, int k) {
        if (!maxHeapForCategory.containsKey(category))  return new ArrayList<>();

        PriorityQueue<Product> maxHeap =maxHeapForCategory.get(category);
        List<Product> res = new ArrayList<>();
        while (res.size() < k && !maxHeap.isEmpty())
            res.add(maxHeap.poll());

        for (Product p: res)
            maxHeap.add(p);

        return res;
    }
}
```

Time complexity:
1. Adding a product: O(log(k)) where k is top highest priced products.
2. Removing a product: O(n), as we need to go through the heap(num of products in the category can be considered as n for category related operations) and remove the product.
3. Updating a product: O(n)
4. Searching a product by name: O(1)
5. Getting all products by category: O(1)
6. Get top k priced products: O(k*log(k)).

Space complexity:
Overall, if n is total number of unique products, O(n) is the space complexity as we are storing these in HashMaps and PriorityQueues.

