```Java
public class Main {
    public static void rotateRight(int[] nums, int k) {
        if (nums == null || nums.length < 2 || k < 0) {
            return;
        }

        k = k % nums.length;

        // Reverse elements part by part
        reverse(nums, 0, nums.length - 1);
        reverse(nums, 0, k - 1);
        reverse(nums, k, nums.length - 1);
    }

    public static void reverse(int[] nums, int start, int end) {
        while (start < end) {
            int temp = nums[start];
            nums[start] = nums[end];
            nums[end] = temp;
            start++;
            end--;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String args[]) {
		int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5,};
		rotateRight(nums, 2);
		for(int i: nums) {
			System.out.print(i + " ");
		}
	}
}
```
Input: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
Rotation = 2
Output: [4, 5, 1, 2, 3]

By first reversing the entire array, and then reversing the elements at the start and end up to the rotation index 'k', we can achieve the desired rotation effect while maintaining the reversed order. 

The time complexity for this method is O(n), where 'n' is the length of the input array. This is because we only traverse the array a fixed number of times. 

In terms of space complexity, this method is O(1) because we are not using any additional data structures and the rotation is carried out in-place. 

This algorithm is efficient as it only uses a constant amount of space and runs in linear time, regardless of the size of the input array or the value of the rotation index 'k'.

