```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

struct Personnel {
    int id;
    std::string email;
};

class PersonnelVector {
    std::vector<Personnel> data;

public:
    void insert(int id, const std::string &email) {
        data.push_back(Personnel{id, email});
    }

    void remove(int id) {
        data.erase(std::remove_if(data.begin(), data.end(), [id](const Personnel &p) { return p.id == id; }), data.end());
    }

    void display() const {
        for (const auto &p : data) {
            std::cout << "ID: " << p.id << ", Email: " << p.email << '\n';
        }
    }
};

int main() {
    PersonnelVector pv;
    pv.insert(1, "person1@example.com");
    pv.insert(2, "person2@example.com");
    pv.display();
    std::cout << "--------\n";
    pv.remove(1);
    pv.display();
}
```

In this code, `insert` appends a `Personnel` object to the vector, `remove` removes all `Personnel` objects with the specified id, and `display` prints all the `Personnel` in the vector. Note that `remove` uses the `std::remove_if` algorithm, which moves the elements that need to be removed to the end of the vector and returns the iterator to the first element to be removed, then `erase` removes those elements from the vector.

The following Python code is a solution to the problem. It utilizes the in-built xml.etree.ElementTree module to parse the XML data. The function `parse_root` is used to recursively parse nested XML data, while handling attributes as well.

The code also implements basic error checking by printing error messages for invalid XML data.

```python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

people_string = "<person><name>John Smith</name><age>50</age><profession>engineer</profession></person>"

def parse_root(root):
    """
    Function to recursively parse XML data
    """
    for child in root:
        print(f"{child.tag.title()}: {child.text}")
        if child.attrib:
            for attr, value in child.attrib.items():
                print(f" ({attr.title()}: {value})")

        # recursively parse nested XML data
        if list(child):
            parse_root(child)

try:
    root = ET.fromstring(people_string)
except ET.ParseError:
    print("The XML data is invalid")
else:
    parse_root(root)
```

The `parse_root` function first prints the tag and text of each element in the XML data. If the element has attributes, these are also printed. If the element has child elements (i.e., it is a nested XML element), the function calls itself recursively on the child element. By calling itself recursively, the function is able to handle nested XML data of arbitrary depth. The error checking is done by using a try/except block to catch any parsing errors. If the XML data is invalid (i.e., it is not well-formed), the code in the except block will run, printing an error message and preventing the parsing function from running.

Here is a Python solution for this problem:

```python
def merge_arrays(arr1, arr2):
    result = []
    i = j = 0
    while i < len(arr1) and j < len(arr2):
        if arr1[i] < arr2[j]:
            if arr1[i] not in result:
                result.append(arr1[i])
            i += 1
        elif arr2[j] < arr1[i]:
            if arr2[j] not in result:
                result.append(arr2[j])
            j += 1
        else:
            if arr1[i] not in result:
                result.append(arr1[i])
            i += 1
            j += 1
    while i < len(arr1):
        if arr1[i] not in result:
            result.append(arr1[i])
        i += 1
    while j < len(arr2):
        if arr2[j] not in result:
            result.append(arr2[j])
        j += 1
    return result
```
This function uses two pointers 'i' and 'j' to track the current elements of arr1 and arr2. It compares the current elements and adds the smaller one to 'result'. If both elements are same, it adds any of them. It continues until it has traversed both arrays. The remaining elements of the longer array are also added to 'result'. The 'not in' operation ensures that no duplicate elements are added to the result.

Let's test this function with an example:

```python
arr1 = [3,5,7,10]
arr2 = [2,4,6,8,10]
print(merge_arrays(arr1, arr2))  # expected output: [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10]
```
The program should give the expected output. Please note that because the 'not in' operation could lead to a worst case scenario of O(n^2+m^2) time complexity as searching for a value in a list takes O(n) time. In Python, if it's allowed, using a set to eliminate duplicates would be more efficient.

