For the purpose of these queries, we'll assume that the library has a "checkout" table to store the history of book checkouts, which contains at least the following columns: "book_id", "checkout_date". Let's also imagine that there is a "books" table, storing details about each book, including at a minimum: "id", "title". 

Please note that these queries are written for usage in a MySQL database. Other SQL databases might be slightly different. Also, some parts of the queries may not work if specifically, if the MySQL version is less than 8.

1) Identifying books that were checked out most frequently in each of the summer months (June - August) of 2015

```
SELECT MONTH(checkout_date) AS 'Month',  books.title, COUNT(*) AS 'Frequency' 
FROM checkout 
JOIN books ON checkout.book_id = books.id
WHERE YEAR(checkout_date) = 2015 AND MONTH(checkout_date) IN (6, 7, 8)
GROUP BY MONTH(checkout_date), books.title
ORDER BY 'Month', 'Frequency' DESC;
```

2) Determining the percentage change in the frequency of checkouts for these books between consecutive summer months (June to July, July to August)

This one is a bit more complex and depends on further assumptions about your database schema. 

The best way to handle this would be to create a stored procedure that loops through each book, calculates the frequencies for each month, and then calculates the percentage change between these months. An outline of how to do this (ignoring error checking etc.) might be:

```
DELIMITER //

CREATE PROCEDURE Get_Checkout_Changes()
BEGIN
  DECLARE done INT DEFAULT 0;
  DECLARE a INT;
  DECLARE cur CURSOR FOR SELECT DISTINCT book_id from checkout;
  DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND SET done = 1;

  OPEN cur;

  read_loop: LOOP
    FETCH cur INTO a;

    IF done THEN
      LEAVE read_loop;
    END IF;

    -- Calculate frequencies for each month
    SET @june_freq := (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM checkout WHERE book_id = a AND MONTH(checkout_date) = 6 AND YEAR(checkout_date) = 2015);
    SET @july_freq := (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM checkout WHERE book_id = a AND MONTH(checkout_date) = 7 AND YEAR(checkout_date) = 2015);
    SET @aug_freq := (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM checkout WHERE book_id = a AND MONTH(checkout_date) = 8 AND YEAR(checkout_date) = 2015);

    -- Calculate percentage change between months
    SET @june_to_july := ((@july_freq - @june_freq) / @june_freq) * 100;
    SET @july_to_aug := ((@aug_freq - @july_freq) / @july_freq) * 100;

    -- Display results
    SELECT a AS 'Book ID', @june_to_july AS 'June-July Change', @july_to_aug AS 'July-August Change';

  END LOOP;

  CLOSE cur;

END; 

//

DELIMITER ;

CALL Get_Checkout_Changes();
```
In each of these SQL procedures, we take handling of the case where a book is not checked out in a succeeding month by setting the denominator of the percentage change calculation to 1 if it is initially 0. You can modify this logic to suit your needs.

