The above code is almost correct but has a minor mistake, it will count “question?” and “question” as two different words because it treats punctuation as part of a word. This might not be the expected behavior, as we usually consider "question" and "question?" to be the same word, irrespective of the punctuation.

The above code uses the `split(' ')` method to segment the string into tokens. This split method uses a space character as a delimiter to break the string into parts. While this does work to separate word tokens, it does not remove punctuation attached to words.

For example, "What is the answer to this question? What is the answer to that question?" would be split into:

`['What', 'is', 'the', 'answer', 'to', 'this', 'question?', 'What', 'is', 'the', 'answer', 'to', 'that', 'question?']`

As you can see, 'question?' is counted as a single token, while really 'question' and '?' should be counted separately. This may inflate the count of certain tokens and create extra token categories that are really not unique. 

We can correct it with using regular expression to split words where it encounters a non-alphabet character. Here is the corrected code:

```javascript
function countTokens(str) {
    let tokens = str.toLowerCase().split(/\W+/);
    let tokenCount = {};
    for (let i = 0; i < tokens.length; i++) {
        if (tokenCount[tokens[i]]) {
            tokenCount[tokens[i]]++;
        } else {
            tokenCount[tokens[i]] = 1;
        }
    }
    return tokenCount;
}
console.log(countTokens(str));
```
Here in this corrected code, I am using a regular expression `/\W+/` to split the string instead of a blank space. `\W` is a shorthand character class that matches any non-alphanumeric character. `+` specifies one or more. So, `/\W+/` will match one or more non-alphanumeric characters. This will effectively split the string at spaces and punctuation, leaving only words. I also added `.toLowerCase()` to make the count case insensitive.

