Yes, in Java, there's a step-by-step process for deleting a node from a singly linked list. In order to delete a node given a key:

1. Store head node
2. If the head node itself holds the key to be deleted
3. If the key is somewhere other than at head

Here's a simple code snippet to demonstrate this:

```Java
class LinkedList {
    Node head; // head of list

    class Node {
        int data;
        Node next;

        Node(int d) {
            data = d;
            next = null;
        }
    }

    void deleteNode(int key) {
        Node temp = head, prev = null;

        if (temp != null && temp.data == key) {
            head = temp.next; 
            return;
        }

        while (temp != null && temp.data != key) {
            prev = temp;
            temp = temp.next;
        } 

        if (temp == null) return;

        prev.next = temp.next;
    }

    public void push(int new_data) {
        Node new_node = new Node(new_data);
        new_node.next = head;
        head = new_node;
    }
}
```

In the deleteNode function, 

