Here's a small C++ program that creates a function to capitalize the first letter of every word, handling potential punctuation and periods, exclamation marks, and question marks.

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <cctype> // for tolower and toupper

using namespace std;

// Function to capitalize the first letter of every word
string capitalize(string str) {
    // in case first character of the string is a letter make sure it is capitalized
    str[0] = toupper(str[0]);

    for(int i = 1; i < str.size(); i++) {
        // check if the character is punctuation or not
        if (str[i - 1] == ' ' || str[i - 1] == '.' || str[i - 1] == '!' || str[i - 1] == '?') {
              str[i] = toupper(str[i]);  
        } 
        else {
            str[i] = tolower(str[i]); // Make sure the remaining characters of a word are lowercase
        }
    }

    return str;
}

int main() {
    string str = "hello world. my name is bob! how are you?";

    cout << "Before: " << str << endl;
    cout << "After: " << capitalize(str) << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This program creates a new string `capitalized` and iteratively adds each character from the original string, using the `toupper` and `tolower` functions to make sure it's correctly capitalized or not. It uses `push_back` function to add characters at the end of the new string.

The condition `if (str[i] == ' ' && str[i+1] != ' ')` check for a space character (which denotes the end of a word), and if it's followed by a non-space character, this character is capitalized. Also, it checks potential punctuations to ensure capitalization afterwards. It will capitalize first letter of the sentence and after every white space and punctuations such as periods, exclamation marks, or question marks.

