Sure, I'll implement a simple bubble sort algorithm in Java. Here’s a possible implementation:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main{

    public static void main(String[] args){
        int[] data = {3, 4, 2, 7, 1};
        bubbleSort(data);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(data));
    }

    public static void bubbleSort(int[] data){
        int n = data.length;
        int temp = 0;
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){
            for(int j = 1; j < (n - i); j++){  
                if(data[j-1] > data[j]){  
                    temp = data[j-1];  
                    data[j-1] = data[j];  
                    data[j] = temp;  
                } 
            } 
        } 
    } 
}
```
The `bubbleSort` function works by repeatedly swapping the adjacent elements if they are in wrong order. The `for` loop compares the adjacent elements and swaps them if they are not in the required order. The second `for` loop reduces its size after every complete traversal because at the end of each cycle the greatest number will have moved to its rightful place. 

Please, keep in mind that Bubble sort isn't the best option for large lists because of its high computational complexity (O(n^2)). You can use Quick sort, Merge sort or Tim sort. The latter one is actually used in Java's `Arrays.sort()`. 

