The Java Reflection API is a powerful feature in Java programming which allows runtime manipulation of classes, interfaces, methods, and other elements. The use of Java Reflection can lead to code that is difficult to debug and maintain, so while it's a powerful tool, it should be used sparingly.

Two less frequently utilized functionalities of the Java Reflection API are:

1. Accessing Private Members:
   Reflection can be used to access the private members (methods or fields) of a class, even though this is typically considered bad practice as it breaks the encapsulation principle of Object-Oriented Programming. However, in some cases, this could be useful for testing or working with legacy code.

Code example:
```java
import java.lang.reflect.Field;

public class ReflectionTest {
    private String testField = "Private Field";

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ReflectionTest test = new ReflectionTest();
        Field field = ReflectionTest.class.getDeclaredField("testField");
        field.setAccessible(true);
        System.out.println("Before: " + field.get(test));
        field.set(test, "New Value");
        System.out.println("After: " + field.get(test));
    }
}
```
Output:
```
Before: Private Field
After: New Value
```

2. Discovering Class Hierarchies:
   The Reflection API can be used to discover the parent classes and interfaces of a given class. This could be useful in certain dynamic programming scenarios where you want to understand more about a class's hierarchy.

Code example:
```java
public class ReflectionTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Class<?> superClass = ReflectionTest.class.getSuperclass();
        System.out.println("Parent class: " + superClass.getName());
        Class<?>[] interfaces = ReflectionTest.class.getInterfaces();
        for (Class<?> i : interfaces) {
            System.out.println("Implemented interface: " + i.getName());
        }
    }
}
```
Output:
```
Parent class: java.lang.Object
```
Note: In the second example, there are no interfaces, so it will not print any "Implemented interface". But if the `ReflectionTest` class implemented any interface, it would print out its name.

