Here is how you can create a PHP function that fulfills the requirements. This function uses the concept of Finite Automaton based pattern searching which is efficient even for large string inputs. 

Algorithm:
1. Create a Finite Automata representation of the pattern.
2. Traverse the main string and for each character, do a state transition in the automaton.
3. Each time we reach the final state of the automaton, we increase our counter for the pattern finding.

The majority of the time complexity is consumed in the creation of the Finite Automata.

```php
function searchText($text, $pattern) {
  $textLength = strlen($text);
  $patLength = strlen($pattern);
  $FA = generateFA($pattern, $patLength);

  // Case insensitive conversion.
  $text = strtolower($text);
  $pattern = strtolower($pattern);

  $count = 0;

  // Start traversing text.
  $state = 0;  // State of the automata.

  for ($i = 0; $i < $textLength; ++$i) {
    $state = $FA[$state][$text[$i]];
    if ($state == $patLength) {
      $count++;
    }
  }
  return $count;
}

function generateFA($pattern, $M) {
  $alphabet = range('a', 'z'); // Considering alphabetical string inputs.
  $FA = []; // Finite Automata.

  for ($state = 0; $state <= $M; ++$state) {
    foreach ($alphabet as $char) {
      $FA[$state][$char] = getNextState($pattern, $M, $state, $char);
    }
  }
  return $FA;
}

function getNextState($pattern, $M, $state, $char) {
  if ($state < $M && $char == $pattern[$state]) {
    return $state + 1;
  }
  for ($nextState = $state; $nextState > 0; --$nextState) {
    if ($pattern[$nextState - 1] == $char) {
      for ($i = 0; $i < $nextState - 1; ++$i) {
        if ($pattern[$i] != $pattern[$state - $nextState + 1 + $i]) {
          break;
        }
        if ($i == $nextState - 1) {
          return $nextState;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return 0;
}

echo searchText("The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy fox", "fox");  // Returns 2.
```
Above function constitutes a linear time complexity O(n) for text traversal, but constructing the Finite Automata takes a time complexity of O(m^3 * ALPHABET_SIZE), where m is the length of the pattern. ALPHABET_SIZE is constant here (26 for letters a-z).

Extra optimization can be done by applying KMP (Knuth Morris Pratt) or Rabin-Karp algorithms that improves pattern preprocessing time to O(m) and overall time complexity to O(m+n) in the average case. However, they could be more complex to implement than the Finite Automata approach.

