Here's a basic algorithm in Java that checks a string for palindromic qualities. This code assumes that whitespace, punctuation, and letter case do not matter when considering a string as a palindrome:

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "A man, a plan, a canal, Panama";
        str = str.toLowerCase().replaceAll("\\W", ""); // Convert to lowercase and remove non-word characters

        if (isPalindrome(str)) {
            System.out.println("This string IS a palindrome.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("This string is NOT a palindrome.");
        }
    }

    public static boolean isPalindrome(String str) {
        int i = 0, j = str.length() - 1;

        while (i < j) {
            if (str.charAt(i) != str.charAt(j))
                return false;

            i++;
            j--;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```
This code snippet works by first standardizing the string to be checked by converting it to lowercase and removing all non-word characters.  Then it checks if it's a palindrome by comparing its characters from ends towards the middle.

Do keep in mind that this only checks the entire string for palindromic qualities. If you want to check for palindromes in terms of its words or phrases, a more complex algorithm will be needed.

