In Java, there is no built-in switch-case functionality to handle complex conditional checks like determining prime numbers as these actions require loops and multiple conditions. But we can implement the logic within our functions dividing it into cases based on input conditions. 

Let's implement it:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        checkNum(5);      //Example usage
    }

    static void checkNum(int num) {
        int flag = 0;
        if (num <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input is not a natural number.");
        }
        switch (num) {
            case 1:
                System.out.println("1 is neither prime nor composite.");
                break;
            default:
                for (int i = 2; i <= num / 2; ++i) {
                    // condition for non-prime number
                    if (num % i == 0) {
                        flag = 1;
                        break;
                    }
                }

                if (flag == 0)
                    System.out.println(num + " is a prime number.");
                else
                    System.out.println(num + " is a composite number.");
                break;
        }
    }
}
```
In the above Java code, we put the logic of finding prime or composite numbers into the `checkNum` function. It throws an exception for non-natural numbers and handles the special case when the number is 1. For any number greater than 1, it checks whether it's prime or composite.

Here is a simple solution in Java using PriorityQueue. PriorityQueue is an implementation of the Queue interface and is part of Java's collections framework. It does not order the elements in ascending or descending order but rather it arranges elements in natural order.

PriorityQueue in Java can be very useful in scenarios where you want to find kth smallest or largest element as per the natural order of elements. Here the elements of PriorityQueue are ordered according to their natural ordering, or by a Comparator provided at queue construction time, depending on which constructor is used.

```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Random;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class KthSmallestNumber {
    private static PriorityQueue<Integer> generateRandomNumbers(int n, int limit) {
        Random rand = new Random();
        PriorityQueue<Integer> numbers = new PriorityQueue<>();

        while (numbers.size() != n) {
            int randomNum = rand.nextInt((limit - 1) + 1) + 1;
            if (!numbers.contains(randomNum)) {
                numbers.add(randomNum);
            }
        }
        return numbers;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
            System.out.println("Enter the range of numbers (n): ");
            int n = input.nextInt();
            System.out.println("Enter the limit of random numbers: ");
            int limit = input.nextInt();

            if (n <= 0 || limit <= 0 || limit < n) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Inputs should be positive integers and the limit should be greater than or equal to n.");
            }

            PriorityQueue<Integer> numbers = generateRandomNumbers(n, limit);

            System.out.println("Enter the kth smallest number to find: ");
            int k = input.nextInt();

            if (k <= 0 || k > n) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("K should be positive integer and less than or equal to n.");
            }

            int kthSmallestNumber = 0;
            for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
                kthSmallestNumber = numbers.poll();
            }

            System.out.println("The " + k + "th smallest number is: " + kthSmallestNumber);

        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

Program creates a PriorityQueue of n unique random integers within the given limit. Then it polls k elements from the priority queue, at the end of which the last polled number will be the kth smallest number.

Please remember that PriorityQueue implementation in Java provides O(log(n)) time for enqueu and dequeue methods hence proving to be very efficient especially when dealing with finding kth smallest or largest numbers.

