Here is a simple example of a class "Person" that implements the Comparable interface and overrides the compareTo method. This class represents a person with a name and an age, and the compareTo method is defined to compare persons based on their ages.

```java
public class Person implements Comparable<Person> {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public Person(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Person other) {
        // Ascending order
        return this.age - other.age;

        // If you want descending order you can use
        // return other.age - this.age;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Person{" +
                "name='" + name + '\'' +
                ", age=" + age +
                '}';
    }
}
```

In this class, the compareTo method takes another Person object as an argument and subtracts the age of this person from the age of the other person. If the result is positive, this person is older than the other person. If the result is negative, this person is younger than the other person. If the result is zero, both persons are the same age. This allows us to sort a list of persons in ascending or descending order based on age.

