    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        sum += arr[i];
    }
    return sum;
}

int main() {
    int size = 10;

    // dynamically allocate memory using new
    int* arr = new int[size];

    // assigning values
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        arr[i] = i * 2;
    }

    // Print array elements
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        std::cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }

    // Calculate and print the sum of elements
    std::cout << "\nSum: " << sum(arr, size);

    delete[] arr;

    return 0;
}
```
This program creates a dynamic array using pointers and `new` operator. Afterwards, we assign values to the elements of the array -- each element gets a value that's equal to its index multiplied by 2. Finally, we calculate the sum of all elements using the `sum` function and output it. The `delete[]` operator deallocates the memory after we're done with the array, preventing memory leaks.

Remember, in C++, it is vital to match each `new[]` operation with a `delete[]` to avoid memory leaks.

