This problem can be approached using a programming language such as Python, leveraging its built-in functionalities such as the .lower() method to convert the string to lowercase. We can use the html.parser from html.parser module to validate the HTML. Complete validation of HTML can be difficult (or impossible), but we can at least check for balance of tags. Below is a solution.

```python
from HTMLParser import HTMLParser

class MyHTMLParser(HTMLParser):
    def __init__(self):
        HTMLParser.__init__(self)
        self.stack = []

    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):
        self.stack.append(tag)

    def handle_endtag(self, tag):
        if not self.stack:
            print("Error: End tag {} has no start tag".format(tag))
        elif self.stack[-1] != tag:
            print("Error: End tag {} does not match start tag {}".format(tag, self.stack[-1]))
        else:
            self.stack.pop()

def transform_and_validate(html_string):
    html_string_lower = html_string.lower()
    parser = MyHTMLParser()
    parser.feed(html_string_lower)
    if parser.stack:
        print("Error: unmatched start tags", parser.stack)
    else:
        print("Valid HTML")

# test the function with a HTML string
transform_and_validate('<HTML><BODY><P>HELLO WORLD</P></BODY></HTML>')
```

This will output "Valid HTML" or any errors in tag balance. Note that it relies on html.parser module which is a simple HTML parser, so it will not be able to handle complex or erroneous HTML beyond simple issues with tag balance. If you have complex HTML and need full validation, you would need to use a more robust HTML parser, possibly in combination with other tools or services.

