```javascript
const mergeSortedArrays = (arrA, arrB) => {
  // Verify if the inputs are Arrays
  if (!Array.isArray(arrA) || !Array.isArray(arrB)) {
    throw new TypeError('Both inputs must be arrays');
  }

  // Filter non-integer values
  arrA = arrA.filter(el => Number.isInteger(el));
  arrB = arrB.filter(el => Number.isInteger(el));

  const result = [];

  while (arrA.length > 0 && arrB.length > 0) {
    // Always take the smaller element
    if (arrA[0] <= arrB[0]) {
      result.push(arrA.shift());
    } else {
      result.push(arrB.shift());
    }
  }

  // If arrA still has elements, add them to the end of result
  while (arrA.length !== 0) {
    result.push(arrA.shift());
  }

  // If arrB still has elements, add them to the end of result
  while (arrB.length !== 0) {
    result.push(arrB.shift());
  }

  return result;
};

try {
    console.log(mergeSortedArrays([1, 2, 3], ["abc", 4, 5])); // [1,2,3,4,5]
    console.log(mergeSortedArrays([1, 2, 3], "abc")); // Throws Error
} catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
}
```

In the above code, the function `mergeSortedArrays()` checks if the inputs are arrays. If not, it throws an error. Also, the provided arrays are then filtered to remove any non-integer values, so that only numeric data is processed. The sorted arrays are then merged in a way that retains numerical order. If an array has elements left over after the other is empty, these elements are added to the end of the result array, as they are already in the correct position according to sorting rules. In the end, the function returns the resulting array. Also, a `try catch` block is added to handle the error.

