Following is the JavaScript code using JSON for deep copy.

```js
const deepCopyWithJSON = (obj) => {
    return JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj));
}

let obj = {
    name: 'Jack',
    age: 30,
    car: {
        model: 'BMW',
        year: 2020
    }
}

let copiedObj = deepCopyWithJSON(obj);
console.log(copiedObj);
```

The limitation of this method is that it doesn't handle circular reference, won't copy functions, and other special types because JSON.stringify() doesn't support it. 

For handling circular reference, functions and other special types, we need our own custom deep copy method like this:

```js
function isObject(input) {
    return Object.prototype.toString.call(input) === '[object Object]';
}

const deepCopyWithCustom = (obj, hash = new WeakMap()) => {
    if (!isObject(obj)) return obj;
    if (hash.has(obj)) return hash.get(obj);

    let newObj = Array.isArray(obj) ? [] : {};
    hash.set(obj, newObj);

    for (let key in obj) {
        if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(obj, key)) {
            newObj[key] = deepCopyWithCustom(obj[key], hash);
        }
    }
    return newObj;
}

let copiedObjCustom = deepCopyWithCustom(obj);
console.log(copiedObjCustom);
```

In order to compare the execution time, we use `console.time()` like this:

```js
console.time('JSON deep clone time');
deepCopyWithJSON(obj);
console.timeEnd('JSON deep clone time');

console.time('Custom deep clone time');
deepCopyWithCustom(obj);
console.timeEnd('Custom deep clone time');
```

Generally, the JSON method could be faster than the custom method because native methods are usually optimized. But if the object is extremely large or it includes circular references, complicated types like functions, the JSON method will fail or take a very long time due to recursive JSON.stringify() and JSON.parse(). Therefore, it is recommended to use the JSON method for simple data or small data sizes, while for complex or large data, using a custom method is a better choice.

In general, finding the intersection of two linked lists within O(n log n) time complexity and O(1) space complexity isn't feasible, because we're not allowed to use any external data structures and built-in functions. However, here is a possible solution with O(n log n) time complexity (due to sorting) which uses no extra space other than few variables.

This approach modifies the original lists and it doesn't create any new nodes.

0. Let 'a' and 'b' be the two linked lists to intersect.

1. Sort the lists 'a' and 'b' in ascending order. We can use merge sort as it can sort the linked list in O(n log n) time complexity. 

2. Compare elements of 'a' and 'b' from head to tail.

    2.1 If node a's value is less than node b's value, move node 'a' forward.

    2.2 If node a's value is greater than node b's value, move node 'b' forward.

    2.3 If they are equal, that is the intersection point, store it to a temporary node and move both nodes forward.

3. Return the head of the intersected list.

Again, please note that this modifies the original lists. If preserving the original lists is important in your application, this solution should not be used.

Here is the pseudo code:

```
function findIntersection(a, b) {
    a.sort() // O(n log n)
    b.sort() // O(n log n)
    intersection = null
    tail = null

    while (a is not null and b is not null) { // O(n)
        if (a.value < b.value) {
            a = a.next
        } else if (a.value > b.value) {
            b = b.next
        } else {
            if (intersection is null) {
                intersection = a
                tail = a
            } else {
                tail.next = a
                tail = tail.next
            }

            a = a.next
            b = b.next
        }
    }

    if (tail is not null) {
        tail.next = null
    }

    return intersection
}
```

The time complexity is dominated by the sorting process, which is O(n log n).
And the space complexity is O(1) because we only use a few extra variables.

