JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight data interchange format that is easy for humans to read and write and easy for machines to parse and generate. JSON is often used to transmit data between a web application and a server. In the context of representing an HTML document, JSON can be used to represent the tree-like structure making it easy for various tools, libraries, and programming languages to work with the data.

HTML documents are made up of elements, which are nested within other elements, forming a tree-like structure. Elements can have attributes and children, creating a hierarchical structure where the root is typically the 'html' element.

To represent such a structure using JSON, objects and arrays can be used. Objects can be used to store key-value pairs, where keys are strings and values can be strings, numbers, booleans, null, arrays, or other objects. Arrays can be used to store ordered lists of values.

For instance, consider the following simple HTML document:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Sample Page</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Welcome to the Sample Page</h1>
    <p>Sample paragraph.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Item 1</li>
      <li>Item 2</li>
    </ul>
  </body>
</html>
```

Using JSON to represent the tree-like structure of the HTML document, we might have:

```json
{
  "tagName": "html",
  "children": [
    {
      "tagName": "head",
      "children": [
        {
          "tagName": "title",
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "Sample Page"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "tagName": "body",
      "children": [
        {
          "tagName": "h1",
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "Welcome to the Sample Page"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "tagName": "p",
          "children": [
            {
              "textContent": "Sample paragraph."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "tagName": "ul",
          "children": [
            {
              "tagName": "li",
              "children": [
                {
                  "textContent": "Item 1"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "tagName": "li",
              "children": [
                {
                  "textContent": "Item 2"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

In this representation, each HTML element is an object with a "tagName" and "children" property. The "tagName" value is a string corresponding to the element's tag name, while the "children" value is an array of child elements or text content. By nesting objects and arrays this way, the entire structure of the HTML document can be encoded as a JSON object.

Various tools and libraries, such as jQuery, React, or Angular, can use this JSON representation to manipulate and render the HTML content efficiently. The JSON format is also language-agnostic, which means it can be used with any programming language that has a JSON parser.

