Here's a PHP function that accomplishes the trimming of the string and all extra inline whitespace:

```php
function trimWhitespaces($str) {
    $result = '';
    $word = '';
    $len = strlen($str);

    for($i = 0; $i < $len; $i++) {
        if ($str[$i] != ' ') {
            $word .= $str[$i];
        } else {
            if ($word) {
                $result .= $word . ' ';
                $word = '';
            }
        }
    }
    $result .= $word; // adds the last word
    return $result;
}

$string = "   Hello      World   ";
$result = trimWhitespaces($string);
echo $result; // Outputs: "Hello World"
```

With the help of a buffer variable `$word`, we store sequence of characters until we encounter a white space. Then, we add it to the result string followed by a space, and reset the buffer. 
After the end of the loop (in case our sentence ends with alpha-numeric characters rather than a space), we add the remaining buffer (if any) to the result.

This algorithm skips all leading and trailing white spaces and handles inline multiple spaces. The words themselves are not effected by this function.

