}
```

The latter version of the code lacks synchronization and waiting/notify mechanism. Without these, the `ProcessorThread` might run before the `ReaderThread` has finished reading. The `ProcessorThread` could even read a value before it has been updated, processing the previous value twice. This can lead to undesirable behavior and the result of such a multi-threaded program can be unpredictable due to potential race conditions.

Step 1: Define the Singleton Class
In Java, the Singleton pattern makes the default constructor private and creates a method that creates a new instance of the class when needed. 

```java
public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton uniqueInstance;

    // other useful instance variables here

    private Singleton() {
        // Private Constructor
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if(uniqueInstance == null) {
            uniqueInstance = new Singleton();
        }
        return uniqueInstance;
    }

    // other useful methods here
}
```

This code creates a private static variable of the class itself, and then a public method `getInstance()` that creates a new instance of the class if it doesn’t exist yet. If it does exist, it simply returns the existing instance.

Step 2: Synchronization
In a multithreaded environment, the getInstance() method might create two instances of Singleton when called concurrently. This can be prevented by modifying the getInstance() to be **synchronized**, which ensures that only one thread can execute this method at a time.

```java
public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton uniqueInstance;

    // other useful instance variables here

    private Singleton() {}

    public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
        if (uniqueInstance == null) {
            uniqueInstance = new Singleton();
        }
        return uniqueInstance;
    }

    // other useful methods here
}
```

Step 3: Double-Checked Locking
Synchronizing the `getInstance()` method has some performance drawbacks. An "upgrade" for the Singleton Design Pattern is using "double-checked locking."


```java
public class Singleton {
    private volatile static Singleton uniqueInstance;

    private Singleton() {}

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (uniqueInstance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (uniqueInstance == null) {
                    uniqueInstance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return uniqueInstance;
    }
}
```
With double-checked locking, we first check to see if an instance is created, and only if it was not, then we synchronize. This ensures that we only synchronize the first time, which is the exact moment we need thread safety.

Step 4: Test the Singleton pattern
Now that we've implemented our Singleton class, we can test that it does indeed work as expected.
```java
public class SingletonTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Singleton instanceOne = Singleton.getInstance();
        Singleton instanceTwo = Singleton.getInstance();

        System.out.println(instanceOne == instanceTwo);  //Should print true
    }
}
```
In this test, we get two instances of the Singleton class and then verify that they are indeed the same instance by comparing their memory references. If we've implemented our Singleton correctly, this will print "true," otherwise, it will print "false."

