Here is a Java program that solves the problem as stated. This program uses a HashMap to store the frequency of each character and a TreeMap to sort the characters based on their frequency and lexicographic order.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "example string";
        findStringLength(str);
        findCharacterFrequency(str);
    }

    public static void findStringLength(String str) {
        int length = 0;
        for(char c : str.toCharArray()) {
            length++;
        }
        System.out.println("Length of the string: " + length);
    }

    public static void findCharacterFrequency(String str) {
        // outputFrequency HashMap stores frequency of each character in string
        HashMap<Character, Integer> characterFrequency = new HashMap<>();

        for(char c : str.toCharArray()) {
            characterFrequency.put(c, characterFrequency.getOrDefault(c, 0) + 1);
        }

        // inputTreeMap sorts characters in lexicographical order for each frequency
        TreeMap<Integer, TreeSet<Character>> sortedMap = new TreeMap<>();

        for(Map.Entry<Character, Integer> entry : characterFrequency.entrySet()) {
            if(!sortedMap.containsKey(entry.getValue())) {
                sortedMap.put(entry.getValue(), new TreeSet<Character>());
            }
            sortedMap.get(entry.getValue()).add(entry.getKey());
        }

        for(Map.Entry<Integer, TreeSet<Character>> entry : sortedMap.entrySet()) {
            for(Character ch : entry.getValue()) {
                System.out.println("Character " + ch + " appears " + entry.getKey() + " times");
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In the findStringLength function, the program iterates through each character of the string to get the length.

In the findCharacterFrequency function, a HashMap is used to store the frequency of each character in the string. Then a TreeMap is used to sort these characters first by their frequency, then in lexicographical order (achieved by using TreeSet to store characters with the same frequency).

This program first prints the length of the string, and then prints each unique character in the string with its frequency. Both are printed in ascending order: characters first by frequency then lexicographical order.

