This function starts with the first character of the string and checks whether it is a vowel, consonant, number, space or special character. It increments the corresponding count in the `count_dict` dictionary. It then calls itself, but with the index of the next character. This continues until there are no more characters to process.

To ignore case, the function converts each character to lowercase before checking which category it falls under.

The count of each type of character is kept in a dictionary (`count_dict`), which is returned by the function when it finishes processing the string. The keys of this dictionary are the character types, while the values are the counts.

Note: Unicode characters are not considered as Python's string type already supports Unicode out of the box, and deciding whether a Unicode character is a vowel, consonant, etc. is a complex problem that can vary based on language and other factors.

