import java.time.DayOfWeek;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.List;

class DateCounter {
    // count number of weekdays, weekends and holidays between two dates.
    public void countDays(LocalDate startDate, LocalDate endDate, List<LocalDate> holidays) {
        int weekdays = 0;
        int weekends = 0;
        int holidayDays = 0;

        LocalDate date = startDate;
        while (!date.isAfter(endDate)) {
            DayOfWeek dw = date.getDayOfWeek();
            if (dw == DayOfWeek.SATURDAY || dw == DayOfWeek.SUNDAY || holidays.contains(date)) { 
                weekends++;
                if(holidays.contains(date)) {
                    holidayDays++;
                }
            } else {
                weekdays++;
            }

            date = date.plusDays(1);
        }

        System.out.println("Number of weekdays: " + weekdays);
        System.out.println("Number of weekends: " + weekends);
        System.out.println("Number of holidays: " + holidayDays);
    }
}
```

This Java function goes through every date from the start date to the end date, checks if it is a weekend or a holiday, and increments the corresponding counter. Note that this code considers holidays that fell on a weekend as both a weekend and a holiday. If you want to count such days only as holidays, you would need to adjust the condition in the if statement.

Also this function assumes that the 'holidays' list is already populated with the holiday dates.

Note this solution also handles the case of leap years because LocalDate is Leap Year aware and able to correctly iterate over the dates in February of a leap year, and also over days at the end of a leap year. This is true for any date range you specify even if it includes leap years. 

