Here is a basic implementation of a singly linked list in C++. This example utilizes smart pointers for memory management:

```cpp
#include <memory>
#include <iostream>

class Node {
public:
    int data;
    std::shared_ptr<Node> next;

    Node(int data) {
        this->data = data;
        this->next = nullptr;
    }
};

class LinkedList {
private:
    std::shared_ptr<Node> head;

public:

    void append(int data) {
        if (this->head == nullptr) {
            this->head = std::make_shared<Node>(data);
        } else {
            std::shared_ptr<Node> current = this->head;
            while (current->next != nullptr) {
                current = current->next;
            }
            current->next = std::make_shared<Node>(data);
        }
    }

    void print() {
        std::shared_ptr<Node> temp = this->head;
        while (temp != nullptr) {
            std::cout << temp->data << " ";
            temp = temp->next;
        }
        std::cout << std::endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    LinkedList list;
    list.append(5);
    list.append(6);
    list.append(7);
    list.print();  // Output: 5 6 7
    return 0;
}
```

This implementation includes two classes: Node and LinkedList. The Node class represents an individual element in the list with a data field and a next pointer. The LinkedList class represents the list itself, containing a head pointer that points to the first element in the list. 

The LinkedList class has two methods: append and print. The append method adds a new Node to the end of the list. The print method traverses the list from the head to end, printing each Node's data field.

This implementation also demonstrates optimal memory allocation by using smart pointers, which can automatically deallocate memory that is no longer needed, reducing the risk of memory leaks.

