Here is a Java program that addresses the prompt. It uses the java.util.PriorityQueue data structure to keep track of the top N values. PriorityQueue is an ideal data structure for this problem because it maintains a sorted order of its elements (by default, in ascending order). By default, PriorityQueue uses the natural order of the elements, but a custom Comparator can be used to order the elements in descending order. This program also includes error checking to ensure that the user inputs valid integers and a proper value for N.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Program {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        PriorityQueue<Integer> queue = new PriorityQueue<>();

        int n;
        while(true) {
            try {
                System.out.print("Enter the number of top integers to keep: ");
                n = scanner.nextInt();
                if (n > 0) {
                    break;
                }
                System.out.println("You have to input a positive integer. Try again.");
            } catch (InputMismatchException exception) {
                System.out.println("You have to input an integer. Try again.");
                scanner.next();
            }
        }

        while(true) {
            try {
                System.out.print("Enter an integer (or 'q' to quit): ");
                int num = scanner.nextInt();

                if (queue.size() < n) {
                    queue.offer(num);
                } else if (num > queue.peek()) {
                    queue.poll();
                    queue.offer(num);
                }
            } catch (InputMismatchException exception) {
                String stringInput = scanner.next();
                if (stringInput.equalsIgnoreCase("q")) {
                    break;
                }
                System.out.println("You have to input an integer or 'q' to quit. Try again.");
            }
        }

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>(queue);
        Collections.sort(result, Collections.reverseOrder());
        System.out.println("The top " + n + " numbers are: " + result);
    }
}
```

Please note that people might normally expect the largest number to be at the front of the list of top N numbers. For this reason, the program uses Collections.reverseOrder() to sort the result in descending order before printing it out. However, if the requirement of the problem is to return the top N numbers in ascending order, you could easily change this by omitting the parameters to Collections.sort().

