Here's a simple implementation:

```javascript
window.onload = function() {
  let messages = ["Welcome!", "Enjoy your visit!", "Did you find what you're looking for?", "Thanks for your visit!"];
  let delay = 5000; // 5000 millisecond = 5 second
  let index = 0;

  function displayMessage() {
    if(index < messages.length) {
      alert(messages[index]);
      index++;
      setTimeout(displayMessage, delay);
    }
  }

  setTimeout(displayMessage, delay);
}
```

How does it work?

This script bundles all the messages into an array and sets a delay of 5 seconds. Once the webpage is fully loaded, the `displayMessage` function is called after a delay of 5 seconds. This function displays the initial pop-up message and then, after the prescribed delay, calls itself to display the next message. It repeats this until all of the messages have been shown.

This does not interfere with user's interaction with the webpage during the load sequence. The pop-up messages will appear one by one after 5 seconds delay and once the previous pop-up message has been closed.

