In interpolation search, the middle element is not chosen as the midpoint in the case of binary search. Instead, a position nearer to the expected location of the key value is selected for comparison. Here is how the C program would look like using the Interpolation Search algorithm:

```c
#include<stdio.h>

int interpolationSearch(int arr[], int n, int x)
{
    int low = 0, high = (n - 1);

    while (low <= high && x >= arr[low] && x <= arr[high]) {
        if (low == high) {
            if (arr[low] == x) 
                return low;
            return -1;
        }

        int pos = low + (((double)(high - low) / 
            (arr[high] - arr[low])) * (x - arr[low]));

        if (arr[pos] == x)
            return pos;

        if (arr[pos] < x)
            low = pos + 1;

        else
            high = pos - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

void main()
{
   int arr[] = {10, 12, 13, 16, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 33, 35, 42, 47};
   int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);

   int x = 18; 
   int index = interpolationSearch(arr, n, x);

   if (index != -1)
     printf("Element found at index %d", index);
   else
     printf("Element not found.");
}
```

In this program, the `interpolationSearch()` function will return the index of the element `x` if found in the array `arr[]`. Otherwise, `-1` is returned. The main section of the program will print the result. The test is done with the element `18` in the sorted array.

Please, note that Interpolation search works better than Binary Search for a sorted and uniformly distributed array, and it is likely to perform more comparisons in cases, where the elements are not uniformly distributed.

