Here's a simple C code to do Fibonacci based encryption and decryption. In this code, the Fib series starts with 0, 1, 1, 2, ..., and the shift happens accordingly. Special characters and whitespace are handled by not shifting them. The functions `encode` and `decode` are the main functions for encryption and decryption.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

#define SIZE 128

// Generate Fibonacci array
void gen_fib(int* arr, int n) {
    arr[0] = 0;
    arr[1] = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
        arr[i] = arr[i-1] + arr[i-2];
    }
}

// Encryption function
void encode(char* str, int* fib) {
    for (int i = 0; i < strlen(str); i++) {
        if (isalpha(str[i]) || isdigit(str[i])) {
            str[i] = (str[i] + fib[i % SIZE]) % 128;
        }
    }
}

// Decryption function
void decode(char* str, int* fib) {
    for (int i = 0; i < strlen(str); i++) {
        if (isalpha(str[i]) || isdigit(str[i])) {
            str[i] = (str[i] - fib[i % SIZE] + 128) % 128;
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    char message[] = "Hello World! Your score is 100.";
    int fib[SIZE];
    gen_fib(fib, SIZE);

    printf("Original message: %s\n", message);
    encode(message, fib);
    printf("Encoded message: %s\n", message);
    decode(message, fib);
    printf("Decoded message: %s\n", message);

    return 0;
}
```
This program first generates a Fibonacci sequence in the array of `fib`. Then it encrypts the input string by adding the corresponding Fibonacci number to each character's ASCII value (only if it's a alphabet or digit) and does so Modulo 128 (standard ASCII count). The decryption does the reverse operation, subtracting the Fibonacci sequence from the ASCII value.

Note: The output of the encoding might include unreadable characters as it welcomes all ASCII codes (including non-readable ones). Also, it does not handle uppercase and lowercase separately. ASCII character value difference is used for shifting, so an uppercase character may shift to a lowercase character or vice versa.

Consider the risks with this simple encryption method. This can be cracked easily because Fibonacci numbers are well known, and the sequence itself is deterministic. This code was written as an easy exercise and is not recommended for protecting sensitive information.

