Here is a Python solution for the above problem. This solution uses a queue and creates two lists to check for visited nodes and parent existence. 

```python
def validateBinaryTreeNodes(n, leftChild, rightChild):
    parent = [-1] * n
    for i in range(n):
        for child in leftChild[i], rightChild[i]:
            if child != -1:
                # If a child node has more than one parent, return False
                if parent[child] != -1:
                    return False
                parent[child] = i

    root = [i for i, p in enumerate(parent) if p == -1]

    # If there is no root or more than one root, return False
    if len(root) != 1:
        return False

    # BFS to check every node is reachable
    visited = [False] * n
    queue = [root[0]]
    while queue:
        node = queue.pop(0)
        visited[node] = True
        for child in leftChild[node], rightChild[node]:
            if child != -1:
                queue.append(child)

    # If any node is not reachable, return False
    if any(not visited[i] for i in range(n)):
        return False

    return True
```

The algorithm runs in O(n) time complexity and uses O(n) space for storing lists such as parent and visited. Note that a node should only have a single parent node, and it should only visit once during traversing all nodes using BFS. If either of these two conditions is violated, the function should return `False`. Otherwise, it should return `True` after visiting all nodes.

