In the Gregorian calendar, a common year contains 365 days and a leap year 366 days, with an extra, or intercalary, day added on February 29. The additional day ensures that the calendar year synchronizes with the solar year. Leap years generally occur every 4 years, but there are some exceptions:

1. If the year can be evenly divided by 100, it is NOT a leap year, unless;
2. The year is also evenly divisible by 400. Then it is a leap year.

Applying these rules, here is a JavaScript algorithm that checks if a given year is a leap year:

```javascript
function isGregorianLeapYear(year) {
    if(year % 4 === 0) {
        if(year % 100 === 0) {
            if(year % 400 === 0) {
                return true;
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        } else {
            return true;
        }
    } else {
        return false;
    }
}

console.log(isGregorianLeapYear(2100));
```

When you run this script, it will check if 2100 is a leap year and display the result in the console. According to the rules stated in the problem, the year 2100 is not a leap year because it is divisible by 100 but not divisible by 400.

Here is a JavaScript function that constructs Pascal's Triangle up until a user-defined limit (n).

```js
function getPascalsTriangle(n) {
    var pascalTriangle = [];
    for(var i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        pascalTriangle[i] = [];
        for(var j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
            if(j === 0 || j === i) {
                // ends of the row are always 1
                pascalTriangle[i][j] = 1;
            } else {
                // other positions are sum of the two numbers above
                pascalTriangle[i][j] = pascalTriangle[i-1][j-1] + pascalTriangle[i-1][j];
            }
        }
    }
    return pascalTriangle;
}
```

This function starts by initializing an empty array `pascalTriangle`. Then, for each row up to `n`, it creates a new array `pascalTriangle[i]`. 

For the cells in each row, it checks if the cell is at the start or end of the row using `j === 0 || j === i`, or if it's in the middle. 

If it's at the start or end, it sets the value to 1. 

If it's in the middle, it sets the value to the sum of the two cells directly above it in the previous row, as per the rule of the Pascal's triangle.

At last, the complete Pascal's Triangle is returned by the function.

