Here is the revised code implementing the Singleton pattern correctly, displaying good code practices, and adding methods to interact with siblings' data:

```javascript
class Human {
    constructor(moniker, surname, siblings=[]) {
        if (typeof moniker !== 'string' || typeof surname !== 'string') {
            throw new Error('Name and Surname should be a string');
        }

        if (!Array.isArray(siblings)) {
            throw new Error('Siblings should be an array');
        }

        if (Human.instance) {
            if (Human.instance.moniker === moniker && Human.instance.surname === surname) {
                return Human.instance;
            } else {
                throw new Error('An instance with a different name and surname already exists');
            }
        }

        this.moniker = moniker;
        this.surname = surname;
        this.siblings = siblings;
        Human.instance = this;
    }

    addSibling(sibling) {
        if (typeof sibling === 'string') {
            this.siblings.push(sibling);
        } else {
            throw new Error('Sibling name should be a string');
        }
    }

    getSibling(index) {
        if (this.siblings[index]) {
            return this.siblings[index];
        } else {
            throw new Error('No sibling at this index');
        }
    }

    removeSibling(index) {
        if (this.siblings[index]) {
            this.siblings.splice(index, 1);
        } else {
            throw new Error('No sibling at this index');
        }
    }

    updateSibling(index, newSibling) {
        if (this.siblings[index]) {
            if (typeof newSibling === 'string') {
                this.siblings[index] = newSibling;
            } else {
                throw new Error('New sibling name should be a string');
            }
        } else {
            throw new Error('No sibling at this index');
        }
    }
}

```

In this revised code:

- The constructor performs extensive error checking and creates a singleton instance if it does not exist. If an instance already exists, the constructor checks whether the provided name and surname match the existing one. If they don't, an error is thrown; if they do, the existing instance is returned.
- The `addSibling` method adds a sibling to the siblings array if the provided sibling is a string.
- The `getSibling` method returns a sibling at a certain index or throws an error if there's no sibling at this index.
- The `removeSibling` method removes a sibling at a certain index or throws an error if there's no sibling at this index.
- The `updateSibling` method updates a sibling at a certain index to a new value, throwing an error if either there's no sibling at this index or the new value is not a string.

