Here's a JavaScript function named buildArrayPascalTriangle, which can create a deeply layered, hierarchical array based on Pascal's Triangle:

```javascript
function buildArrayPascalTriangle(n) {
    // Initialize the top-tier array 
    const arr = new Array(n);

    // Iteratively construct the Pascal's Triangle
    for (let line = 0; line < n; line++) {
        arr[line] = new Array(line + 1);

        for (let i = 0; i <= line; i++) {
            arr[line][i] = new Array(i + 1);
            if (line == i || i == 0)
                arr[line][i] = 1;
            else
                arr[line][i] = arr[line - 1][i - 1] + arr[line - 1][i];
        }
    }

    return arr;
}
```

This function constructs Pascal's triangle by filling in each number, which is always the sum of the two numbers directly above it.

For example, calling `buildArrayPascalTriangle(5)` would return:

```javascript
[
  [1],
  [1, 1],
  [1, 2, 1],
  [1, 3, 3, 1],
  [1, 4, 6, 4, 1]
]
```
