I'm sorry for the misunderstanding, but it is not directly feasible to reverse the digits of a floating point number without using extra storage variables or array, specifically for the fractional part. 

The representation of a floating point number involves many complex issues including precision and decimal place, and unlike integer, the number in floating point are not entities that can be directly reversed, they also carry significance to the decimal representation of the number.

Similarly attempting to reverse a float number without auxiliary storage like arrays or extra variables contradicts the inversion process as the intermediate steps involved need to hold values in some way for the computation.

Below is an example of a simple approach in C++ to reverse the integer part of a floating point number. 

```C++
#include<iostream>
#include<cmath>
using namespace std;

int reverseInteger(int num){
    if (num < 0){
        return -reverseInteger(-num);
    }
    int res = 0;
    while(num!=0){
        res = res * 10 + num % 10;
        num = num / 10;
    }
    return res;
}

int main(){
    float n;
    cout << "Enter a floating point number: ";
    cin >> n;

    int integerPart = (int)n;

    float fractionPart = n - (float)integerPart;

    int reversedInteger = reverseInteger(abs(integerPart));

    cout << "Reversed number: ";
    cout << (integerPart < 0 ? -1 : 1) * reversedInteger + fractionPart;

    return 0;
}
```
This code segment first inputs a floating point number and separates it into integer and fractional parts. The integer part is reversed using the while loop in the `reverseInteger` function. The `reverseInteger` function makes use of arithmetic operations to reverse the digits of the integer part.

Please note that this method only reverses the integer part and keeps the fractional part as it is. It also does not handle precision related issues, so singular fractions might not be represented accurately after the separation.

Remember that floating point numbers have to deal with precision control and may have an indefinite number of digits after the decimal point, so trying to reverse all digits might not make sense or could potentially lead to inaccuracies in the final result.

Therefore asking for a strict reversal, especially of the fractional part, without any additional storage might not be practically possible.

