Thread safety can be achieved in C++ using different synchronization primitives like mutexes, condition variables etc. Here, the example of a thread-safe circular queue is implemented where all public methods are guarded by a std::mutex to ensure mutual exclusion:

```cpp
#include <mutex>
#include <vector>
#include <exception>

class OverflowException : public std::exception {
    const char* what() const noexcept override {
        return "Overflow: the circular queue is full.\n";
    }
};

class UnderflowException : public std::exception {
    const char* what() const noexcept override {
        return "Underflow: the circular queue is empty.\n";
    }
};

template<typename T>
class CircularQueue
{
public:
    explicit CircularQueue(size_t size)
        : data(size),
          head(0),
          tail(0),
          count(0),
          capacity(size)
    {
    }

    void enqueue(const T& val)
    {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        if (isFull())
            throw OverflowException();

        data[tail] = val;
        tail = (tail + 1) % capacity;
        ++count;
    }

    T dequeue()
    {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        if (isEmpty())
            throw UnderflowException();

        T result = data[head];
        head = (head + 1) % capacity;
        --count;
        return result;
    }

    T front() const
    {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        if (isEmpty())
            throw UnderflowException();

        return data[head];
    }

    T rear() const
    {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

        if (isEmpty())
            throw UnderflowException();

        return data[(tail - 1 + capacity) % capacity];
    }

    bool isFull() const
    {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        return count == capacity;
    }

    bool isEmpty() const
    {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        return count == 0;
    }

private:
    std::vector<T> data;
    size_t head;
    size_t tail;
    size_t count;
    size_t capacity;

    mutable std::mutex mtx;
};
```

In the code above, notice that:

- We declare two classes `OverflowException` and `UnderflowException` to handle overflow and underflow conditions. These classes inherit from the standard exception base class.
- A `std::mutex` is used to ensure that only one thread can execute any of the public methods at any given time. This is achieved by using `std::lock_guard` which locks the mutex in its constructor and releases it in its destructor.
- All public methods are locked with a `std::lock_guard`. This ensures that our circular queue implementation is thread-safe.
- The `isFull` and `isEmpty` methods check if the queue is full or empty, and return a boolean result.
- The `front` and `rear` methods return the front and rear elements of the queue.
- The `enqueue` adds an item at the end of the queue, and `dequeue` removes an item from the front of the queue. If the queue is full or empty, an exception is thrown.

