Here is a Java program implementing a simple LinkedList interface with methods for adding and removing elements:

```java
interface LinkedListInterface<T> {
    void add(T element);
   void remove(T element);
}

class Node<T> {
    T data;
    Node<T> next;

    Node(T data) {
        this.data = data;
        next = null;
    }
}

class LinkedList<T> implements LinkedListInterface<T> {
    Node<T> head;

    @Override
    public void add(T element) {
        Node<T> newNode = new Node<>(element);
        if(head == null) {
            head = newNode;
        } else {
            Node<T> currentNode = head;
            while(currentNode.next != null) {
                currentNode = currentNode.next;
            }
            currentNode.next = newNode;
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void remove(T element) {
        if(head == null) return;
        if(head.data.equals(element)) {
            head = head.next;
            return;
        }
        Node<T> currentNode = head;
        while(currentNode.next != null) {
            if(currentNode.next.data.equals(element)) {
                currentNode.next = currentNode.next.next;
                return;
            }
            currentNode = currentNode.next;
        }
    }
}


public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedList<Integer> linkedList = new LinkedList<>();
        linkedList.add(5);
        linkedList.add(10);
        linkedList.add(15);
        linkedList.remove(10);
        // The linked list now contains: 5 -> 15
    }
}
```

This code defines an interface `LinkedListInterface`. Then we define a `Node` class which represents a node in the linked list. After that, `LinkedList` class implementing `LinkedListInterface` is defined, implementing methods for adding and removing elements. In the `Main` class, we use these classes to create a linked list, add elements to it, and remove an element.

Note that this is a very simpler version of LinkedList with limited functionality just to demonstrate the interface.

