Here is a JavaScript solution to the problem. This algorithm utilizes recursion to traverse through the nested arrays, keeping track of the depth level and finding the two most significant numbers along the way.

```javascript
function mostSignificantNumbers(array, depth = 1) {
  let max1 = { value: Number.MIN_SAFE_INTEGER, position: [], depth: 0 };
  let max2 = { value: Number.MIN_SAFE_INTEGER, position: [], depth: 0 };

  function traverse(arr, pos = [], level = 1) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
      let newPos = pos.concat(i);
      if (Array.isArray(arr[i])) {
        traverse(arr[i], newPos, level + 1);
      } else {
        if (arr[i] > max1.value) {
          max2 = max1;
          max1 = { value: arr[i], position: newPos, depth: level };
        } else if (arr[i] > max2.value) {
          max2 = { value: arr[i], position: newPos, depth: level };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  traverse(array);

  return { max1, max2 };
}

let array = [[3, 6, 8, [5, [9]], 1], 7, [10, [2, [15]]]];
let result = mostSignificantNumbers(array);
console.log(result);
```

This function will return an object containing two objects `max1` and `max2` for the two most significant numbers respectively. Each of these objects contains:
1. `value`: the value of the significant number,
2. `position`: an array representing the indices needed to access the number in the nested array, and
3. `depth`: the depth level of the number in the nested array.

In the case of the provided array as input, it would return:

```javascript
{
  max1: { value: 15, position: [1, 2, 1], depth: 3 },
  max2: { value: 10, position: [1, 0], depth: 2 }
}
```
This means that `15` is the most significant number, which is found at the 3rd depth level at the position array `[1, 2, 1]`. The second most significant number is `10`, found at the 2nd depth level at the position `[1, 0]`.

