In Java, we can utilize built in classes such as String, StringBuilder, Character, and thread ExecutorService to develop the solution. It's important to note that words like 'of' and 'and' are not reversed since they are prepositions and conjunctions.

Here is the code solution:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class Main {
    private static String sentence = "Hello world of artificial intelligence";
    private static final List<String> excludeList = Arrays.asList("of", "and", "but", "or", "yet", "so");

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10); // create a task pool of up to 10 threads
        List<Future<String>> list = new ArrayList<>();
        String[] words = sentence.split(" ");

        for (String word: words) {
            if (excludeList.contains(word.toLowerCase())) {
                list.add(CompletableFuture.completedFuture(word)); // No need to reverse. So, complete this future now.
            } else {
                Future<String> future = executor.submit(new WordReverser(word));
                list.add(future);
            }
        }

        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
        for(Future<String> future: list) {
            result.append(future.get());
            result.append(' ');
        }

        executor.shutdown(); // shut down the executor
        System.out.println(result.toString());
    }

    public static class WordReverser implements Callable<String> {
        private String word;

        public WordReverser(String word) {
            this.word = word;
        }

        @Override
        public String call() throws Exception {
            StringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder(word.length());
            for (char ch: word.toCharArray()) {
                if (Character.isLowerCase(ch)) {
                    builder.insert(0, Character.toLowerCase(ch));
                } else {
                    builder.insert(0, Character.toUpperCase(ch));
                }
            }
            return builder.toString();
        }
    }
}
```

In this program, we split the sentence into words and then for each word submit a new task to the executor service to reverse the word. We keep track of the futures and then collect the results in original order when all tasks complete. The WordReverser class implements Callable interface and does the word reversal in its call method. It keeps the uppercase letters uppercase and lower case letters lower case. If the word is a preposition or conjunction, we don't reverse it. 

Please note that multi-threading part here is mainly for illustration purpose, not something that is necessary in this specific problem, as the overhead of creating threads may outweigh the benefit of parallelism. But in a real world scenario if the operations on words were more complex and time-consuming could benefit from parallel execution.

