Here is a clean and efficient solution in C++. This function takes as parameters the roots of the two pre-sorted linked lists, recursively merges them in a sorted manner and finally returns the head of the merged linked list.

The Linked List Node definition may look like:
```cpp
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node(int x) : data(x), next(NULL) {} // Node constructor
};
```

The function to merge two sorted lists looks like:

```cpp
Node* mergeSortedLists(Node* head1, Node* head2) {
    // Base cases
    if (head1 == NULL) return head2;
    if (head2 == NULL) return head1;

    Node* mergedHead = NULL; // This will be the head of merged list

    // Pick the smaller value between the two lists
    if (head1->data < head2->data) {
        mergedHead = head1;
        mergedHead->next = mergeSortedLists(head1->next, head2);
    } else {
        mergedHead = head2;
        mergedHead->next = mergeSortedLists(head1, head2->next);
    }

    return mergedHead;
}
```

This function works by first checking if any of the lists have reached their end. If one has, then it just returns the head of the other list, since there is nothing else to merge. If not, it picks the node with the less value, sets that as the head of the merged list, and then continues the process with the next element of that list and the current element of the other list. The function continues in this recursive manner until one of the list reaches its end. At that point, the function just returns the head of the other list for further merging. This merged link gets attached to the correct place via recursive call stacks. This algorithm runs in O(n) time, where n is the total number of nodes in the linked list.

