#include <stdio.h>

// A generic function to swap elements
void swapElements(int arr[], int x, int y) {
    // Checking for array bounds
    if(x >= sizeof(arr) || y >= sizeof(arr) || x < 0 || y < 0){
        printf("Invalid indices\n");
        return;
    }

    int temp = arr[x];
    arr[x] = arr[y];
    arr[y] = temp;
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    printf("Before Swapping: ");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++){
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }

    swapElements(arr, 0, 3);

    printf("\nAfter Swapping: ");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++){
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Now let's intentionally introduce a flaw into this function and discuss it:

```c
void swapElements(int arr[], int x, int y) {
    int arr_copy[] = arr;

    int temp = arr_copy[x];
    arr_copy[x] = arr_copy[y];
    arr_copy[y] = temp;
}
```

In this flawed code, I created a copy of the incoming array and then swapped the elements of this copy. This code will compile and run smoothly, but won't give the expected result because the changes made by this function won't reflect back in the main function.

This is because in C, arrays are passed to functions by value, not by reference. So, creating a copy of the array and swapping its elements doesn't affect the original array.

This problem can be fixed by directly swapping the intended elements of the original array rather than its copy, as demonstrated in the correct code.

