Here is a function in JavaScript that will find the second largest number in an array:

```javascript
function secondLargest(arr) {
    let max = null;
    let secondMax = null;

    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        let currentNumber = arr[i];

        if (max == null || currentNumber > max) {
            secondMax = max;
            max = currentNumber;
        } else if (secondMax == null || (currentNumber > secondMax && currentNumber < max)) {
            secondMax = currentNumber;
        }
    }

    return secondMax;
}

let arr = [5, 19, 2, 46, 15, 31];
console.log(secondLargest(arr)); // it will return 31 which is the second largest number
```

The function `secondLargest()` loops through the array comparing each number with the current max and second max. If it finds a number greater than the current max, it moves the max to second max and updates the max. If it finds a number that is not greater than the max but is greater than the second max (and is not equal to the max), it updates the second max. At the end of the loop, it will return the second max.

