To measure the efficiency of a vehicle, I am choosing to divide the speed of the vehicle (in km/h) by the fuel consumed to sustain that speed (in liters per hour). This essentially gives us a measure of "distance traveled per unit of fuel consumed", which is a typical way of measuring vehicle efficiency.

Code:
```c++
class Object {  // Class names should be capitalized
public:
    Object() : engine_capacity(0.0), fuel_type("") {};
    Object(double engine_capacity, std::string fuel_type) : engine_capacity(engine_capacity), fuel_type(fuel_type) {};
    virtual ~Object() {};  // The destructor should be virtual for base classes

protected:
    double engine_capacity;
    std::string fuel_type;

public:
    virtual double calculateEfficiency(int speed, int fuelConsumption) {
        if (fuelConsumption == 0)
            throw std::invalid_argument("Fuel consumption cannot be zero!");
        return static_cast<double>(speed) / fuelConsumption;
    }
};

class Vehicle : public Object { // Class should inherit from Object correctly
public:
    Vehicle(double engine_capacity, std::string fuel_type) : Object(engine_capacity, fuel_type) {}
    ~Vehicle() override {}
};

class Car : public Vehicle {
public:
    Car(double engine_capacity, std::string fuel_type) : Vehicle(engine_capacity, fuel_type) {}
    ~Car() override {}

    // For cars, we might consider additional factors like aerodynamics or weight into the efficiency calculation
    double calculateEfficiency(int speed, int fuelConsumption) override {
        if (fuelConsumption == 0)
            throw std::invalid_argument("Fuel consumption cannot be zero!");
        return (static_cast<double>(speed) * 0.9) / fuelConsumption;  //0.9 is a constant representing aerodynamics adjustments
    }
};

class Bicycle : public Vehicle {
public:
    Bicycle() : Vehicle(0.0, "None") {}
    ~Bicycle() override {}

    // Bicycles don't consume any fuel, thus we can consider its efficiency to be proportional to its speed
    double calculateEfficiency(int speed, int fuelConsumption) override {
        return static_cast<double>(speed);
    }
};
```
The additional method, classes, and handling of errors have been addressed in the provided code. The classes Car and Bicycle have different definitions of efficiency, as explained in the comments above. For the error handling, an exception is thrown when the fuelConsumption is equal to zero (since division by zero is undefined). 

Please note the use of the 'override' keyword, this is not strictly necessary but it is good programming practice to use it when we are overwriting virtual functions in C++, as it helps catch errors.

