Java does not support multiple inheritance with classes, but you can achieve something similar using Java interfaces with default methods (from Java 8 onwards) to illustrate the Diamond Problem. However, in this case, you have to manually resolve the ambiguity by providing an implementation of the `display()` method in class `D`. Here's how it can be done:

```java
class A {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Class A");
    }
}

interface B extends A {
    default void display() {
        System.out.println("Class B");
    }
}

interface C extends A {
    default void display() {
        System.out.println("Class C");
    }
}

class D implements B, C {
    public void display() {
        B.super.display(); // You can call either B.super.display() or C.super.display() to resolve the ambiguity
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        D obj = new D();
        obj.display();
    }
}
```

Output will be:

```
Class B
```

You can change the implementation of the `display()` method in class `D` to choose which interface's `display()` to call (either `B.super.display()` or `C.super.display()`).

