Sure, please find below a piece of Javascript code demonstrating the difference between the `===` and `==` operators. Type coercion in JavaScript means the automatic or implicit conversion of values from one data type to another.

```javascript
// Example 1
let num = 0;
let str = '0';

console.log(num === str); // false because '===' checks type and value
console.log(num == str);  // true because '==' checks only value, string is coerced into a number

// Example 2
let num1 = 1;
let bool = true;

console.log(num1 === bool); // false because '===' checks type and value
console.log(num1 == bool);  // true because '==' checks only value, boolean true is coerced into a number 1

// Example 3
let nullVar = null;
let undefinedVar = undefined;

console.log(nullVar === undefinedVar); // false because '===' checks type and value
console.log(nullVar == undefinedVar);  // true because '==' checks only value, null and undefined are considered equal

// Example 4
let array = [];
let obj = {};

console.log(array == obj); // false because object and array are different even though they might be similar in structure
console.log(array === obj); // also false

// Example 5
let strBool = "true";
let boolVal = true;

console.log(strBool == boolVal); // False: Here '==' gives unexpected results due to type coercion
console.log(strBool === boolVal); // False: '===' gives the correct result because the types of string and boolean are different.

```

From the above examples, it's evident that `==` operator may give unexpected results in certain circumstances due to type coercion. So it's generally recommended to use `===` which enforces both type and value equality.

