Here is a solution to the problem in Java. 

Trick is to recursively pop elements from the stack and hold the popped one, until the stack becomes empty. Then for each popped element, add it to the sum and push it back onto the stack. 

```java
import java.util.Stack;

public class Main {

    public static int calculateSum(Stack<Integer> data) {
        if (data.isEmpty()) { 
            return 0;
        } else {
            int num = data.pop();
            int sum = calculateSum(data) + num; 
            data.push(num);
            return sum;
        }
    }


    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Stack<Integer> data = new Stack();
        data.push(1);
        data.push(2);
        data.push(3);
        data.push(4);
        data.push(5);

        System.out.println("Sum of elements in stack is " + calculateSum(data));
    }
}
```

This solution uses recursion to calculate the sum of elements in the stack. It pops each element off the stack, recursively calculates the sum of the remaining elements, then adds the popped element to this sum. Finally, it pushes the popped element back onto the stack so that the original stack is preserved.

The stopping criterion for the recursion is when the stack becomes empty, in which case the function returns 0.

