```C#
int x = 25;
int y = 45;
Console.WriteLine("The total is " + (x + y));
```

In your original program, you tried to directly combine the variables x and y with the string without performing the addition first. 

In C#, as in many other programming languages, operations are executed from left to right unless otherwise specified with parenthesis. So in this case, `"The total is " + x + y` is evaluated as `("The total is " + x) + y`; the program first combines the string with the value of x, and then "adds" the value of y to the new string. 

By putting `x + y` in parentheses, we ensure that this addition is executed before combining the result with the string.

Alternatively, string interpolation can be used to make the code cleaner and easier to read:

```C#
int x = 25;
int y = 45;
Console.WriteLine($"The total is {x + y}");
```

