Creating a perl program for this problem would essentially involve breaking down the text into sequences of three words (lexemes) and maintaining a hash (associative array) for keeping track of the frequencies. Below you will find a simple Perl solution for your problem:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use feature 'say';

# Convert the text into lowercase and split it into words
my $text = "I adore apples but not as much as I adore apricots because apricots are healthier than apples but not as sweet as apples. I suppose it's a good thing that apricots aren't as sweet as apples because then I'd probably like apricots more than apples.";
$text = lc $text;
my @words = split /\s+/, $text;

# Create a hash to track the frequency of each triplet
my %triplets;

for my $i (0 .. $#words - 2) {
    my $triplet = join ' ', @words[$i .. $i+2];
    $triplets{$triplet}++;
}

# Print the triplets that appear more than once
foreach my $triplet (sort { $triplets{$b} <=> $triplets{$a} } keys %triplets) {
    if ($triplets{$triplet} > 1) {
        say "'$triplet' - $triplets{$triplet} times";
    }
}
```

This script will first normalize the input text by converting it to lowercase, then split the text into individual words. It then creates sequences of three words (triplets) and increments a counter for each triplet in a hash. At the end, it sorts and prints the triplets that appear more than once along with their frequency.

Here's a simple program to demonstrate your requirements:

```C
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    while(1) {
        printf("Infinite loop\n");

        // Erroneous code that won't affect the loop
        int err = "error";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The erroneous line `int err = "error";` is not valid because it's trying to assign a `char string` ("error") to an `integer variable` (err). The compiler will raise an error or warning because of incompatible types at this line.

However, this does not affect the infinite loop because the error is a compile-time error, not a runtime error. A compile-time error causes the compiler to stop compilation and spit out an error message because it encounters something it cannot translate into machine code. So, the error is found and necessary steps are taken before the program ever runs. As this error is inside the infinite loop, the infinite loop itself will not run due to this compile-time error but assuming that we somehow bypass this compile-time error, this faulty statement can't stop the infinite loop.

Please note that you cannot actually compile this script as C is a statically typed language and the code has a compile-time error. The line of code `int err = "error";` is merely there for illustrative purposes and will not compile.

If you want a program that compiles, with an error that does not affect an infinite loop, consider the program below:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int x = 0;
    while(1) {
        printf("Infinite loop\n");

        // Erroneous code that won't affect the loop
        x = x / 0;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
The division by zero error `x = x / 0;` is a run-time error, or exception, which is not caught by the compiler but will trigger a runtime error. However, because the value of `x` is never used, the runtime error is not actually triggered, and the infinite loop continues indefinitely. If you would read or print the x, the program would stop with an error.

