Here's the JavaScript function to demonstrate how to inject a CSS class to a specific div element. This function uses `document.querySelectorAll` method to cycle through every div element on the HTML document and check if the text content of the div element matches the "Hello World" string. If it does, it'll add a new CSS class to this div. 

```javascript
function dynamicClassInject(targetText, newClass) {
    document.querySelectorAll("div").forEach(function(divElement){
        if (divElement.textContent.trim() === targetText) {
            divElement.classList.add(newClass);
        }
    });
}

dynamicClassInject("Hello World", "changeTextColor");
```

In this function, you need to already have a CSS class defined in your CSS file or in a style tag, for example:

```css
<style>
    .changeTextColor {
        color: red;
    }
</style>
```

However, let's take a look at an erroneous version of the above function:

```javascript
function dynamicClassInject(targetText, newClass) {
    document.querySelectorAll("div").forEach(function(divElement){
        if (divElement.innerHtml.trim() === targetText) {
            divElement.classList.add(newClass);
        }
    });
}

dynamicClassInject("Hello World", "changeTextColor");
```

The error in this piece of code lies in the condition of the if statement. The property used should be `textContent` instead of `innerHtml`. This is because `textContent` gets the content of all elements, including <script> and <style> elements. In contrast, innerHTML gets the content of all elements, excluding <script> and <style> elements. So when we want to match string exactly, we should use `textContent`. Therefore, `divElement.innerHtml.trim()` should be replaced with `divElement.textContent.trim()`.

Sure, below is a PHP subroutine which will isolate words beginning with a specified character from an array of lexemes.

```php
function wordsStartingWith($array, $char) {
    return array_filter($array, function ($word) use ($char) {
        return strpos($word, $char) === 0;
    });
}

$array = ["apple", "ball", "cat", "dart", "elephant"];
$char = 'e';

$result = wordsStartingWith($array, $char);
print_r($result);
```

In this subroutine, the `array_filter` function is used on the input array `$array` to filter through each item. It uses an anonymous function as the callback, which takes each word of the array as an argument. The `strpos()` function is used to determine if the target character `$char` is the first character of the word. If it is (i.e., the position is `0`), that item is included in the resulting output array. The output is an array of all the words that start with the specified character.

The `use` keyword imports the `$char` variable from the parent scope into the scope of the anonymous function.

The `print_r($result);` statement is used to print the resultant array.

