Here is a simple way to implement a Paragraph palindrome in Java:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.lang.StringBuilder;

public class PalindromeChecker {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Enter a sentence to check");
        String palindrome = in.nextLine();
        try{
            checkPalindrome(palindrome);
        }
        catch(Exception e) {
            System.out.println("An error occurred, please try again");
        }

        in.close();
    }

    public static void checkPalindrome(String sentence) {
        sentence = sentence.replaceAll("\\W", "").toLowerCase();
        StringBuilder build = new StringBuilder(sentence);
        build.reverse();
        if(sentence.equals(build.toString())) {
            System.out.println("This sentence is a palindrome");
        }
        else {
            System.out.println("This sentence is not a palindrome");
        }
    }

}
```
In the code above, the method `checkPalindrome` takes in a string argument. The string is then converted to lower case and all non-alphanumeric characters (spaces, punctuation, etc) are replaced with an empty string to make the palindrome checking more reliable. We then reverse the string and check if the reversed string is equal to the original, if so it's a palindrome otherwise it's not.

The code uses `StringBuilder` to reverse the string and regex (`\\W`) to replace all non-alphanumeric characters. If an invalid input is provided (not in the scanner), the system will catch this as an error, print out "An error occurred, please try again" and end gracefully. The scanner is then closed to prevent memory leaks.

Please note that this is a simple implementation and you might need to adjust the program according to detailed requirements of your project, like how to handle numeric characters or special characters in the sentence.

