Here is a Python solution to find the penultimate highest value in the list using a selection sort-like algorithm. It iterates through the list, keeping track of the top two highest values found so far and replacing them when a higher value is found.

```python
def penultimate_max(arr):
    if len(arr) < 2:
        return None  # If the list has less than two elements, there is no penultimate max
    max1, max2 = (arr[0], arr[1]) if arr[0] > arr[1] else (arr[1], arr[0])

    for num in arr[2:]:
        if num > max1:
            max1, max2 = num, max1  # If number is greater than max1, update max1 and demote max1 to max2
        elif num > max2:
            max2 = num  # If number is not higher than max1 but higher than max2, then update max2
    return max2


print(penultimate_max([10, 33, 21, 50, 100, 55, 78]))
```

When you run this algorithm, it will return 78 which is the second largest number in the given list.

This algorithm works in O(n) time where n is the length of the list as it only requires a single pass through the list. It works by saving the highest and second highest numbers found so far in the list, and updating these as appropriate as each number in the list is considered.

