Here is a simple Java implementation of the task. This program takes an array of integers, a target value and retrieves the 10 numbers which are closest to the target.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    int[] arr = {95, 32, 21, 47, 56, 78, 12, 65, 89, 45, 66, 76, 88, 94, 29, 18, 100, 1, 22, 35};
    int target = 50;

    System.out.println("The ten closest numbers to " + target + " are: " + findClosest(arr, target));
  }

  static List<Integer> findClosest(int[] arr, int target) {
    PriorityQueue<NumWithDiff> pq = new PriorityQueue<>((n1, n2) -> n2.diff - n1.diff);

    for(int num : arr) {
      pq.add(new NumWithDiff(num, Math.abs(num - target)));
      if(pq.size() > 10) {
        pq.poll();
      }
    }

    List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
    while(!pq.isEmpty()) {
      res.add(pq.poll().num);  
    }

    return res;
  }
}

class NumWithDiff {
  int num;
  int diff;

  public NumWithDiff(int num, int diff) {
    this.num = num;
    this.diff = diff;
  }
}
```

This solution leverages a priority queue to constantly maintain the 10 numbers with the smallest difference to the target. As we iterate through the array, we calculate the absolute difference between each number and the target, then we add it to the queue. If the size of the queue exceeds 10, we remove the number with the largest difference. At the end, we pull the remaining elements from the queue and return them as our result.

Here is an example Vue.js mixin that updates the "currentPage" data based on the "page" query parameter of the current URL:

```JavaScript
export default {
    data() {
        return {
            currentPage: null,
        }
    },
    methods: {
      setQueryParams() {
         this.currentPage = parseInt(this.$route.query.page, 10) || 1
      }
    },
    watch: {
        '$route.query.page': 'setQueryParams'
    },
    created() {
        this.setQueryParams()
    }
}
```

Here is an explanation of what's happening in this mixin:

- In "data", we declare our `currentPage` variable that we will be updating based on the "page" query parameter.

- In "methods", we define a `setQueryParams` method, which parses the query parameter and assigns it to `currentPage`. If the "page" parameter isn't present or isn't a number, `currentPage` is set to 1.

- In "watch", we watch for changes to the "page" query parameter in the URL with `'$route.query.page'`. Whenever this changes, we call `setQueryParams` to update `currentPage`.

- Finally, in the "created" lifecycle hook, we call `setQueryParams` to set the initial value of `currentPage` when the component is created. This is because the "watch" only triggers when the "page" query parameter changes after component creation.

This mixin can be added to any Vue component and it will automatically keep `currentPage` in sync with the "page" query parameter.

