Here is a C++ solution using the `std::nth_element` and `std::sort` functions.

```c++
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

std::vector<int> sort_second(std::vector<int> vec) {
    std::vector<int> mult_of_2;
    std::vector<int> mult_of_3;

    for (int i = 0; i < vec.size(); ++i) {
        if ((i+1) % 2 == 0) {
            mult_of_2.push_back(vec[i]);
        }   
        if ((i+1) % 3 == 0) {
            mult_of_3.push_back(vec[i]);
        }  
    }

    std::sort(mult_of_2.begin(), mult_of_2.end(), std::greater<int>());
    std::sort(mult_of_3.begin(), mult_of_3.end());

    for (int i = 0, j = 0, k = 0; i < vec.size(); ++i) {
        if ((i+1) % 2 == 0 && (i+1) % 3 == 0) {
            vec[i] = mult_of_3[k] > mult_of_2[j] ? mult_of_2[j++] : mult_of_3[k++];
        }   
        else if ((i+1) % 2 == 0) {
            vec[i] = mult_of_2[j++];
        }
        else if ((i+1) % 3 == 0) {
            vec[i] = mult_of_3[k++];
        }
    }

    return vec;
}
```

In the function above, we prepare two vectors: `mult_of_2` and `mult_of_3`, that hold elements at positions multiple of 2 and 3, respectively. After sorting `mult_of_2` in descending order and `mult_of_3` in ascending order, we then traverse the original vector `vec` again and replace the elements at positions multiple of 2 with the next elements from `mult_of_2`, and at positions multiple of 3 with the next elements from `mult_of_3`. 

In case an index is a common multiple of 2 and 3, we have to ensure that the requirement of descending order for multiples of 2 and ascending order for multiples of 3 is not violated. Hence, we choose the smaller one between the next element of `mult_of_2` and `mult_of_3`.

Please note that this function starts the position count from 1. So if it says "positions that are multiples of 2", it means the second position, fourth position, sixth position, etc. (not zero-based indexing).

