Here is a simple algorithm to reverse a singly linked list in C:

1. Initialize three pointers: previous (prev), current (curr), and next (next).

2. Assign the head of the list to curr.

3. Loop through the linked list until curr is not `NULL`.

4. Assign the next node to next.

5. Change the 'next' of the current node to prev, which reverses the link.

6. Move prev and curr one step forward.

7. Once the loop finishes (when curr is `NULL`), change the head to prev because it will be the last non-NULL node.

The code for the algorithm is as follows:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct Node
{
    int data;
    struct Node* next;
};

// Function to reverse the linked list
void reverse(struct Node** head_ref)
{
    struct Node* prev   = NULL;
    struct Node* curr = *head_ref;
    struct Node* next = NULL;
    while (curr != NULL)
    {
        next  = curr->next; 
        curr->next = prev;  
        prev = curr;
        curr = next;
    }
    *head_ref = prev;
}

// Function to push a node
void push(struct Node** head_ref, int new_data)
{
    // allocate node
    struct Node* new_node =
        (struct Node*) malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    // put in the data 
    new_node->data  = new_data;
    // link the old list off the new node
    new_node->next = (*head_ref);     
    // move the head to point to the new node
    (*head_ref) = new_node;
}

// Function to print linked list
void printList(struct Node *head)
{
    struct Node *temp = head;
    while(temp != NULL)
    {
        printf("%d  ", temp->data);    
        temp = temp->next;  
    }
}

int main()
{
    struct Node* head = NULL;

    push(&head, 20);
    push(&head, 4);
    push(&head, 15); 
    push(&head, 85);      

    printf("Original Linked list\n");
    printList(head);

    reverse(&head);                      
    printf("\nReversed Linked list \n");
    printList(head);   

    return 0;
}
```

In this program, `push()` is used to insert nodes into the linked list and `printList()` is used to print the linked list. `reverse()` is the key function that reverses the linked list. This code adds four nodes to the list and then calls `reverse()`, thus displaying the reversed list. 
Please do note that this program should be executed in an environment that supports C syntax.

