Function overloading and function overriding are key concepts in Object-oriented programming(OOP). Though they both involve the use of the same function name for various uses, their usage, scope, and functionality are different. 

1. Function Overloading:

Function overloading is a feature of Programming languages like C++, Java that allows creating several methods with the same name which differ from each other in terms of the type of the input and the output of the function. It's compile-time polymorphism. It's mainly used to enhance the readability of the program.

For instance, in C++, we can have:

```cpp
void func(int x) {
    cout << "value is " << x << endl;
}

void func(double x) {
    cout << "Value is " << x << endl;
}

void func(int x, int y) {
    cout << "Value is " << x * y << endl;
}
```
Different methods have the same name "func" but different signatures. One method has one integer parameter, one has double parameter, while the third has two integer parameters. 

2. Function Overriding:

Function overriding is used for runtime polymorphism. It happens in the same inheritance chain, and both base and derived classes have the same function name with the same parameters. The method that is going to be called is determined at runtime based on the object used to call the function.

Here's a code snippet in Java: 

```java
class Animal {
   void sound(){
	System.out.println("Animal is making a sound");   
   }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
   @Override
   void sound(){
	System.out.println("Meow");
   }
}

public class Main {
   public static void main(String args[]){
	Animal obj = new Cat();
	obj.sound();
   }
}
```
The sound method in Animal class is overridden by the sound method of Cat class, and the type of the object determined which method is to be executed.

Differences:
i. Overloading is the concept of using one function or operator for more than one purpose while overriding means to provide a new implementation for the base class function in the derived class.
ii. Overloading is an example of compile-time polymorphism, but overriding is an example of dynamic or run time polymorphism.
iii. Overloading is performed within the class. Overriding occurs in two classes that have an IS-A relationship.

Practical Applications:
i. Function overloading is used when we want to perform one operation with different types or numbers of arguments.
ii. Function overriding is used to provide specific implementation of the method that is already provided by its superclass.

Implications:
i. Use of proper function overloading will lead to better, cleaner, and more understandable code.
ii. Function overriding enables Java to support specific behavior through the same method, improving the readability of the program.

