Here is a Java function that uses the Euclidean algorithm to recursively find the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD) of two numbers:

```java
public class GCD {
    public static int gcd(int num1, int num2) {
        if (num2 == 0) {
            return num1;
        } else {
            return gcd(num2, num1 % num2);
        }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(gcd(60, 48));  // Outputs 12
    }
}
```

In the above code, function `gcd` is defined that takes two integers num1 and num2 as inputs. The function recursively calls itself with num2 and the remainder of num1/num2 until num2 is 0. At that point, it returns num1, which is the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD) of the original two numbers.

In the main function, the `gcd` function is called with two integers (e.g., 60 and 48) and the output is printed.

Here is a JavaScript function to find the median from a set of numerical elements. This function first sorts the list of numbers to identify the middle number(s). If the list has an odd length, it returns the middle number directly. If it has an even length, it gets the average of the two middle numbers to calculate the median:

```javascript
function findMedian(arr) {
    // Sort the array in ascending order
    arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);

    // Calculate the index of the middle element
    let midIndex = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);

    // If the array length is even, the median is the average of the two middle elements
    if(arr.length % 2 === 0) {
        return (arr[midIndex - 1] + arr[midIndex]) / 2;
    }
    // If the array length is odd, the median is the middle element
    else {
        return arr[midIndex];
    }
}

console.log(findMedian([2, 7, 12, 15]));  // output: 9.5
```

This function can handle the edge cases if the length of the array is even or odd. The sort functionality of JavaScript handles the placement of duplicate numbers naturally. Always remember that the median calculation requires the numbers to be sorted in numerical order before identifying the central number, so don't forget the sorting step.

