First, we need to define the structure of our doubly linked list.

```cpp
#include <iostream>

// Node structure
struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node* next;
    struct Node* prev;
};

struct Node* newNode(int data) {
    struct Node* new_node = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    new_node->data = data;
    new_node->next = NULL;
    new_node->prev = NULL;
    return new_node;
}
```

The key to this problem is to realize that in a doubly-linked list, we can switch the 'next' and 'prev' pointers for each node, and then set the 'head' of the list to be what was previously the last node (which is now the first because we reversed the links). 

```cpp
// Function to reverse a doubly linked list
struct Node* reverse(struct Node* node) {
    struct Node* temp = NULL;  
    struct Node* current = node;

    while (current !=  NULL) {
       temp = current->prev;
       current->prev = current->next;
       current->next = temp;              
       current = current->prev;
    }     

    // Before changing head, check for the cases like an empty list or list with only one node
    if(temp != NULL )
        node = temp->prev;

    return node;
} 
```

You should note that we did not allocate any additional memory so we won't have any memory leaks. To clear memory when you're finished with the list, you'll want to delete each node individually.

```cpp
void deletelist(struct Node** node) {
    struct Node* current = *node;
    struct Node* next;

    while (current != NULL) {
        next = current->next;
        free(current);
        current = next;
    }

    *node = NULL;
}
```

So, this program introduces a way to reverse a doubly-linked list as well as properly manages memory.

You would call these functions in your main application like this:

```cpp
int main() {
    Node* head = newNode(1);
    head->next = newNode(2);
    head->next->prev = head;
    head->next->next = newNode(3);
    head->next->next->prev = head->next;
    // And so on...

    head = reverse(head);
    // Process reversed list...

    deletelist(&head);

    return 0;
}
```

Always remember to free memory you have allocated when you're finished using it to prevent memory leaks.

Here is an example of how to create this script using Perl. Note that it requires the use of the File::Path and File::Basename modules to properly handle paths and the use of POSIX to format dates.

```
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use File::Basename;
use File::Path 'make_path';
use POSIX;

# Define paths
my $src = "/home/user1/sample.txt";
my $dest = "/home/user2/sample.txt";
my $log_file = "/home/user/sample_link.log";

# Open log file
open(my $log, ">>", $log_file) or die "Could not open $log_file: $!\n";

# Check and log starting time
my $start_time = localtime;
print $log "[$start_time] - Script started.\n";

# Check if source file exists
if (!-e $src) {
    my $error_time = localtime;
    print $log "[$error_time] - Source file $src does not exist.\n";
    close $log;
    exit;
}

# Check if we have read permissions
if (!-r $src) {
    my $error_time = localtime;
    print $log "[$error_time] - Do not have read permissions on $src.\n";
    close $log;
    exit;
}

# Check if destination exists
my($filename, $dirname) = fileparse($dest);
if (!-e $dirname) {
    make_path($dirname) or die "Failed to create path: $dirname";
}

# Check if we have write permissions
if (!-w $dest) {
    my $error_time = localtime;
    print $log "[$error_time] - Do not have write permissions on $dest.\n";
    close $log;
    exit;
}

# Attempt to create hard link
if (!link $src, $dest) {
    my $error_time = localtime;
    print $log "[$error_time] - Failed to create link from $src to $dest: $!.\n";
} else {
    my $success_time = localtime;
    print $log "[$success_time] - Successfully created link from $src to $dest.\n";
}

close $log;
```

This script will create a log file in the specified location, check if the source file exists and has read permissions, check if the destination exists and has write permissions, and attempt to create a hard link. All of these actions, along with their success or failure, will be logged in the log file. If an error occurs, the script will exit and the error will be logged.

