Here is a simple Java-based algorithm that can be used to invert the order of elements within an integer array:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arrayToInvert = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        invertArray(arrayToInvert);
        for(int i = 0; i < arrayToInvert.length; i++) {
            System.out.println(arrayToInvert[i]);
        }
    }

    public static void invertArray(int[] array) {
        int start = 0;
        int end = array.length - 1;
        while (start < end) {
            // Swap elements
            int temp = array[start];
            array[start] = array[end];
            array[end] = temp;

            // Move start and end inward
            start++;
            end--;
        }
    }
}
```

This algorithm works by iterating over the array from both ends. On each loop iteration, it swaps the elements at the start and end indices, then it moves the start and end pointers inward. The loop continues until the start pointer is no longer less than the end pointer, which indicates that we've reached the middle of the array and the inversion is complete.

