function processArray(inputArray) {
  if (!Array.isArray(inputArray)) {
    throw new TypeError('Expected an array');
  }

  let longestString = "";
  let longestIndex = -1;
  let outputArray = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < inputArray.length; i++) {
    const element = inputArray[i];
    if (typeof element !== 'string') continue; // handle null or undefined entries

    // reverse the string and add to the output array
    outputArray.push(element.split('').reverse().join(''));

    // update longest string and its index if necessary
    if (element.length > longestString.length) {
      longestString = element;
      longestIndex = i;
    }
  }

  // sort the array in alphabetical order
  outputArray.sort();

  return {
    longestStringLength: longestString.length,
    longestStringIndex: longestIndex,
    processedArray: outputArray
  };
}

console.log(processArray(["Apple","Banana","Mango", null]));
```

In the code above, the function first checks if the input is an array and then iterates over the array. When it encounters a string, it reverses the string and adds it to a new array. It also updates the longest string and its index if the current string is longer. Null or undefined entries are ignored. After that, it sorts the new array in alphabetical order. Finally, it returns an object containing the length and index of the longest string and the processed (reversed and sorted) array.

Time Complexity: The time complexity of the solution is O(n log n) where n is the number of elements in the array. This is because we are using a built-in sort function which on average takes O(n log n) time. 

Space Complexity: The space complexity is O(n) because we are creating a new array of the same size as the input array.

