Here is a simple Java program which calculates the multiplication matrix of prime numbers from 2 to 31. The prime numbers are validated by a method and they are multiplied with each other to generate a multiplication matrix. This program has proper exception handling. The code is properly documented for clear understanding.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Main Driver Class
 */
public class PrimeMultiplication {
    // Prime numbers to be considered
    private static final int MAXIMUM_PRIME = 31;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 2; i <= MAXIMUM_PRIME; i++) {
                if (isPrime(i)) {
                    primes.add(i);
                }
            }

            printMatrix(primes);

        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("An error occurred: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    /**
     * Function to check if number is prime
     *
     * @param num number to consider
     * @return true if number is prime, false otherwise
     */
    public static boolean isPrime(int num) {
        if (num <= 1) return false;
        if (num <= 3) return true;
        if (num % 2 == 0 || num % 3 == 0) return false;
        for (int i = 5; i * i <= num; i = i + 6) {
            if (num % i == 0 || num % (i + 2) == 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    /**
     * Function to print prime multiplication matrix
     *
     * @param primes list of prime numbers
     */
    public static void printMatrix(List<Integer> primes) {
        System.out.print("\t");
        for (int prime : primes) {
            System.out.format("%4d\t", prime);
        }
        System.out.println();

        for (int prime1 : primes) {
            System.out.format("%4d\t", prime1);
            for (int prime2 : primes) {
                System.out.format("%4d\t", prime1 * prime2);
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

This program starts by checking every number until 31 (inclusive) if it is prime. The method `isPrime` validates this by dividing the number from 2 to the square root of the number.

The valid primes are added to a list which is later used to print the multiplication matrix.

The `printMatrix` method prints the multiplication matrix. Firstly, it prints all the prime numbers as column headers. Then it iterates over the primes again, printing each prime as row header and its multiples forming the matrix values.

Note that running this program might produce a large display output due to the multiplication of numbers. You may need to adjust your console settings accordingly.

