In Java, a simple way of creating a thread-safe singleton class is by using the "synchronized" keyword. 

Here is the Singleton class design:

```java
public class Singleton {

    private static Singleton singletonInstance;

    private Singleton() {
    }

    public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
        if (singletonInstance == null) {
            singletonInstance = new Singleton();
        }
        return singletonInstance;
    }
}
```

The keyword synchronized in the getInstance() method makes sure that only one thread can access the method at a time on any instance. Therefore, this prevents two threads from simultaneously entering the "if" condition and creating two singleton instances.

A common mistake in creating a singleton class in a multithreaded environment is not using the synchronized keyword. 

Here is the erroneous code:

```java
public class Singleton {

    private static Singleton singletonInstance;

    private Singleton() {
    }

    // This method is not thread safe
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (singletonInstance == null) {
            singletonInstance = new Singleton();
        }
        return singletonInstance;
    }
}
```

This code is erroneous in a multithreaded environment because if two threads access the getInstance() method at the same time when singletonInstance is null, both of them might end up creating a new Singleton object. This violates the singleton design principle as multiple instances of the Singleton class are created.

The code can be corrected by adding the synchronized keyword to the getInstance() method as shown in the first code snippet provided.

However, making a method synchronized can cause performance issues because it locks the whole method while an instance is being created. So, for better performance with thread safety, you can use the "double-checked locking" idiom:

```java
public class Singleton {
    private static volatile Singleton singletonInstance;

    private Singleton() {}

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (singletonInstance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (singletonInstance == null) {
                    singletonInstance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return singletonInstance;
    }
}
```
"Double-checked locking" checks if an instance is already created, if not, then it synchronizes. This way, the synchronized block is executed only once when the instance is null and provides a significant performance benefit. The 'volatile' keyword ensures that multiple threads handle the uniqueSingletonInstance variable correctly when it is initialized to the Singleton instance.

