This can be solved with the help of "ephem" library in Python which provides astronomers about lots of astronomical calculations. It is used to calculate the phase of the moon for any specified date in the Gregorian calendar.

Before using the "ephem" library, make sure you have installed it using pip command (pip install ephem). 

Here is a simple Python function to determine the phase of the moon:

```python
import ephem

def get_moon_phase(day):
    """
    Function to get moon phase.
    It returns closest full moon phase name : 
    New moon, First quarter, Full moon, Last quarter.
    """
    phases = [ "New moon", "First quarter", "Full moon", "Last quarter" ]

    p = ephem.previous_new_moon(day)
    n = ephem.next_new_moon(day)
    first_quarter = ephem.date(p + (n - p) * 0.5)
    last_quarter = ephem.date(n + (first_quarter - n) * 0.5)
    fullmoon = ephem.date(p + (first_quarter - p) * 0.5)

    if ephem.date(day) == ephem.date(p):
        date_phase = phases[0]
    elif ephem.date(day) == ephem.date(first_quarter):
        date_phase = phases[1]
    elif ephem.date(day) == ephem.date(fullmoon):
        date_phase = phases[2]
    elif ephem.date(day) == ephem.date(last_quarter):
        date_phase = phases[3]
    else:
        if ephem.date(day) < ephem.date(fullmoon):
            if ephem.date(day) < ephem.date(first_quarter):
                date_phase = phases[0]
            else:
                date_phase = phases[1]
        else:
            if ephem.date(day) < ephem.date(last_quarter):
                date_phase = phases[2]
            else:
                date_phase = phases[3]

    return date_phase


print(get_moon_phase('2022/03/16'))  # Sample date usage
```

You can change the passed date in `get_moon_phase` to get the phase of the moon for your required date.

