This React component will use react-three-fiber, a React renderer for three.js on the web and react-native that provides a declarative way to handle three.js scenes. Here's a basic implementation of a component to display a 3D model using GLTFLoader:

```jsx
import React, { Suspense } from 'react';
import { Canvas, useLoader } from '@react-three/fiber';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/examples/jsm/loaders/GLTFLoader';
import { DRACOLoader } from 'three/examples/jsm/loaders/DRACOLoader';

function Model({ url }) {
  const gltf = useLoader(GLTFLoader, url, dracoLoader => {
    const draco = new DRACOLoader()
    draco.setDecoderPath('/draco/')
    dracoLoader.setDRACOLoader(draco)
  })
  return <primitive object={gltf.scene} dispose={null} />
}

export default function App() {
  return (
    <Canvas style={{ background: 'lightblue' }}>
      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
        <Model url='/path/to/3d_model.gltf' />
      </Suspense>
    </Canvas>
  );
}
```
The "Model" component uses GLTFLoader to load the model from a given URL. The useLoader hook is provided by react-three-fiber to abstract away the loading process. An instance of DRACOLoader is also passed, which is used to decode compressed mesh data. 

Please note that the setup of draco decoding might vary based on the build system and we assume the draco binaries are located at '/draco/'.

The "Model" component is then included in the "App" component, which wraps everything in a Canvas component (also provided by the react-three-fiber library) that will handle creating a WebGL context, defining and running the render loop, and more.

The model is rendered within a Suspense fallback container, meaning while the model is being loaded, it will render 'Loading...' until model is ready to display. Please replace '/path/to/3d_model.gltf' with path to your actual 3d model.

