To solve this problem, we need to conduct a comparison of each string against every other string within the array. We should use a sorted version of the string to make this comparison more straightforward, because anagrams will have the same characters in the same quantities, just positioned in different orders. 

Here's the revised version of the function:

```Java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] lexicon = {"cat", "act", "tac", "dog", "god", "odg", "red", "der", "rde", "icse"};
        String[] anagrams = discernAnagramsWithinArray(lexicon);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(anagrams));
    }

    public static String[] discernAnagramsWithinArray(String[] lexicon) {
        Map<String, List<String>> anagramMap = new HashMap<>();
        for (String term : lexicon) {
            char[] charArray = term.toCharArray();
            Arrays.sort(charArray);
            String sorted = new String(charArray);

            if (!anagramMap.containsKey(sorted)) {
                anagramMap.put(sorted, new ArrayList<>());
            } 

            anagramMap.get(sorted).add(term);
        }

        List<String> extractedAnagrams = new ArrayList<>();
        for (List<String> anagramList : anagramMap.values()) {
            if (anagramList.size() > 1) {
                extractedAnagrams.addAll(anagramList);
            }
        }
        return extractedAnagrams.toArray(new String[0]);
    }
}
```
In this version:
- Instead of just checking whether the length of the word is more than 2, we stored sorted versions of words as keys in a HashMap, with the original words stored in Lists as values.
- After that we loop over the HashMap and only add entries (anagram groups) to the output list if the size of that grouping is more than 1 (i.e. if there is actually more than one anagram present).
- Finally, we return an array created from that List.

Please note that if you simply want clusters/groupings of words that are anagrams of each other, and not specifically a flattened list of words, then you should consider returning a List of Lists or a similar structure rather than a single array.

