Here is a simple Java program implementing Bubble Sort algorithm on a pre-defined array of 10 integers. 

```java
public class BubbleSort {
   void bubbleSort(int nums[]) {
      int n = nums.length;
      for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
         for (int j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++) {
            if (nums[j] > nums[j+1]) {
               // Swap nums[j+1] and nums[j]
               int temp = nums[j];
               nums[j] = nums[j+1];
               nums[j+1] = temp;
            }
         }
      }
   }

   // Method to test above
   public static void main(String args[]) {
      BubbleSort ob = new BubbleSort();
      int nums[] = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90, 88, 76, 44};
      ob.bubbleSort(nums);
      System.out.println("Sorted sequence");
      for(int i=0; i<nums.length; i++) {
         System.out.print(nums[i] + " ");
      }
   }
}
```

This program applies bubble sort by repeatedly swapping the adjacent elements if they are in wrong order. When it finishes, it prints out the sorted sequence of numbers. The pre-defined numeric matrix can be replaced with any other sequence of 10 integers.

