```java
public class Main {
    final static int iterations = 50000;

    public static void main(String[] args){
        Runtime runtime = Runtime.getRuntime();
        long startMemoryUsage, endMemoryUsage;

        //String concatenation with +" operator
        runtime.gc();
        startMemoryUsage = runtime.totalMemory() - runtime.freeMemory();
        String str = new String();
        for(int i = 0; i < iterations; i++){
            str += i;
        }
        endMemoryUsage = runtime.totalMemory() - runtime.freeMemory();
        System.out.println("String concatenation memory usage: " + (endMemoryUsage - startMemoryUsage));

        //String concatenation with StringBuilder
        runtime.gc();
        startMemoryUsage = runtime.totalMemory() - runtime.freeMemory();
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for(int i = 0; i < iterations; i++){
            sb.append(i);
        }
        endMemoryUsage = runtime.totalMemory() - runtime.freeMemory();
        System.out.println("StringBuilder concatenation memory usage: " + (endMemoryUsage - startMemoryUsage));
    }
}
```

This Java code measures the memory used by the JVM process during string concatenation by the "+" operator and by `StringBuilder` respectively. The `runtime.gc()` is to force the garbage collector to run, so that it clears the memory and gives us a more accurate memory reading.

When you run the program, you might see that despite both parts of code doing seemingly similar operations, the memory usage is drastically different. With a large number of iterations, simple string concatenation with the "+" operator will use significantly more memory than StringBuilder.

The reason behind this is that strings in Java are immutable. Every time you concatenate a string with "+", it does not actually append to the existing text. Instead, it creates a new string and copies the content of the old string and the additional string into it. As a result, each concatenation creates a new string, resulting in a lot of memory being used, especially if the operation is repeated numerous times (as in a loop).

On the other hand, `StringBuilder` saves memory by preserving the original string buffer and providing room for additional strings. When you call `append()`, it simply extends the original buffer and adds the new string into it without creating a new string object. Thus resulting in less memory used.

Therefore, if you're dealing with a lot of strings, especially in loops and large-scale operations, using `StringBuilder` (or `StringBuffer` for thread-safe operations) is more efficient and saves a lot of memory.


Please see the code in action with higher iteration counts. With lower counts, memory usage difference might be negligible due to the optimizations already being conducted by the JVM in string concatenations via the "+" operator.

