Matrix addition is a straightforward task in which corresponding elements of two matrices are summed up to form a new matrix. That is, if `A` and `B` are our matrices, matrix `C=A+B` will be formed with `C[i][j] = A[i][j] + B[i][j]`.

Code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main(){
   int r, c;

   cout << "Enter number of rows (between 1 and 100): ";
   cin >> r;
   cout << "Enter number of columns (between 1 and 100): ";
   cin >> c;

   // Declare the matrices
   int A[100][100], B[100][100], sum[100][100];

   // Input elements of first matrix
   cout << endl << "Enter elements of 1st matrix: " << endl;
   for(int i=0; i<r; ++i)
      for(int j=0; j<c; ++j)
         cin >> A[i][j];

   // Input elements of second matrix
   cout << endl << "Enter elements of 2nd matrix: " << endl;
   for(int i=0; i<r; ++i)
      for(int j=0; j<c; ++j)
         cin >> B[i][j];

   // Add corresponding elements of two arrays
   for(int i=0; i<r; ++i)
      for(int j=0; j<c; ++j)
         sum[i][j] = A[i][j] + B[i][j];

   // Displaying the resultant sum matrix.
   cout << endl << "Sum of two matrix is: " << endl;
   for(int i=0; i<r; ++i)
      for(int j=0;j<c;++j)
      {
         cout << sum[i][j] << "  ";
         if(j == c - 1)
            cout << endl;
      }
   return 0;
}
```

This code first takes as input the sizes of the matrices (number of rows and columns). Then it asks for input for the values of two matrices A and B. The summation of these matrices is stored in the 'sum' matrix. The final loop at the end is responsible for printing the resultant sum matrix. It is important to note that the two matrices should be of the same size to be able to perform the addition.

This assumes the maximum possible size of the array is 100x100. The size of the array could be dynamically allocated based on user input for a more advanced version.

