This code checks for each character in the cell. If it's an opening bracket, add it to open_brackets. If it's a closing bracket, verify if the last opening bracket corresponds to this type, and if yes, remove it from the list. If at any point, it's a closing bracket but there's no corresponding opening one in the list, return "No". Likewise, if there's any leftover in open_brackets after all characters are checked, return "No". Return 'Yes' only when all opening and closing brackets are paired up nicely.

A Promise in JavaScript is an object that may produce a single value sometime in the future. Either a resolved value, or a reason that it’s not resolved (a network error for instance). It is a proxy for a value not necessarily known when the promise is created. It allows you to associate handlers with an asynchronous action's eventual success value or failure reason. This lets asynchronous methods return values like synchronous methods: instead of immediately returning the final value, the asynchronous method returns a promise to supply the value at some point in the future.

Promise has three states:
1. Pending: initial state, neither fulfilled nor rejected.
2. Fulfilled: meaning the operation was completed successfully.
3. Rejected: meaning an operation failed.

Promises are particularly useful in managing asynchronous operations, where you have tasks that depend on the completion of each other. This is commonly seen in web development where you are interacting with an API and do not know immediately when the data will be ready.

Here is an example of a promise and how it can handle asynchronous tasks:

```javascript
let promise = new Promise(function(resolve, reject) {
  // some asynchronous operation here
  setTimeout(() => {
    if (/* everything turned out fine */) {
      resolve("Data loaded!");
    } else {
      reject(Error("Unable to load data!"));
    }
  }, 1000);
});

promise.then((result) => {
  console.log(result); // "Data loaded!"
}, (error) => {
  console.log(error); // Error: "Unable to load data!"
});
```

In this example, we wrap a setTimeout function inside a promise. This represents an asynchronous operation that may take some time to complete. When the operation is successfully completed, we call the resolve function with a message "Data Loaded!". If there was an error, we reject the promise with an error.

In an application with strict latency requirements, you may use promises to handle network requests or CPU-intensive tasks in the background while the main thread continues executing other tasks, thus keeping the UI responsive. It also provides a clean, chaining way to handle callback hell and provide sequential execution. With Promises, error handling becomes much easier as we can handle all errors from a central location using the catch() method.

