Java Reflection API is an advanced feature and not typically used in most programming scenarios. It is essentially a powerful tool for obtaining information about classes, interfaces, fields, and methods at runtime.

Below are four less commonly used features of Reflection API.

1. Discovering Method Information: It allows you to get the list of methods of a class including public, private, protected, and default methods.

2. Discovering Constructor Information: It is possible to get the list of constructors of a class at runtime.

3. Dynamic Instantiation and Method Invocation: Reflection allows us to create an instance of a class at runtime, and also call methods of a class dynamically at runtime.

4. Changing Field Values: We can get and set the values of the fields of a class at runtime.

Here's a Java program illustrating these features of Reflection.

```java
import java.lang.reflect.*;

public class ReflectionTest {

    // Class to demonstrate Reflection functionality
    public static class Demo {
        private String privateString = null;

        public Demo(String privateString) {
            this.privateString = privateString;
        }

        public void display() {
            System.out.println("Display: " + privateString);
        }

        private void displayPrivate() {
            System.out.println("Display Private: " + privateString);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception{
        // Get Class using reflection
        Class cls = Class.forName("Demo");
        // Get Constructor Information
        System.out.println("Constructors in " + cls.getName());
        Constructor[] constructors = cls.getConstructors(); // get constructors
        for (Constructor constructor : constructors) {
            System.out.println(constructor.toString());
        }
        // Get Method Information
        System.out.println("Methods in " + cls.getName());
        Method[] methods = cls.getMethods(); // get methods
        for (Method method : methods) {
            System.out.println(method.toString());
        }
        // Dynamic Instantiation and Method Invocation
        Demo obj = (Demo) cls.getConstructor(String.class).newInstance("Reflection Test");
        obj.display(); // public method invocation
        Method methodcall = cls.getDeclaredMethod("displayPrivate");
        methodcall.setAccessible(true); // turn off access check for private method
        methodcall.invoke(obj); // private method invocation
        // Changing Field Values
        Field field = cls.getDeclaredField("privateString");
        field.setAccessible(true); // turn off access check for private field
        field.set(obj, "Reflection Test Changed!");
        methodcall.invoke(obj); // private method invocation to verify field value change
    }
}
```

Please remember that this is an advanced feature in Java and it can potentially violate the design principle of encapsulation in OOP. So always use the Reflection API very carefully.

In Javascript, there is no concept of multithreading as it's a single-threaded language that runs in web browsers and powered by event loop and callbacks, promises for async operations.

However, to simulate multithreading, we can use Web Workers, which run scripts in background threads. Here is some code for a binary search. Note that this does not split into subtasks if the array size exceeds a threshold, and therefore wouldn't show much of a speed increase with Web Workers due to the relative simplicity of the binary search algorithm:

```javascript
// Binary Search Function
const binarySearch = (arr, x, start, end) => {

    // Base Condition
    if (start > end) return false;

    // Find the middle index
    let mid = Math.floor((start + end) / 2);

    // Compare mid with given element x
    if (arr[mid] === x) return true;

    // If element at mid is greater than x, search in the left half of mid
    if (arr[mid] > x) return binarySearch(arr, x, start, mid - 1);
    else return binarySearch(arr, x, mid + 1, end); // If element at mid is smaller than x, search in the right half of mid
};

// Test Code
let arr = [];
for(let i = 1; i <= 1000000; i++) {
    arr.push(i);
}
let x = Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000000) + 1;

let start = new Date().getTime();
let res = binarySearch(arr, x, 0, arr.length - 1);
let end = new Date().getTime();
console.log("Result: " + res);
console.log("Time: " + (end - start) + "ms");
```

Distributing tasks to multiple threads can reduce the total execution time for certain tasks, but also involves overhead for communication between threads. In the case of too small workloads or if the problem isn’t suited for parallelization (like binary search), using multithreading can actually result in slower total execution time.

Moreover, multi-threading can be hard to implement correctly and create race conditions if not properly handled.

You should therefore only use multithreading in scenarios which would benefit from parallel execution and where you can properly manage the coordination between threads.

