The given task is incorrect because in Java, an interface cannot extend a class. Therefore, an interface B and C cannot extend class A. Therefore, to solve Diamond Dilemma in Java, it can only be solved using interfaces and classes due to the nature of Java's single inheritance model.

Therefore, the correct formulation could be as follows:

```java
interface A {
    default void display() {
        System.out.println("Interface A");
    }
}

interface B extends A {
    default void display() {
        System.out.println("Interface B");
    }
}

interface C extends A {
    default void display() {
        System.out.println("Interface C");
    }
}

class D implements B, C {
    public void display() {
        B.super.display(); // calling display() of interface B
        C.super.display(); // calling display() of interface C
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        D obj = new D();
        obj.display();
    }
}
```

In the code above, class D implements the interfaces B and C. Consequently, there's an ambiguity as to which display method to call: B.display() or C.display(). This is known as Diamond Problem. Java 8 provides a solution by allowing the use of super keyword to call the method of a specific interface like B.super.display() and C.super.display().

