In Java, there are two types of exceptions: checked and unchecked. Checked exceptions are checked at compile-time and must be explicitly caught or declared to be thrown. These types of exceptions typically represent unavoidable, predictable, and relatively frequent conditions such as file not found, invalid user inputs etc. Unchecked exceptions, on the other hand, are not checked at compile-time but at runtime, so they are also called runtime exceptions. These exceptions represent defects in the program, often invalid arguments passed to a non-private method.

In terms of the JDBC Connection object, we're particularly interested in `java.sql.SQLException` which is a checked exception and `java.lang.NullPointerException` which is an unchecked exception.

Here's an example code segment illustrating the use of these exceptions:

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.sql.Statement;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Connection conn = null;
        Statement stmt = null;
        ResultSet rs = null;
        try {
            // Step 1: Load JDBC driver
            Class.forName("com.mysql.jdbc.Driver");
            // Step 2: Establish the connection to the database
            conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost/testdb","user", "password");
            // Step 3: Create a statement object
            stmt = conn.createStatement();
            // Step 4: Execute SQL query
            rs = stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM employees");

            while(rs.next()) {
                System.out.println(rs.getString("name"));
            }
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            e.printStackTrace(); // Handle situation where JDBC Driver is not found
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace(); // Handle SQL related exceptions
        } finally {
            try {
                if(rs != null) rs.close();
                if(stmt != null) stmt.close();
                if(conn != null) conn.close();
            } catch (SQLException e) {
                e.printStackTrace(); // Handle exceptions during closing connections
            }
        }

        // Here is where unchecked exception can cause a problem
        stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM departments"); // Since stmt object may be closed, it can throw NullPointerException
    }
}
```

In the above example, we've handled both checked (ClassNotFoundException, SQLException) and unchecked (NullPointerException) exceptions. Note that trying to execute an SQL query after closing the statement (`stmt`) leads to NullPointerException which is an example of a common misapplication.

Since the requirements of the problem are very broad without specifying a specific problem that needs to be solved using multi-threading and AOP principles, here is an example of how we can solve a simple problem using these principles. The used programming language will be Java.

Please note that AOP (Aspect Oriented Programming) is a programming paradigm that aims to increase modularity by allowing the separation of cross-cutting concerns. It includes programming methods and tools that support the modulation of concerns at the level of the source code.

The problem we will solve will just demonstrate how a simple logging aspect can be implemented using AOP that will log function calls in a multi-threaded program. Our simple multi-threaded program will be just simply printing out numbers.

1. Let's start by creating the aspect:

```java
@Aspect
public class LoggingAspect {

    @Before("execution(* com.example..*(..))")
    public void logBefore(JoinPoint joinPoint) {
        System.out.println("Executing: " + joinPoint.getSignature().getName());
    }
}
```
This aspect class has a function `logBefore()` which will be called before calling any function in the `com.example` package. It simply logs the name of the function being called.

2. Next, we will create our simple multi-threaded program:

```java
public class MyRunnable implements Runnable {

    private int id;

    public MyRunnable(int id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            printNumber(i);
        }
    }

    public void printNumber(int number) {
        System.out.println("Thread " + id + " - Number: " + number);
    }
}
```
Here, we just have a `MyRunnable` class which implements `Runnable`. This means instances of this class can be used to create threads. The `run()` function here is just printing out numbers from 0-9 using the `printNumber()` function.

3. Finally, we create our threads and start them:

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread thread1 = new Thread(new MyRunnable(1));
        Thread thread2 = new Thread(new MyRunnable(2));

        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();
    }
}
```
We create and start two threads here, both of them will be logging their function calls due to AOP. 

There you go! We have used Aspect-Oriented Programming principles to add logging functionality to our multi-threaded program. 

One of the main challenges encountered here was figuring out how to correctly define point-cuts that target the right methods. The principles of AOP came in helpful here because we can modularize the logging function and easily apply it to any part of our code, even a multi-threaded one.

Please note that this is a pretty basic example and real-life uses of AOP and multi-threading would be much more complex.

