Here is a C# program that finds the median value of a jagged array of nullable integers:

```c#
using System;
using System.Linq;
using System.Collections.Generic;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        int?[][] nums = new int?[][] {
            new int?[] {10, 2, 36},
            null,
            new int?[] {14, 25}
        };

        try
        {
            List<int> allNums = new List<int>();

            foreach (int?[] arr in nums)
            {
                if (arr != null)
                    allNums.AddRange(arr.Where(n => n.HasValue).Select(n => n.Value));
            }

            if (allNums.Count == 0)
                throw new Exception("Array is empty");

            allNums.Sort();

            var median = allNums.Count % 2 == 0
                ? (allNums[allNums.Count / 2] + allNums[(allNums.Count / 2) - 1]) / 2.0
                : allNums[allNums.Count / 2];

            Console.WriteLine($"Median: {median}");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
        }
    }
}
```

This program first collects all the non-null values from the jagged array into a single list of integers, throwing an exception if no values are found. The list is then sorted, and the median is computed by finding the middle value (or the average of the two middle values if the count is even).

