The "this" keyword in Java is a reference variable that refers to the current object. Below are few main uses of "this" keyword:

1. It can be used to refer to the current class instance variable.
2. It can be used to invoke the current class method.
3. It can be used to invoke the current class constructor.

Given below is an example to demonstrate a scenario where misuse of "this" could lead to unexpected behaviors or errors:

class Student{
    int id;
    String name;

    Student(int id, String name){
        id = id; //should be this.id = id;
        name = name; //should be this.name = name;
    }

    void display(){
        System.out.println(id+" "+name);
    }
}

In this case, you'll get output as `0 null` instead of actual values because we are assigning the local variable to itself, not updating the instance variable. 

Correct version of this code would be:

