    this.name = name;
}

Overloading in Java is a feature that allows a class to include two or more methods with the same name but different parameters. It doesn't necessarily affect the usage of "this", because "this" keyword is still referring to the current object no matter how many methods you have with the same name. Only the parameters of the method and the method being called changes.

Overriding in Java is a feature used when a subclass wants to modify an existing method in the parent class. Here, the usage of "this" will refer to the methods and variables of the subclass if they are called from the subclass’s object. And superclass methods or variables would be referred to if they are called from the superclass's object.

For example, if you have a method in both the superclass and subclass, calling "this.method()" in subclass would call the subclass method. But if you want to call the superclass's method in subclass, you would need to use super keyword, i.e., "super.method()".

