Here is a simple solution using recursion and Java's BigInteger class with memoization (storing previously calculated results to avoid recomputing). Error handling is also added for invalid inputs.

```java
import java.math.BigInteger;
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class Fibonacci {
    private static Map<Integer, BigInteger> memo = new HashMap<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in)) {
            System.out.print("Enter the desired Fibonacci term: ");
            int n = scanner.nextInt();

            if (n < 1) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input! The number must be positive.");
            } else {
                BigInteger fibonacci = getFibonacci(n);
                System.out.println("The " + n + "th term of the Fibonacci series is: " + fibonacci);
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input! Please input a valid number.");
        }
    }

    private static BigInteger getFibonacci(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return BigInteger.ZERO;
        }
        else if (n == 1) {
            return BigInteger.ONE;
        }
        // exploit previously calculated terms
        if (memo.containsKey(n)) {
            return memo.get(n);
        }

        BigInteger v = getFibonacci(n - 2).add(getFibonacci(n - 1));
        memo.put(n, v);
        return v;
    }
}
```
This code will read the number of the desired Fibonacci term, process it, and output the result. It will also handle common errors like non-numeric or out-of-range input.

The complexity of this approach is O(n), as we only calculate each term once and store it in the memo map to be used in future calculations if needed. This makes it significantly more efficient than a straightforward recursive solution, which has a complexity of O(2^n) due to recalculations of the same terms. It can handle large inputs without problems related to stack overflow or computational speed, thanks to the BigInteger class and memoization.

In TypeScript, to define a stack data structure we could create an interface named `Stack`. Here is a simplistic example:

```typescript
interface IStack<T> {
    // Push item onto the stack
    push(item: T): void;

    // Pop item from the stack
    pop(): T;

    // View item on top of the stack
    peek(): T;

    // Check if stack is empty
    isEmpty(): boolean;

    // Get size of the stack
    size(): number;
}
```

In this example, I have used a generic `<T>` in order to make the stack re-usable with any type.

Here's a brief explanation of the methods:

1. `push(item: T)`: It adds new items to the top of the stack.

2. `pop()`: It removes the top most element of the stack.

3. `peek()`: It displays the top most element of the stack.

4. `isEmpty()`: It checks if the stack is empty or not.

5. `size()`: It returns the size of the stack.

An instance of the stack could then be implemented in a class that extends this interface, where the behavior of these methods would be defined.

