There are several ways to optimize a C++ program that calculates all prime numbers between 0 and 100. Here, we will use the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm, which is efficient for finding all primes up to a given limit.

```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

void SieveOfEratosthenes(int n) {
    std::vector<bool> prime(n+1, true);  // Initialize all numbers as prime

    prime[0] = prime[1] = false; // 0 and 1 are not prime

    for (int p = 2; p*p <= n; p++) {
      // If prime[p] is not changed, then it is a prime
      if (prime[p] == true) {
        // Update all multiples of p
        for (int i = p * p; i <= n; i += p)
          prime[i] = false;
      }
    }

    // Print all prime numbers
    for (int p = 2; p <= n; p++)
      if (prime[p])
        std::cout << p << " ";
}

int main() {
    int n = 100;
    std::cout << "The prime numbers between 0 and " << n << " are: ";
    SieveOfEratosthenes(n);
    return 0;
}
```
This is an efficient program to print all prime numbers between 0 and 100. The time complexity of this approach is O(n log log n), which is much better than the standard approach of O(n^2). It utilizes a property of prime numbers, which is that a number is prime if it is not divisible by any prime number less than or equal to its square root.

