In C++, a variable and a constant have different characteristics and functionalities.

Variable:
A variable represents a memory location where a programmer can store a value. The value of a variable can be changed throughout the program. There are different types of variables, such as local variable, global variable, and static variable. The usage of a variable is mostly where the value needs to be changed during the execution of the program. Examples of potential errors due to misuse of variables include: not initializing a variable before using it, naming conflicts in large code bases, memory leaks because of improper use of dynamic variables, and trying to modify the value of a const variable.

Constant:
In contrast to variables, constants are immutable. Once a value is assigned to a constant, it cannot be changed throughout the program. This can be useful for values that are reused instead of hardcoding them multiple times. Examples of constants are pi (3.1415...) or the speed of light. They also reassure the programmer the value won't be changed unknowingly due to complicated code scopes. A potential pitfall or misuse of constants is trying to modify their value after assignment, which will result in a compile-time error.

Here is a code snippet in C++ that illustrates the difference:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#define PI 3.14  // this is a constant

int main() {

    int radius = 5; // this is a variable
    float area_of_circle; // this is also a variable

    // This line of code will work fine as we are allowed to change the value of a variable.
    radius = 10; 

    area_of_circle = PI*radius*radius;

    // This line of code would cause a compile-time error as PI is a constant and its value can't be changed.
    // PI = 3.1415; 

    std::cout << "Area of circle is: " << area_of_circle << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

As good programming practice, it is often recommended to make use of constants to give semantic meaning to values and use variables judiciously to manage the state of the application.

