In order to solve the following problem in JavaScript, we could utilize brute force and compare each individual character of each string within the provided array.

However, a more efficient approach involves using the concept of Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) in Dynamic Programming.

The steps to find the smallest common super-sequence (SCS) are as follows:

1. Calculate the length of the longest common subsequence (LCS).
2. Subtract the length of the LCS from the length of each individual string.
3. Sum the lengths derived from point 2, add the length of the LCS, and you have the length of the SCS.

Here is the JavaScript code for the problem:

```javascript
function lcs(a, b){
    var m = a.length;
    var n = b.length;
    var L = [];

    // Initialize L array with zeroes
    for(var i=0 ; i<=m ; i++) {
        var tmp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
        L.push(tmp);
    }

    // Compute longest common subsequence of a and b
    for(var i=1 ; i<=m ; i++) {
        for(var j=1 ; j<=n ; j++){
            if(a[i-1] == b[j-1]){
                L[i][j] = L[i-1][j-1] + 1;
            }
            else {
                L[i][j] = Math.max(L[i-1][j], L[i][j-1]);
            }
        }
    }

    return L[m][n];
}

function smallestCommonSupersequence(array){
    var scs = array[0];
    for(var i=1; i<array.length; i++){
        var lcsLength = lcs(scs, array[i]);
        scs = scs.length + array[i].length - lcsLength;
    }
    return scs;
}

console.log(smallestCommonSupersequence(["apple", "ape", "apricot"]));
// Expected output for case-sensitive comparison: 15
```

This function calculates the length of the smallest common super-sequence and not the string itself. We are checking for the existence of characters, not their order. Thus, APE and AEP are equal in the eyes of this function.

