Here is an implementation of a Go program which uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm to find the first 100 prime numbers. This algorithm has a time complexity better than O(n²), which is O(n log log n). As a trade-off, it uses O(n) space. 

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
)

// Function to generate primes
func generatePrimes(n int) []int {
    primes := make([]bool, n+1)
    for i := 2; i <= n; i++ {
        primes[i] = true
    }

    p := 2
    for p*p <= n {
        if primes[p] {
            for i := p * p; i <= n; i += p {
                primes[i] = false
            }
        }
        p += 1
    }

    primeNums := make([]int, 0)
    for p := 2; p <= n; p++ {
        if primes[p] {
            primeNums = append(primeNums, p)
        }
    }

    return primeNums
}

// Function to find the first n prime numbers > 2
func firstNPrimes(n int) []int {
    length, increment := 100, 100
    for {
        primes := generatePrimes(length)
        if len(primes) >= n {
            return primes[:n]
        }
        length += increment
    }
}

func main() {
    fmt.Println(firstNPrimes(100))
}
```

This program first choses an arbitrary length for the list of prime numbers. If this length is not enough, it increases the length and tries again, until it has found the required amount of prime numbers. The generatePrimes function implements the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm, which generates all prime numbers up to a given limit. It creates a list of prime numbers by iteratively marking the multiples of each prime number starting from 2.

This problem is a slightly modified version of the standard problem of finding the maximum area of an island in a binary matrix. The major difference here is having obstacles in the grid.

It can be solved using Depth First Search (DFS) technique. Here's the Python code for it.

``` python
class Solution:
    def maxAreaOfIsland(self, grid):
        if not grid:
            return 0

        max_area = 0
        rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])

        def dfs(i, j):
            if i<0 or j<0 or i>=rows or j>=cols or grid[i][j] != 1:
                return 0
            # mark as visited
            grid[i][j] = 0
            return 1 + dfs(i-1, j) + dfs(i+1, j) + dfs(i, j-1) + dfs(i, j+1)

        for i in range(rows):
            for j in range(cols):
                if grid[i][j] == 1:
                    max_area = max(max_area, dfs(i, j))

        return max_area
```

In the solution above, we just do DFS on each land cell. Obstacle cells are treated like water cells because you can't traverse through them, hence we return 0 if we encounter a cell with 2 in our dfs. When we have the area of each island, we keep track of the maximum area encountered. In the end, we return `max_area` which will be the maximum area of an island considering the obstacles.

Note that, it destroys the original grid by marking visited places with '0'. If you do not want to destroy the original grid, you can use an additional visited[][] grid for storing the visited nodes.

以下是製作基數排序算法程式的Java實現。我們使用陣列與Queue來增強算法性能並具有异步性。該演算法會從最小的位數（在此例中是個位數）開始順序排序。

```java
import java.util.*;

public class RadixSort {

    // The main function to that sorts array[] using Radix Sort
    static void radixsort(int arr[])
    {
        // Find the maximum number to know number of digits
        int m = getMax(arr);

        for (int exp = 1; m/exp > 0; exp *= 10) //exp is 10^i where i is the current digit number
            countSort(arr, exp);
    }

    static void countSort(int arr[], int exp)
    {
        int output[] = new int[arr.length]; // output array
        int i;
        int count[] = new int[10]; // holding the frequency of digit numbers. This is stable sort so counting sort is used.

        Arrays.fill(count,0);

       // Count frequency 
        for (i = 0; i < arr.length; i++)
            count[ (arr[i]/exp)%10 ]++;

       // Change count[i] so that count[i] now contains
        // actual position of this digit in output[]
        for (i = 1; i < 10; i++)
            count[i] += count[i - 1];

        // Output from back to retain stable sort
        for (i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        {
            output[count[ (arr[i]/exp)%10 ] - 1] = arr[i];
            count[ (arr[i]/exp)%10 ]--;
        }

        // Copy back the output array to arr[] so that arr[] contains
        // sorted numbers according to current digit position
        for (i = 0; i < arr.length; i++)
            arr[i] = output[i];
    }

    // A utility function to get maximum value in arr[]
    static int getMax(int arr[])
    {
        int mx = arr[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++)
            if (arr[i] > mx)
                mx = arr[i];
        return mx;
    }

    // A utility function to print array of size n
    static void print(int arr[])
    {
        for (int i=0; i<arr.length; i++)
            System.out.print(arr[i]+" ");
        System.out.println();
    }

    // Driver Method
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        int arr[] = {170, 45, 75, 90, 802, 24, 2, 66};
        radixsort(arr);
        print(arr);
    }
}
```

