Here is a solution in Java:

First, define a class `QuadraticCumulativeFibonacci` :

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class QuadraticCumulativeFibonacci {
    private static final int FIBO_INITIAL_VALUE_ONE = 0;
    private static final int FIBO_INITIAL_VALUE_TWO = 1;
    private static final int SQUARE_FACTOR = 2;

    public long performOperation(int k, int n) {
        long sum = 0;
        int fiboNumOne = FIBO_INITIAL_VALUE_ONE;
        int fiboNumTwo = FIBO_INITIAL_VALUE_TWO;

        while (fiboNumOne <= n) {
            if (fiboNumOne >= k && fiboNumOne % SQUARE_FACTOR == 0) {
                sum += Math.pow(fiboNumOne, SQUARE_FACTOR);
            }

            int newFiboNum = fiboNumOne + fiboNumTwo;
            fiboNumOne = fiboNumTwo;
            fiboNumTwo = newFiboNum;
        }

        return sum;
    }
}
```
This class defines the `performOperation` method which evaluates quadratic expressions of even Fibonacci numbers within the range `[k, n]`. The computation of the Fibonacci series is done in an iterative fashion. For each Fibonacci number, we perform a modular operation to check if it's even. The time complexity of this operation is O(n), n is the limit of Fibonacci series.

Now, create a main class to test `QuadraticCumulativeFibonacci`:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        QuadraticCumulativeFibonacci obj = new QuadraticCumulativeFibonacci();
        System.out.println(obj.performOperation(1, 10));
    }
}
```
Please note that Fibonacci series grows exponentially, therefore be careful with the range you are passing into your function because it can lead to overflow problems or program termination if the number is too large.

