Here's a simple Perl script that accepts input and tries to determine whether it's treated as a boolean or an array. Please note that in Perl, a single value is not strictly a boolean. Perl doesn't have a dedicated boolean data type. It uses other types, like numbers or strings, in boolean context. In most cases, `0`, `"0"`, `" "` and `""` are false and everything else is true. 

Full script:
```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;

print "Input something: ";
my $input = <STDIN>;
chomp $input;

if ($input =~ m/^(true|false)$/) {
    print "The input can be treated as a boolean value (in context).\n";
} elsif ($input =~ m/^\[.*\]$/) {
    print "The input can be treated as an array (by syntax).\n";
} else {
    print "The input is neither a recognizable boolean nor an array.\n";
}
```
This script firstly asks for an input. Then it checks whether the input is in the form of `true` or `false`. If it is, the script will treat it as a boolean. If the input includes square brackets like an array (e.g., `[1,2,3]`), the script will treat it as an array.

But this is a very simple script, and there are many rules in Perl to determine the type context of a variable. For instance, an empty array (in list context) and 0 are both considered false!

