Here is a simple example of a JavaScript object that can dynamically add and execute methods based on conditional circumstances.

```javascript
var DynaObject = {
    conditions: [],

    addCondition: function(newCondition, newMethod) {
        // Add new condition and method
        this.conditions.push({condition: newCondition, method: newMethod});
    },

    executeMethods: function(input) {
        // Check for errors
        if(!this.conditions.length) {
            throw new Error("No conditions have been defined.");
        }

        // Loop through each condition
        for(var i = 0; i < this.conditions.length; i++) {
            var conditionObj = this.conditions[i];

            // Execute method if condition is met
            if(conditionObj.condition(input)) {
                conditionObj.method();
            }
        }
    }
};

// Add new condition and method at runtime
DynaObject.addCondition(function(input){return input > 5;}, function(){console.log("Input is greater than 5");});

// Manually throw error when trying to execute with no conditions
try {
    DynaObject.executeMethods([]);
} catch(err) {
    console.log("Caught error: " + err.message);
}

// Execute methods with an input of 6
DynaObject.executeMethods(6);
```

In this script, an object called `DynaObject` is created. It has two properties: An empty array called `conditions` and two methods called `addCondition` and `executeMethods`. The `addCondition` method is used to push a new condition and corresponding method into the `conditions` array. The `executeMethods` method is used to iterate over the `conditions` array and execute any method whose corresponding condition produces `true` when given an input. Note that methods are only executed if their conditions are met.

If `executeMethods` is called when no conditions have been added, it will throw an error. This can be caught and handled using a `try-catch` block. An example is provided where `executeMethods` is called without any conditions, resulting in an error being thrown and caught.

