Implementing a live ETL data processing pipeline using Apache Flink involves these steps:

1. Set up Apache Flink Environment.

    You need to download the latest Apache Flink binary from the official website and extract it in your local system. Apache Flink requires Java 8.x to run, ensure that you have it.

2. Create a Source

    Now, to create an E-commerce specific pipeline, you would need to gather data from different sources such as clickstream logs, product specific details, and user details. In Flink, you can define sources from where these data can be ingested.

3. Transform Data: 

    Once the data is ingested, you may need to perform transformations on that data. These transformations might be simple such as filtering specific data or complex like joining streams, windowing, etc.

4. Load Data: 

    Once the data is transformed according to your needs, you can store it into a storage hub or data warehouse. Apache Flink provides various sinks such as HDFS, Cassandra, Elasticsearch, etc. where you can store your data.

Below is a basic blueprint of what the ETL pipeline might look like in Flink:

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.datastream.DataStream;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;

public class ETLFlink {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        // Set up the execution environment
        final StreamExecutionEnvironment env = StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // Ingest data from various sources
        DataStream<String> clickStreamData = env.addSource(new ClickStreamSource());
        DataStream<String> productDetails = env.addSource(new ProductSource());
        DataStream<String> userDetails = env.addSource(new UserSource());

        // Perform transformations
        DataStream<String> transformedClickStreamData = clickStreamData.map(x -> transformClickStream(x));
        DataStream<String> transformedProductDetails = productDetails.map(x -> transformProductDetails(x));
        DataStream<String> transformedUserDetails = userDetails.map(x -> transformUserDetails(x));

        // Store data
        transformedClickStreamData.addSink(new DataWarehouseSink());
        transformedProductDetails.addSink(new DataWarehouseSink());
        transformedUserDetails.addSink(new DataWarehouseSink());

        // Execute program
        env.execute("Flink ETL");
    }

    // Implement your transformation and source/sink methods
}
```
Remember, you would need to implement the source and sink methods to handle data ingestion and egress, and transform methods for relevant data manipulation.

This example shows one string taken in and one string output. If you require multiple types of transformations including filtering, mapping, joining, etc. you can do so by changing the type of Data Stream and using the corresponding transformations.

Ensure you have exception handling mechanisms to handle any exceptions during data ingestion or transformation processes. Also, Apache Flink comes with exactly once processing semantics, which ensures no data is lost or duplicated during processing.

