This Java program utilizes the built-in `Calendar` and `YearMonth` classes of Java to generate a calendar based on the user's input.

```java
import java.util.Calendar;
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.time.YearMonth;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Enter Year: ");
        int year = sc.nextInt();

        //Iterating over all months in the given year
        for (int month = 1; month <= 12; month++) {
            YearMonth ym = YearMonth.of(year, month);
            int len = ym.lengthOfMonth();
            Calendar cal = Calendar.getInstance();
            cal.set(year, month - 1, 1);
            System.out.printf(" %s %d\n", ym.getMonth(), year);
            System.out.println(" Su Mo Tu We Th Fr Sa");

            //Printing empty spaces for the day of the week
            int dayOfWeek = cal.get(Calendar.DAY_OF_WEEK);
            for (int i = 1; i < dayOfWeek; i++)
                System.out.print("   ");

            //Printing the dates in the calendar
            for (int day = 1; day <= len; day++) {
                System.out.printf(" %2d", day);
                if ((day + dayOfWeek - 1) % 7 == 0)
                    System.out.println();
                else
                    System.out.print(" ");
            }
            System.out.println("\n");
        }
    }
}
```

This program prompts the user to enter the year and subsequently prints out a beautifully formatted calendar for that entire year.

Note: This program uses the `printf` function to format the output nicely, and it uses the `Calendar.DAY_OF_WEEK` field to determine the first day of each month.

Please, ensure your development environment supports Java 8 or later since the `YearMonth` was added in Java 8.

