Here is an implementation of a simple decision tree in JavaScript that can handle the questions you provided. Note that this is a very simple decision tree that does not implement all of the features you mentioned, like weighting attributes or handling real-time changes, due to the complexity of those features. 

```javascript
class Node {
    constructor(attribute, splitPoint, left = null, right = null) {
        this.attribute = attribute; // which attribute to split on
        this.splitPoint = splitPoint; // value to split the attribute at
        this.left = left; // items less than splitPoint
        this.right = right; // items greater than splitPoint
    }
}

class DecisionTree {
    constructor() {
        this.root = null;
    }

    buildTree(data) {
        if (!data || data.length === 0) {
            throw new Error("Invalid input");
        }

        // calculate weight for each attribute
        const weights = data[0].map(() => Math.random());

        // create nodes based on weighted attributes
        this.root = this.createNode(data, weights);
    }

    createNode(data, weights) {
        if (!data || data.length === 0) {
            return null;
        }

        // find attribute with highest weight and use as split point
        const maxWeightIndex = weights.indexOf(Math.max(...weights));
        const splitPoint = data[0][maxWeightIndex];

        // split data into two sets based on the value of the attribute at split point
        const leftData = data.filter(item => item[maxWeightIndex] < splitPoint);
        const rightData = data.filter(item => item[maxWeightIndex] >= splitPoint);

        // create child nodes recursively
        const left = this.createNode(leftData, weights);
        const right = this.createNode(rightData, weights);

        // create new node with chosen attribute, split point, and child nodes
        return new Node(maxWeightIndex, splitPoint, left, right);
    }
}

const data = [
    [3, 'crimson', 5, 'metal'],
    [4, 'emerald', 10, 'wood'],
    [3, 'scarlet', 8, 'plastic'],
    [6, 'turquoise', 12, null],
];

const tree = new DecisionTree();
tree.buildTree(data);
```

This script creates a Decision Tree based on a data-set. It creates nodes by finding the attribute with the highest weight (at random) and splitting the data based on that. It continues to do this recursively until no more splits can be made (when the data-set is empty). Note that invalid inputs or missing attributes are discarded when creating child nodes as they would not meet any splitting criteria.
It doesn't handle real-time changes; handling real-time changes would be a lot more complex and usually involves rebuilding or significantly updating the tree whenever the underlying data changes. It's also somewhat tricky/nonsensical to try to assign weight to different attributes in this context, but in this script, we did it randomly for demonstration purposes.

