Here's an example of a Java program that creates either a right-angled triangle or an equilateral triangle based on user input. Input validation is performed to ensure that the user's input is sensible:

```Java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main 
{
    public static void main(String[] args) 
    {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Enter 1 for right-angled triangle and 2 for equilateral triangle:");
        int choice = scanner.nextInt();

        while (choice != 1 && choice != 2) 
        {
            System.out.println("Invalid Input! Please enter either 1 or 2:");
            choice = scanner.nextInt();
        }

        System.out.println("Enter the size of the triangle:");
        int size = scanner.nextInt();

        while (size <= 0) 
        {
            System.out.println("Invalid Input! Size must be greater than 0. Enter again:");
            size = scanner.nextInt();
        }

        System.out.println("Enter 1 for right-side up triangle and 2 for upside-down triangle:");
        int direction = scanner.nextInt();

        while (direction != 1 && direction != 2) 
        {
            System.out.println("Invalid Input! Please enter either 1 or 2:");
            direction = scanner.nextInt();
        }

        triangle(choice, size, direction);
        scanner.close();
    }

    public static void triangle(int type, int size, int direction) 
    {
        if (type == 1) 
        {
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) 
            {
                if(direction == 1) 
                {
                    printRow(i+1, size);
                } 
                else 
                {
                    printRow(size-i, size);
                }
            }
        } 
        else 
        {
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) 
            {
              if(direction == 1) 
              {
                printEquilateralRow(i+1, size);
              }
              else 
              {
                printEquilateralRow(size-i, size);
              }
            }
        }
    }

    public static void printRow(int stars, int size) 
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) 
        {
            if (i < stars) 
            {
                System.out.print("*");
            } 
            else 
            {
                System.out.print(" ");
            }
        }

        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void printEquilateralRow(int stars, int size) 
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++)
        {
            System.out.print(i < size-stars ? " " : "*");
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < size-1; i++) 
        {
            System.out.print(i < stars-1 ? "*" : " ");
        }

        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

The program works with a loop until the user enters a valid option and size. It allows the user to print the triangle either right-side-up or upside-down. 

For the right-angle triangle, it uses printRow method to print each row of the triangle. For the equilateral triangle, it uses the printEquilateralRow method to print each row. It adds spaces before adding stars for every row according to the size and direction of the triangle. 

Be aware that an equilateral triangle pattern may not seem perfect due to the equal width and height of characters in a typical console, which makes the equilateral triangle look skewed.

