To merge two sorted arrays in-place without using extra space in C++, you can follow the steps mentioned in the algorithm below. Please note that it is a bit tough to ensure that the time complexity does not exceed O(n log n) in this case as we need to shift the elements, and worst-case time complexity is up to O(m*n).

Algorithm:

1. Traverse from the end of the first array, if the current element in the first array is greater than the current element in the second array then swap both the elements.
2. If the current element in the first array is smaller than the current element in the second array than decrement the second array index.
3. Repeat the step 1 and 2 until the second array index is greater than zero.
4. After merging both the array, sort the first array using sort function.

Here is an example in C++:

```c++
#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

// Function to merge
void merge(int arr1[], int arr2[], int m, int n) {
    // Iterate through all elements
    // of array2[] starting from the last element
    for (int i=n-1; i>=0; i--) {
        // Find the smallest element greater than arr2[i]
        // Move all elements one position ahead till the
        // smallest greater element is not found
        int j, last = arr1[m-1];
        for (j=m-2; j >= 0 && arr1[j] > arr2[i]; j--)
            arr1[j+1] = arr1[j];

        // If there was a greater element
        if (j != m-2 || last > arr2[i]) {
            arr1[j+1] = arr2[i];
            arr2[i] = last;
        }
    }
}

// Utility function to print an array
void printArray(int arr[], int size) {
    for (int i=0; i<size; i++)
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    cout << "\n";
}

// Driver program
int main(void) {
    int arr1[] = {1, 5, 9, 10, 15, 20};
    int arr2[] = {2, 3, 8, 13};
    int m = sizeof(arr1)/sizeof(arr1[0]);
    int n = sizeof(arr2)/sizeof(arr2[0]);
    merge(arr1, arr2, m, n);
    cout << "Modified array 1: ";
    printArray(arr1, m);
    cout << "Modified array 2: ";
    printArray(arr2, n);
    return 0;
}
```

This will print:
```
Modified array 1: 1 2 3 5 8 9 
Modified array 2: 10 13 15 20 
```

Here's a simple Vuex solution that creates a Vuex state to hold a list of progress trackers, mutations to manipulate the state, and actions to commit those mutations.

```JavaScript
import Vue from 'vue'
import Vuex from 'vuex'

Vue.use(Vuex)

export default new Vuex.Store({
  state: {
    progressTrackers: [],  // Empty initial list of progress trackers
  },

  mutations: {
    ADD_TRACKER(state, tracker) {
      // Add a new tracker to the list
      state.progressTrackers.push({
        id: tracker.id,       // Assume unique ID, replace with own logic if necessary
        name: tracker.name,
        current: tracker.current,
        total: tracker.total,
      })
    },

    UPDATE_TRACKER(state, tracker) {
      // Update the progress of a tracker
      let existingTracker = state.progressTrackers.find(t => t.id === tracker.id)
      if (existingTracker) {
        existingTracker.current = tracker.current
      }
    },

    REMOVE_TRACKER(state, trackerId) {
      // Remove a tracker from the list
      let index = state.progressTrackers.findIndex(t => t.id === trackerId)
      if (index !== -1) {
        state.progressTrackers.splice(index, 1)
      }
    },
  },

  actions: {
    addTracker({ commit }, tracker) {
      commit('ADD_TRACKER', tracker)
    },

    updateTracker({ commit }, tracker) {
      commit('UPDATE_TRACKER', tracker)
    },

    removeTracker({ commit }, trackerId) {
      commit('REMOVE_TRACKER', trackerId)
    },
  },

  getters: {
    getProgressTrackers: state => {
      return state.progressTrackers;
    },
  }
})
```
Here, we implemented three mutations that change the Vuex state:

- `ADD_TRACKER` adds a new tracker to the list of progress trackers.
- `UPDATE_TRACKER` updates the progress of an existing tracker.
- `REMOVE_TRACKER` removes a tracker from the list.

We call these mutations via actions. Actions are what your components will call when they want to trigger a mutation. This abstracts away the direct state manipulation and enforces the Flux architecture principle of "actions up, mutations down".

We also implemented a getter `getProgressTrackers` that can be used by Vue components to retrieve the list of progress trackers.

You can then use the actions in one of your Vue components to manipulate the progress trackers. This can look something like this:

```JavaScript
this.$store.dispatch('addTracker', {
  id: 'tracker1',
  name: 'My Tracker',
  current: 0,
  total: 100,
});

let trackers = this.$store.getters.getProgressTrackers;
```
Keep in mind that this is quite a basic skeleton. You'll probably want to extend this code to fit your needs, e.g., handle API calls to persist the progress trackers, handle errors, improve the ID logic, etc.

