Building the required functionality involves several steps: 

1. Fetching the data from the API,
2. Creating a table dynamically and populating it with the data,
3. Adding pagination to the table,
4. Making the table sortable by country names and population numbers,
5. Adding live search functionality.

Please note, Javascript can't perform HTTP requests to 3rd party servers because of security concerns, except when these servers have CORS headers set up for the requesting domain. For the sake of the answer to this problem, let's imagine our API endpoint and domain we perform the requests from are agreed on these rules. Also please note that you need to put this code in a .html file and run in a browser to work correctly. XMLHttpRequest won't work with local files because of security rules.

Example code:

```HTML
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.5.1/jquery.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
    <input type="text" id="search" placeholder="Search..." />
    <table id="data-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th class="sortable" id="name">Name</th>
                <th class="sortable" id="population">Population</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody></tbody>
    </table>
    <button id="prev">Previous</button>
    <button id="next">Next</button>

    <script>
        var data = [];
        var page = 0;
        var rpp = 10; // rows per page

        function getData() {
            var ajax = new XMLHttpRequest();
            ajax.open('GET', 'API_ENDPOINT', true);
            ajax.onload = function () {
                if (ajax.status >= 200 && ajax.status < 400) {
                    data = JSON.parse(ajax.responseText).data;
                    showPage(0);
                }
            };
            ajax.send();
        }

        function showPage(n) {
            var tbody = document.querySelector('#data-table tbody');
            tbody.innerHTML = '';
            for(var i=n*rpp; i<(n+1)*rpp; i++) {
                if(i<data.length) {
                    var tr = document.createElement('tr');
                    tr.innerHTML = '<td>'+data[i].name+'</td><td>'+data[i].population+'</td>';
                    tbody.appendChild(tr);
                }
            }
            page = n;
        }

        document.querySelector("#next").addEventListener('click', function(){showPage(page+1);});
        document.querySelector("#prev").addEventListener('click', function(){if(page>0) showPage(page-1);});

        document.querySelectorAll('.sortable').forEach(function(th){
            th.addEventListener('click', function(){
                var property = th.id;
                data.sort(function(a, b){return a[property] > b[property] ? 1 : -1;});
                showPage(page);
            });
        });

        document.querySelector('#search').addEventListener('keyup', function(){
            var value = this.value.toLowerCase();
            var filteredData = data.filter(function(row){
                return row.name.toLowerCase().includes(value) || row.population.toString().includes(value);
            });
            data = filteredData;
            showPage(0);
        });

        getData();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```
Remember to replace `'API_ENDPOINT'` with your actual API Endpoint. This approach only provides a basic solution for the pagination, sorting and filtering functionality. The dataset size, performance, error handling, non-numeric population data sorting etc. are not considered. Depending on the actual needs of your project you should consider implementing these additional features.

