The provided C++ code performs multiple operations on an integer array named arr. This code is essentially calculating the weighted sum of the array's elements, where each weight is the corresponding index of the element, and this is done in a progressive manner.

In essence, for each i (on a 0-based index), it multiplies arr[i] with i, then it goes through all elements arr[j] where j > i, and multiplies each arr[j] with j, then adds these products to a variable "val". This process is repeated for each i on the array, and the "val" is added to a running total "sum". The final sum is printed out. The resulting sum is the sum of the products of each element and its index for every possible combination of distinct elements in the array.

Talking about the time complexity, for each element, the code loops over all the future elements. So, we could say that the time complexity of this code is O(n^2), where n is the size of the array. This is because for each element in the array, we go down a nested loop of size proportional to the array.

