Here is a simple example of how you might implement such a class in Java:

```java
public class Parrot {
    private String name; 
    private int age;
    private int wordCount;
    private Set<String> words;

    // Parrot constructor
    public Parrot(String name, int age, int wordCount) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
        this.wordCount = wordCount;
        this.words = new HashSet<String>();
    }

    // Method to teach the parrot a new word
    public void teachWord(String word) {
        // If the word is new and does not exist in the set, increase word count
        if(!words.contains(word)) {
            words.add(word);
            wordCount++;
        }
    }

    // Method to get the number of words the parrot can say
    public int getWordCount() {
        return wordCount;
    }

    // Method to output the parrot's information
    public void outputInfo() {
        System.out.println("Parrot name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Parrot age: " + age);
        System.out.println("Number of words can say: " + wordCount);
    }
}
```

In the main method, you can use it like:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    Parrot polly = new Parrot("Polly", 5, 30);

    polly.teachWord("Hello");
    polly.teachWord("Bye");

    polly.outputInfo();
}
```

Time complexity of teachWord method is O(1) because HashSet in java utilizes hashing, and searching in hashing takes O(1) time in average case. It can be O(N) in worst case if hash function generates same hash for all keys.
In the outputInfo method, time complexity is O(1) as we access and print out variables directly. So, it is an efficient implementation.

