The provided code does not correctly handle the removal of duplicates due to the in-place modification of the array that results in some elements being skipped in the nested loop. Hence, a better approach would be to track a unique position, shifting unique elements to said position and ultimately returning its value. This way, the program avoids redundantly traversing or modifying the array.

Here's the corrected code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int removeDuplicates(int arr[], int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) return n;

    // Variable to track unique elements
    int uniqueIndex = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        // If current element is not equal 
        // to next element, then update the 
        // uniqueIndex element
        if (arr[i] != arr[i+1]) {
            arr[uniqueIndex++] = arr[i];
        }
    }

    // Store the last element as whether
    // it is unique or repeated, it hasn't been
    // stored in uniqueIndex element
    arr[uniqueIndex++] = arr[n-1];

    return uniqueIndex;
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {1, 3, 1, 5, 5};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    // Array should be sorted to correctly remove duplicates
    sort(arr, arr + n);

    n = removeDuplicates(arr, n);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) cout << arr[i] << " ";
    return 0;
}
```

Note: The provided code assumes that the input array is sorted. Without sorting, it won't work correctly. If you can't assume sorted input, replace the inner loop in the original code with a searching function that searches the rest of the array for duplicates instead of only checking the next element. The requirement of not using any built-in or external libraries further complicates removal of duplicates from an unsorted array. Please reconsider this requirement if a sorted array cannot be guaranteed.

