Error handling in JavaScript is an essential part of programming, as it is with any language. This is due to the fact that during the course of programming it is quite common to encounter a variety of errors--syntax errors, runtime errors, and logical errors. Syntax and runtime errors are often caught by the JavaScript environment but logical errors can occur at any time during the execution of a script.

The most basic process of error handling in JavaScript is using the try...catch structure. You wrap your potentially error-raising block of code in a "try" and then catch any errors that come up in the "catch". Here is a basic example.

```javascript
try {
    // code that may raise an error
} catch (e) {
    // handle the error
}
```

In the "catch" you get an exception object (here "e") that you can use to investigate what kind of error arose. This object will contain a message about the error, the name of the error, and often a stack trace. 

You can classify the errors according to their names or some other properties. For example you can do this:

```javascript
try {
    // code that may raise an error
} catch (e) {
    if (e instanceof ReferenceError) {
        // handle a reference error
    } else if (e instanceof TypeError) {
        // handle a type error
    } else {
        // handle other sorts of errors
    }
}
```

Recovering from an error depends on the specifics of what your code is trying to accomplish and how it can reasonably continue after an error. It may be as simple as assigning a default value to a variable, or it may require complex logic or even user interaction.

You can also create custom errors using the Error constructor or a subclass of Error. This lets you signal specific kinds of errors that aren't represented by the standard JavaScript exceptions. Here is a simple example.

```javascript
class ValidationError extends Error {
    constructor(message) {
        super(message);
        this.name = "ValidationError";
    }
}

try {
    throw new ValidationError("Invalid input");
} catch (e) {
    if (e instanceof ValidationError) {
        // handle a validation error
    } else {
        // handle other sorts of errors
    }
}
```

In addition to the basic try...catch, JavaScript also provides the "finally" clause, which contains code that will be run after the try and catch, regardless of whether an error was thrown. This can be useful for cleaning up.

