In Kotlin, you can create a function that generates a sequence of prime numbers up to a certain limit. Here's a simple implementation of the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm, which is one of the most efficient ways to find all prime numbers smaller than a given number n:

```kotlin
fun generatePrimes(limit: Int): List<Int> {
    val numbers = BooleanArray(limit + 1) { true }
    numbers[0] = false
    numbers[1] = false
    var p = 2
    while (p * p <= limit) {
        if (numbers[p]) {
            for (i in p * p..limit step p) {
                numbers[i] = false
            }
        }
        p += 1
    }
    val primes = mutableListOf<Int>()
    for (i in 2..limit) {
        if (numbers[i]) {
            primes.add(i)
        }
    }
    return primes
}

fun main() {
    val limit = 100
    val primes = generatePrimes(limit)
    println("Prime numbers up to $limit: $primes")
}
```

This program works by initially assuming every number is prime. It starts from 2, the first prime number, and marks every multiple of 2 as not prime. Then it moves to the next unmarked number and repeats the process, until it has processed every number up to the square root of the limit. The remaining unmarked numbers are all primes. After calculating, it adds these numbers to a list and the list of prime numbers is returned. 

In the main function, you can set your limit and then it will print out all prime numbers up to this limit.

