Sorting an array is a form of array organization that can be accomplished through various sorting algorithms. The choice of algorithm often depends on the specific requirements such as the size of the array and the order of initial elements.

The most efficient, general-purpose, comparison-based sorting algorithm in theoretical computer science is considered to be quicksort, with a time complexity of O(n log n) in the average case. However, quicksort has the drawback of having a worst-case time complexity of O(n²). Merge sort also runs in O(n log n) time in all cases, but has the disadvantage of requiring O(n) auxiliary space.

In special cases, more efficient sorting can be achieved. For example, for an array of integers where the range of possible values is limited, counting sort can sort the array in O(n) time. Beyond that, radix sort and bucket sort can also offer O(n) complexity under certain specific conditions. 

Here's a basic implementation of quicksort in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int* a, int* b) {
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

int partition (int arr[], int low, int high) {
    int pivot = arr[high];
    int i = (low - 1);

    for (int j = low; j <= high- 1; j++) {
        if (arr[j] <= pivot) {
            i++;
            swap(&arr[i], &arr[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(&arr[i + 1], &arr[high]);
    return (i + 1);
}

void quickSort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
        int pi = partition(arr, low, high);
        quickSort(arr, low, pi - 1);
        quickSort(arr, pi + 1, high);
    }
}
```

