Here is an example demonstrating how to recursively traverse and manipulate a deeply nested object in JavaScript and convert all keys to camelCase. This implementation includes a tracker object to handle circular references.

```javascript
function toCamelCase(str) {
    return str.replace(/([-_][a-z])/ig, ($1) => {
        return $1.toUpperCase()
            .replace('-', '')
            .replace('_', '');
    });
}

function isObject(object) {
    return object === Object(object) && !Array.isArray(object) && typeof object !== 'function';
}

function convertKeysToCamelCase(nestedObj, tracker = new WeakMap()) {
    if (tracker.has(nestedObj)) {
        return tracker.get(nestedObj);
    }

    if (isObject(nestedObj)) {
        const convertedObject = {};
        tracker.set(nestedObj, convertedObject);

        Object.keys(nestedObj)
            .forEach(key => {
                convertedObject[toCamelCase(key)] = convertKeysToCamelCase(nestedObj[key], tracker);
            });

        return convertedObject;
    } else if (Array.isArray(nestedObj)) {
        return nestedObj.map(item => convertKeysToCamelCase(item, tracker));
    }

    return nestedObj;
}

const nestedObject = {
    a_first_key: {
        a_second_key: {
            a_third_key: 'value'
        },
        another_second_key: 'value'
    },
    another_first_key: 'value'
}

console.log(convertKeysToCamelCase(nestedObject));
```

You may replace 'toCamelCase()' function with any other function that serves your purpose. 

Note that this implementation won't take care of the case when your values themselves are strings that you want to convert to camelCase.

The function "toCamelCase()" takes a string and converts it to camelCase.
The function "isObject()" checks if a variable is an object, but not an array or function.
The function "convertKeysToCamelCase()" manipulates a deeply nested objects to camelCase, including their keys, values, and subkeys at all depths of the input object. The helper WeakMap "tracker" is used as a mechanism to prevent stack overflow with circular structures: it keeps track of the already visited references and upon encountering them again, it uses the already computed value for them.

