The main error in the provided code is the use of `HashSet` instead of `TreeSet`. `HashSet` does not guarantee any specific order of its elements, whereas `TreeSet` stores elements in a sorted (ascending) order. Here's the corrected code:

```java
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.TreeSet;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<Integer> set = new TreeSet<Integer>(); // Use TreeSet instead of HashSet

        set.add(23);  // Add elements to the set
        set.add(44);
        set.add(37);
        set.add(29);
        set.add(31);

        for (Integer num : set){
            System.out.println(num);  // Print each element in the set
        }
    }
}
```

Explanation:

- `Set<Integer> set = new TreeSet<Integer>();`: This line initializes a new `TreeSet` object that will hold `Integer` objects. A `Set` object in Java holds a collection of objects, and does not allow duplicate elements. `TreeSet` is a class that implements the `Set` interface and maintains its elements in a sorted order.
- `set.add(VALUE);`: These lines add integers to the `TreeSet`. When adding elements to a `TreeSet`, the set automatically sorts its elements.
- `for (Integer num : set) { ... }`: This is a for-each loop that iterates over each element in the set. The variable `num` takes on the value of each element in the set one-by-one, from the first to the last.
- `System.out.println(num);`: This line prints the current value of `num` to the console. Since `num` takes on the value of each element in the set in order, this will print all the elements of the set to the console, in ascending order.

