Here is a C++ solution that solves the problem via dynamic programming approach. The function getMaxProductOfNonAdjacent accepts an array along with its size and returns the maximum product of non-adjacent elements in the array.
```cpp
#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

long long getMaxProductOfNonAdjacent(int arr[], int n) {
    // handle corner cases - if array size is less than or equal to 3
    if (n <= 0)
        return 0;
    else if (n == 1)
        return arr[0];
    else if (n == 2)
        return max(arr[0], arr[1]);
    else if (n == 3)
        return max({arr[0], arr[1], arr[2]});

    // Initialize an array to store the results of subproblems
    long long dp[n];
    dp[0] = arr[0];
    dp[1] = max(arr[0], arr[1]);
    dp[2] = max({dp[1], dp[0]*arr[2], arr[2]});

    // Fill up the rest of the array
    for (int i = 3; i < n; i++) {
        long long product = arr[i];
        for (int j = i-2; j >= 0; j--) {
            dp[i] = max({dp[i-1], dp[j]*product});
            product *= arr[j];
        }
        dp[i] = max(dp[i], product);
    }

    // Answer is stored at dp[n-1]
    return dp[n-1];
}

int main()
{
    int arr[] = {1,5,7,0,-2,3};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    cout << getMaxProductOfNonAdjacent(arr, n);
    return 0;
}
```
This solution basically keep track of the maximum product of non-adjacent elements for each index up to i in dp[i], where i ranges from 0 to n-1. It also handle corner cases where there are 0, 1 or 2, 3 elements in the array.

Please note that this solution assumes that array can have both positive and negative numbers. And also it is limited by the range of long long as the product can go huge. If you are certain about your input size and range, you should be fine using this code.

