Here is a simple solution to this problem coded in C++. Our first method will use an O(n^2) sorting algorithm which in this case will be the bubble sort algorithm. The are simpler algorithms that can sort arrays, but they generally have a time complexity of O(n^2). The other method we're going to use for sorting the array will be the merge sort algorithm which has a time complexity of O(n log n) which is much better than O(n^2), especially for larger data sets.

Solution 1: Bubble sort
```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <ctime>

void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n)
{
    for(int i = 0; i < n-1; i++)
    {
        for(int j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++)
        {
            if(arr[j] > arr[j+1])
            {
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```
Solution 2: Merge sort
```C++
void merge(int arr[], int l, int m, int r)
{
    int i, j, k;
    int n1 = m - l + 1;
    int n2 = r - m;

    /* create temp arrays */
    int L[n1], R[n2];

    /* Copy data to temp arrays L[] and R[] */
    for (i = 0; i < n1; i++)
        L[i] = arr[l + i];
    for (j = 0; j < n2; j++)
        R[j] = arr[m + 1 + j];

    /* Merge the temp arrays back into arr[l..r]*/
    i = 0;
    j = 0;
    k = l;
    while (i < n1 && j < n2)
    {
        if (L[i] <= R[j])
        {
            arr[k] = L[i];
            i++;
        }
        else
        {
            arr[k] = R[j];
            j++;
        }
        k++;
    }

    /* Copy the remaining elements of L[], if there are any */
    while (i < n1)
    {
        arr[k] = L[i];
        i++;
        k++;
    }

    /* Copy the remaining elements of R[], if there are any */
    while (j < n2)
    {
        arr[k] = R[j];
        j++;
        k++;
    }
}

void mergeSort(int arr[], int l, int r)
{
    if (l < r)
    {
        int m = l + (r - l) / 2;

        mergeSort(arr, l, m);
        mergeSort(arr, m + 1, r);

        merge(arr, l, m, r);
    }
}
```
The bubble sort doesn't need any extra space, therefore, its space complexity is O(1). But the time complexity is O(n^2) in the worst case when the array is reverse sorted. 

The merge sort requires extra space for the temporary arrays while merging. These temporary arrays are needed for every recursive call. Hence, the space complexity is O(n). The time complexity for merge sort is O(nlogn) for all cases.

Besides the time, there's also space to consider. While bubble sort just does in-place swapping, so requires constant space O(1), Merge Sort requires space (for the merge operation) which is O(n). The merge sort algorithm is more efficient than bubble sort on large lists. 

