In JavaScript, we can accomplish this using mutation observers. The MutationObserver interface provides the ability to watch for changes in the DOM.

Here is a JavaScript function that uses a MutationObserver to handle this:

```javascript
function processActiveElements() {
    // Selecting existing active elements
    let activeElements = document.getElementsByClassName('active');

    // Add 'processed' to all existing active elements
    Array.from(activeElements).forEach(element => {
        if (!element.classList.contains('processed')) {
            element.classList.add('processed');
        }
    });

    // Creating a mutation observer
    let observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
        mutations.forEach((mutation) => {
            if (mutation.addedNodes) {
              mutation.addedNodes.forEach((node) => {
                  if (node.nodeType === 1 && node.classList.contains('active')) {
                      // Add 'processed' class to new class active
                      node.classList.add('processed');
                  }
              });
            }
        });
    });

    // Start observing the document with the configured parameters
    observer.observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true });
}

processActiveElements();
```

In this script, we first get all elements with class "active" and add "processed" class to them. Then, the MutationObserver watches for changes in the DOM – specifically, the addition of new nodes. If newly inserted nodes contain the "active" class, the function adds "processed" class to them. 

Be aware that MutationObserver is not supported in Internet Explorer. If you are considering to support IE, you might want to look into some kind of polyfill or reconsider your approach. Also bear in mind that observing the whole document might have performance implications if it's a large application with many DOM changes. In these cases, it would be recommended to narrow down the area which you are observing for mutations.

Here is a simple implementation of a callback function that handles click events while caching result. This scenario uses a memoization technique which will store the count of the clicks and logger it in the console for every click event happens.

HTML: 

