Here's a simple Java program that prints all prime numbers between 1 and 1000:

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 1000; i++) {
            if (isPrime(i)) {
                System.out.println(i);
            }
        }
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int num) {
        if (num <= 1) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int i = 2; i * i <= num; i++) {
            if (num % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

In this program, the `main` method iterates over the numbers from 1 to 1000 and calls the `isPrime` method for each number.

The `isPrime` function checks if a number is prime. It returns `false` for numbers less than or equal to 1 as they are not prime numbers. Then it checks the divisibility of the number from 2 up to the square root of the number. If the number can be divided by any of them without a remainder, the function immediately returns `false` — the number is not prime. If the number passes this test, the function returns `true`, indicating that the number is prime.

