public void reverseArray(int[] arr) {
    for(int i = 0; i < arr.length / 2; i++) {
        arr[i] = arr[i] + arr[arr.length - 1 - i]; 
        arr[arr.length - 1 - i] = arr[i] - arr[arr.length - 1 - i]; 
        arr[i] = arr[i] - arr[arr.length - 1 - i];
    }
}
```

This method uses a trick to swap elements without needing a temp variable. The key lies in these 3 lines of code:

```java
arr[i] = arr[i] + arr[arr.length - 1 - i]; 
arr[arr.length - 1 - i] = arr[i] - arr[arr.length - 1 - i]; 
arr[i] = arr[i] - arr[arr.length - 1 - i];
```

The first line adds the two elements being swapped and assigns the sum to the first element. The second line subtracts the original value of the last element (which is still stored in the first element) from the sum (which is now stored in the first element), thus deriving the original value of the first element and assigning it to the last one. Finally, the third line subtracts the value now stored in the last element (which is actually the original value of the first element) from the first element (which holds the sum of the original values), yielding the original value of the last element.

