```JavaScript 
function addToArray(array, integer) {
    let exists = false;

    // Check if integer exists in array
    for(let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
        if(array[i] === integer) {
            exists = true;
            break;
        }
    }

    // If integer doesn't exist, add to beginning of array
    if(!exists) {
        let newArray = [integer];

        for(let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
            newArray[i + 1] = array[i];
        }

        array = newArray;
    }

    return array;
}
```

This function, `addToArray`, first initializes a variable `exists` to `false`. It then iterates over the input array checking if the input integer is equal to the current element of the array. If at any point it finds a match, it sets `exists` to `true` and breaks from the loop. 

If after checking all elements, `exists` remains `false`, it creates a new array with the input integer as the first element, followed by all elements from the input array.

Finally, the function returns the input array if the integer was found, or the new array if the integer was not found. 

Remember that JavaScript arrays are zero-indexed, so the first element has index 0.

