```

This should return `g~q~f`.

For time complexity, in the worst-case scenario, there are n distinct characters in the string, so `count_chars(s)` has a linear time complexity O(n). The function `remove_dupes(s, limit)` also scans the string once, which is another O(n). However, these operations are performed in sequence, not in nested loops, so the overall time complexity remains linear, O(n).

As for space complexity, the `counts` dictionary will, in the worst-case scenario, hold n different characters, where n is the length of the string. This makes the space complexity O(n) as well.

Hence, the time and space complexity of this solution is O(n).

