Here is a basic implementation of an event system in C#. This may not be the most advanced implementation possible but it should be more than enough to get you started in the right direction.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace EventDemo
{
    // Define a delegate for each type of event
    public delegate void SimpleEvent();
    public delegate void ParameterEvent(object sender);

    //Main event system class
    public class EventSystem
    {
        // Define events based on the delegates
        public event SimpleEvent OnSimpleEvent;
        public event ParameterEvent OnParameterEvent;

        // Define a method for each type of event that will notify the appropriate delegate
        public async void TriggerSimpleEvent()
        {
            try{
                await Task.Run(()=>OnSimpleEvent?.Invoke());
            }
            catch(Exception e){
                //Handle exception
                Console.WriteLine(e);
            }
        }
        public async void TriggerParameterEvent(object sender)
        {
            try{
                await Task.Run(()=>OnParameterEvent?.Invoke(sender));
            }
            catch(Exception e){
                //Handle exception
                Console.WriteLine(e);
            }
        }
    }

    class Program{
        static void Main(string[] args){
            EventSystem eventSystem = new EventSystem();

            //Register for events
            eventSystem.OnSimpleEvent += onSimpleEventTriggered;
            eventSystem.OnParameterEvent += (sender) => { Console.WriteLine($"{sender} has triggered an event"); };

            //Trigger the event
            eventSystem.TriggerSimpleEvent();
            eventSystem.TriggerParameterEvent(DateTime.Now);
        }

        static void onSimpleEventTriggered(){ Console.WriteLine("A simple event has been triggered");}
    }
}
```

This code defines two types of events: SimpleEvent which takes no parameters and ParameterEvent which takes an object parameter. Each event has an associated asynchronous method that triggers the event. The logic of these methods includes the usage of null-conditional invocation which prevents a NullReferenceException in case there are no subscriptions to the event being triggered. And these methods are enclosed within a try-catch block to handle any exceptions that may happen during the invocation of the event.

In this example, we subscribe to the events from our Main function. For the SimpleEvent, we define a simple method "onSimpleEventTriggered()" that will be called when the event triggers. And for the ParameterEvent, we are specifying the event handling logic inline using an anonymous delegate. We then call the trigger methods in the event system to simulate triggering the events.

