Here is a basic solution in JavaScript using Class syntax:

```js
class Point {
    constructor(x, y, z) {
        this.x = Number(x);
        this.y = Number(y);
        this.z = Number(z);
    }

    distanceTo(point) {
        if (!(point instanceof Point))
            throw new Error('Argument should be an instance of Point.');

        let dx = this.x - point.x;
        let dy = this.y - point.y;
        let dz = this.z - point.z;

        return Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz);
    }

    angleTo(point) {
        if (!(point instanceof Point))
            throw new Error('Argument should be an instance of Point.');

        let dx = Math.abs(this.x - point.x);
        let dy = Math.abs(this.y - point.y);
        let dz = Math.abs(this.z - point.z);

        return Math.acos(dx / (Math.sqrt(dy * dy + dz * dz)));
    }

    static areCoplanar(point1, point2, point3) {
        if (!(point1 instanceof Point) || !(point2 instanceof Point) || !(point3 instanceof Point))
            throw new Error('All arguments should be instances of Point.');

        let vector1 = [point2.x - point1.x, point2.y - point1.y, point2.z - point1.z];
        let vector2 = [point3.x - point1.x, point3.y - point1.y, point3.z - point1.z];

        let vectorProduct = [vector1[1]*vector2[2] - vector1[2]*vector2[1], vector1[2]*vector2[0] - vector1[0]*vector2[2], vector1[0]*vector2[1] - vector1[1]*vector2[0]];

        return (vectorProduct[0] === 0) && (vectorProduct[1] === 0) && (vectorProduct[2] === 0);
    }
}
```

This class defines a `Point` that is initialized with `x`, `y`, and `z`. The `distanceTo` method calculates the distance to another point using the Euclidean distance formula. The `angleTo` method calculates the angle in radians between this point and another point through the dot product. The `areCoplanar` static method determines whether three points are on the same plane, which is the case if the vector product of the vectors formed by the three points is zero. Please note that as of JavaScript, the number is treated as a floating-point number, it comes with floating point errors, if precision is a concern, you may need to consider it.

