Here is a Java program that performs a bubble sort:

```Java
public class BubbleSort {
    void bubbleSort(int array[]) {
        int n = array.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++)
                if (array[j] > array[j+1]) {
                    // swap array[j+1] and array[i]
                    int temp = array[j];
                    array[j] = array[j+1];
                    array[j+1] = temp;
                }
    }

    /* Prints the array */
    void printArray(int array[]) {
        int n = array.length;
        for (int i=0; i<n; ++i)
            System.out.print(array[i] + " ");
        System.out.println();
    }

    // Driver method to test above
    public static void main(String args[]) {
        BubbleSort bs = new BubbleSort();
        int array[] = {5, 2, 8, 10, 4, 9};
        bs.bubbleSort(array);
        System.out.println("Sorted array in ascending order:");
        bs.printArray(array);
    }
}
```

This program works by repeatedly swapping the adjacent elements if they are in wrong order. So, when it's done, you will have your array sorted in ascending order.

