Here is a basic implementation that will work in SQL Server. This script expects two arguments: table name and column name.

Please note that dynamically building a SQL statement based purely on user input can lead to a serious security risk, a SQL Injection. It's better to validate the inputs and ensure that only trusted users may run the procedure.

```sql
CREATE PROCEDURE CalcStats 
    @TableName NVARCHAR(128),
    @ColumnName NVARCHAR(128)
AS
BEGIN
    DECLARE @Sql NVARCHAR(MAX);

    SET @Sql = '
        DECLARE @Average FLOAT;
        DECLARE @Min FLOAT;
        DECLARE @Max FLOAT;
        DECLARE @CountLessAverage INT;
        DECLARE @CountMoreAverage INT;

        SELECT @Average = AVG(' + QUOTENAME(@ColumnName) + '), 
               @Min = MIN(' + QUOTENAME(@ColumnName) + '), 
               @Max = MAX(' + QUOTENAME(@ColumnName) + ')
        FROM ' + QUOTENAME(@TableName) + ';

        SELECT @CountLessAverage = COUNT(*)
        FROM ' + QUOTENAME(@TableName) + '
        WHERE ' + QUOTENAME(@ColumnName) + ' < @Average;

        SELECT @CountMoreAverage = COUNT(*)
        FROM ' + QUOTENAME(@TableName) + '
        WHERE ' + QUOTENAME(@ColumnName) + ' > @Average;

        SELECT @Average AS Average, 
               @Min AS Min, 
               @Max AS Max, 
               @CountLessAverage AS CountLessAverage, 
               @CountMoreAverage AS CountMoreAverage;';

    EXEC sp_executesql @Sql;
END;
```
You can call the stored procedure like this:

```sql
EXEC CalcStats 'Student', 'Score';
``` 

This procedure calculates the average, minimum, and maximum values of the column you provide from the table you provide. Then, it calculates the number of rows that have less than and more than the average. Finally, it returns the five calculated values.

