Here is a simple implementation of a Doubly Linked List with a HashMap to optimize search operations. Hash Map stores the nodes of Linked List according to their values. 

While HashMap provides constant time complexity for searching operation, keep in mind that it also uses extra memory to store the same data, so it may not be efficient if memory footprint is a concern and the linked list is big.

```cpp
#include<unordered_map>
using namespace std;

//Node structure
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node* prev;
    Node(int val) : data(val), next(nullptr), prev(nullptr){}
};

//Doubly Linked List Class
class DoublyLinkedList {
    private:
        Node* head;
        Node* tail;
        unordered_map<int, Node*> nodeMap;
    public:
        DoublyLinkedList() : head(nullptr), tail(nullptr){}

        //Insert function
        void insert(int val) {
            Node* newNode = new Node(val);
            nodeMap[val] = newNode;
            if(head == nullptr){
                head = tail = newNode;
            }
            else {
                tail->next = newNode;
                newNode->prev = tail;
                tail = newNode;
            }
        }

        //Search function
        Node* search(int val) {
            if(nodeMap.find(val) != nodeMap.end())
                return nodeMap[val];
            else
                return nullptr;
        }

        //Delete function
        bool erase(int val) {
            Node* node = search(val);
            if(node == nullptr)
                return false;
            if(node->prev) 
                node->prev->next = node->next;
            if(node->next) 
                node->next->prev = node->prev;
            if(node == head) 
                head = node->next;
            if(node == tail) 
                tail = node->prev;
            nodeMap.erase(val);
            delete node;
            return true;
        }
};
```

This design assures O(1) for search function, while insert and erase function have also O(1) (assuming that hash function of unordered_map takes a constant time and do not cause rehashing). 

Please note that in reality there could be collision in hash map, which would result in more than O(1) operations. In addition, if number of erase operations is big, map could become sparse and cause use of extra memory. 

Also, while we assumed that the hash function operations are O(1), the cost of a good hash function often is more than O(1) operations. Hence, always take into consideration the trade-off between time and space complexity based on use case requirements.

