The apex or zenith of C++ is its powerful features such as Object-Oriented Programming (OOP), Standard Template Library (STL), and performance efficiency.

1. Object-Oriented Programming (OOP): C++ introduces the concept of class and object, which allows the structuring of complex real-world problems in a natural way. Additionally, C++ supports other OOP features like inheritance, polymorphism, encapsulation, and abstraction that not only improve the code reusability but also enhance security.

```cpp
// Basic OOP in C++
class Animal{       
    public:
        void sound(){
            cout << "This is an Animal" << endl;
        }         
};
class Dog : public Animal{  
    public:
        void sound(){
            cout << "Dog Barks!" << endl;
        }   
};
```
In the above code, 'Dog' is a derived class that inherits from the base class 'Animal'. The function sound is overridden in the derived class which is an example of polymorphism.

1. Standard Template Library (STL): C++ offers a powerhouse of pre-built libraries for various data structures and algorithms, known as Standard Template Library. They help in expediting the coding process and guarantee efficient execution of the code as they are generally well optimized. The major components are algorithms, containers, functions, and iterators.

```cpp
// STL in C++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {10, 40, 30, 20};
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());

    for (auto i: v)
        std::cout << i << " ";
    return 0;
}
```
In the above code, a vector container is used to hold integers, and a pre-built 'sort' function from STL is used to sort the values.

2. Performance Efficiency: C++ has the capability of low-level manipulation which helps in optimizing the code to run faster and use less memory. It does not have garbage collection and allows explicit control over memory management, which makes it highly efficient.

Here's an example of memory management:

```cpp
// Efficiency in C++
int *p = new int;   // Allocating memory
*p = 10;

cout << *p << endl;

delete p;  // Deleting allocated memory
```
In this code, memory is allocated dynamically and freed up manually thus giving better control over memory management.

In essence, these features place C++ at the apex of programming languages, enhancing the efficiency of the language and enabling the creation of high-performance software applications.

