```python
class BankAccount:
    def __init__(self, name, balance, interest_rate):
        self.name = name
        self.balance = balance
        if interest_rate < 0 or interest_rate > 0.1:
            raise ValueError("Interest rate must be between 0 and 0.1.")
        self.interest_rate = interest_rate

    def withdraw(self, amount):
        if amount < 0:
            raise ValueError("You cannot withdraw a negative amount of money.")
        if amount > self.balance:
            raise ValueError("You do not have sufficient funds to withdraw this amount.")
        self.balance -= amount

    def deposit(self, amount):
        if amount < 0:
            raise ValueError("You cannot deposit a negative amount of money.")
        self.balance += amount

    def transfer(self, other_account, amount):
        if amount < 0:
            raise ValueError("You cannot transfer a negative amount of money.")
        if amount > self.balance:
            raise ValueError("You do not have sufficient funds to transfer this amount.")
        self.withdraw(amount)
        other_account.deposit(amount)
```

This class checks for errors based on conditions in the methods for withdrawing, depositing, and transferring funds - such as making sure you can't withdraw more money than you have in your account, or deposit or transfer negative amounts of money. The interest_rate attribute also has an error check to make sure the rate is sensible (between 0 and 10%).

