In Cascading Style Sheets (CSS), one of the most important aspects is the selection of HTML elements. It gives the ability to apply styling to specific elements. HTML elements can be selected in several ways, namely:

1. Element Selector: The element selector selects HTML elements based on the element type. For instance, if you want to select all paragraph (`<p>`) tags:

   ```css
   p {
     color: blue;
   }
   ```

   This will make the text color of all the paragraph tags blue.

2. ID Selector: If you want to select a specific element, you can use its identifier or id. ID is a unique attribute given to an HTML element. The ID selector is denoted by a hash `#`. For instance, if an element has an id of `myID`:

   ```css
   #myID {
     color: red;
   }
   ```
   This will change the text color to red for the HTML element with id `myID`.

3. Class Selector: Another way to select HTML elements is by using their class. Class is an attribute given to HTML elements and unlike ids, classes are not unique, meaning multiple elements can have the same class. The class selector is denoted by a dot `.`. For instance, if elements have a class of `myClass`:

   ```css
   .myClass {
     color: green;
   }
   ```

   This will change the text color to green for all HTML elements with a class of `myClass`.

4. Attribute Selector: This type of selector chooses HTML elements based on their attributes and values. If you want to select an element with a specific attribute, then this selector is very useful.

   ```css
   input[type="text"] {
     background-color: yellow;
   }
   ```

   This will change the background color to yellow for all input elements of type text.

These selectors can be combined to select elements. For instance, if you want to select all paragraph elements with a certain class inside an element with a certain id, you could do:

```css
#myID .myClass p {
  color: purple;
}
```

This will change the text color to purple for all paragraph elements with a class of `myClass` that are located inside an element with an id of `myID`.

