In the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm, we start from first prime number (2), and mark all of its multiples. The next unmarked number is the next prime number. The process continues until we 
have processed all numbers:

```javascript
let primes = new Array(101).fill(true);
primes[0] = primes[1] = false; // 0 and 1 are not prime numbers.

for (let i = 2; i * i <= 100; i++) {
    if (primes[i]) { // it will only take the ones that are true (prime numbers)
        // Mark factors of i false.
        for (let j = i * i; j <= 100; j += i) {
            primes[j] = false;
        }
    }
}

for (let i = 2; i <= 100; i++) {
    if (primes[i]) {
        console.log(i);
    }
}
```
In this implementation of the Sieve of Eratosthenes, the outer loop runs from 2 to square root of 'n' (100 in this case). The reason is that a larger factor of n must be a multiple of a 
smaller factor that has already been checked.

The second inner loop marks the factors of i as false. We start from i*i because a smaller factor would have already marked its multiples false.

In the final loop, we print all the prime numbers. Those indexes which are true represent primes.

Here's a simple JavaScript function that sorts the numbers in descending order and returns the second number in the sorted list.

Here is the JavaScript function:

```javascript
function secondLargestNumber(arr) {
  let sortedArr = arr.sort(function(a, b) { return b - a });
  return sortedArr[1];
}

let arr = [5, 19, 2, 46, 15, 31];
console.log(secondLargestNumber(arr));  // This would output 31
```
This function works by sorting the array in descending order so the largest number is first and the second largest number is second. By returning `sortedArr[1]`, we're returning the second element in the sorted array, which is the second largest number. 

However, if you want a more performant solution that works in linear time, you can use the following approach that only needs one pass through the array:

```javascript
function secondLargestNumber(arr) {
  let firstLargest = -Infinity, secondLargest = -Infinity;
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] > firstLargest) {
      secondLargest = firstLargest;
      firstLargest = arr[i];
    } else if (arr[i] > secondLargest && arr[i] < firstLargest) {
      secondLargest = arr[i];
    }
  }
  return secondLargest;
}

let arr = [5, 19, 2, 46, 15, 31];
console.log(secondLargestNumber(arr));  // This would output 31
```
This function works by keeping track of the two largest numbers it has seen so far during its iteration over the array. With each new number, it checks if it is larger than the current largest number. If it is, it updates the second largest number to be the current largest number and updates the largest number to be the new number. If it's not larger than the current largest but is larger than the second largest, it updates the second largest number to be the new number.

