This problem requires knowledge of HTML (HyperText Markup Language) and JavaScript, two popular languages for web design. The scenario describes an interactive form with two checkboxes for "Yes" and "No". The JavaScript code should ensure only one checkbox can be selected at a time and that a confirmation box appears when a box is checked. If a user tries to submit the form without selection, an error message appears.

Below you'll find the HTML & JavaScript code to solve this problem:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>

<form id="myForm" onsubmit="return validateForm()">
  <input type="checkbox" id="yes" name="yes" onclick="checkboxSelection('yes')">
  <label for="yes">Yes</label><br>
  <input type="checkbox" id="no" name="no" onclick="checkboxSelection('no')">
  <label for="no">No</label><br>
  <input type="submit" value="Submit">
</form>

<script>
function checkboxSelection(boxId) {
  let box = document.getElementById(boxId); 
  let otherBox = document.getElementById(boxId == 'yes'? 'no' : 'yes');  
  if (box.checked) {
    let result = confirm("Are you sure about your selection?");
    if(result === true){
      otherBox.checked = false;
    } else {
      box.checked = false;
    }
  }
}

function validateForm() {
   let yesBox = document.getElementById('yes');
   let noBox = document.getElementById('no');

   if (!yesBox.checked && !noBox.checked) {
      alert("Please select one of the two available options.");
      return false;
   } else {
      return true;
   }
}
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

In this example, `checkboxSelection` function is attached to the `onclick` event of both checkboxes. When a box is clicked, a confirmation prompt is displayed. If "OK" is selected, the other box is unchecked. If "Cancel" is selected, the click is undone.

`validateForm` function is attached to the form `onsubmit` event. It checks if neither checkbox was selected. If so, it shows a message and returns false to prevent form submission.

