The problem can be solved by divide and conquer algorithm using multithreading in C++. Threading enables a programmer to achieve concurrent processing.

Any divide and conquer algorithm consists of two parts:

- Divide: This involves dividing the data into smaller parts.
- Conquer: Solving the small portion and recombining them to get the solution of the original problem.

Here is a C++ program utilizing the multi-threading and divide-and-conquer mentality to solve the problem:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <climits>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

struct ArraySection
{
    int* start;
    int length;
    int sum;
    int max_subarray_sum;
};

void* max_subarray(void* arg)
{
    ArraySection* sec = (ArraySection*)arg;

    int max_ending_here = 0, max_ending_prev = INT_MIN;
    sec->sum = 0, sec->max_subarray_sum = INT_MIN;

    for(int i = 0; i < sec->length; i++)
    {
        sec->sum += sec->start[i];
        max_ending_here = max(sec->start[i], max_ending_here+sec->start[i]);
        max_ending_prev = max(max_ending_prev, max_ending_here);
    }

    sec->max_subarray_sum = max_ending_prev;

    pthread_exit(0);
}

int max_crossing_sum(ArraySection* sec1, ArraySection* sec2)
{
    int sum = 0;
    int left_sum = INT_MIN;
    for(int i = sec1->length - 1; i >= 0; i--)
    {
        sum += sec1->start[i];
        if(sum > left_sum)
            left_sum = sum;
    }

    sum = 0;
    int right_sum = INT_MIN;
    for(int i = 0; i < sec2->length; i++)
    {
        sum += sec2->start[i];
        if(sum > right_sum)
            right_sum = sum;
    }

    int cross_max = max(max(left_sum, right_sum), left_sum + right_sum);

    return max(cross_max, max(sec1->max_subarray_sum, sec2->max_subarray_sum));
}

int main()
{
    int arr[] = {-2, -5, 6, -2, -3, 1, 5, -6};
    int len = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);

    pthread_t threads[2];
    ArraySection arr_sec[2];

    arr_sec[0].start = &arr[0];
    arr_sec[0].length = len/2;
    arr_sec[1].start = &arr[len/2];
    arr_sec[1].length = len - len/2;

    for(int i = 0; i < 2; i++)
        pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, max_subarray, (void*)&arr_sec[i]);

    for(int i = 0; i < 2; i++)
        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);

    cout << "Max sum: " << max_crossing_sum(&arr_sec[0], &arr_sec[1]) << endl;
}
```
In this program, the main function splits the array in half and assigns each half to a separate thread. These threads call 'max_subarray' function to find the maximum sum of any subarray within their respective half. At the end, 'max_crossing_sum' function is called to find a maximum subarray sum that crosses the midpoint and maximum of these three values is the maximum sum of subarray in whole array. 

This program only works for arrays of even length. Modifications would be needed to function correctly with arrays of odd length. 

Note: This code should be compiled with "-pthread" flag in g++. For example, 
```sh
g++ -pthread -o main main.cpp
```

