Here is a Javascript function that uses a hashmap data structure to solve your problem. 

This function identifies and quantifies the most recurring substring in the given chromatic string:

```Javascript
function findLongestRecurringSubstr(str) {
    let length = str.length;
    let hashmap = new Map();
    let ans = '';
    let maxFrequency = -1;
    for(let i=0; i<length; i++){
        let subStr = '';
        for(let j=i; j<length; j++){
            subStr = subStr + str[j];
            if(hashmap.has(subStr)){
                hashmap.set(subStr, hashmap.get(subStr) + 1);
                if(maxFrequency < hashmap.get(subStr)){
                    maxFrequency = hashmap.get(subStr);
                    ans = subStr;
                }
                else if(maxFrequency === hashmap.get(subStr)){
                    if(ans.length < subStr.length){
                        ans = subStr;
                    }
                }
            }
            else{
                hashmap.set(subStr, 1);
            }
        }
    }
    return {"Substring": ans, "Frequency": maxFrequency};
}

console.log(findLongestRecurringSubstr("banana"));
```

The time complexity of this function can be considered to be O(n^3) due to the nested for loop and substr. The space complexity will be O(n^2) as we're storing all substrings in the map. Please note, this solution is not optimized and the execution time may increase with large inputs.

