Here is a function written in C:
```C
#include <stdio.h>

void interchange(int *p, int *q) {
    // checking either or both are 0
    if (!*p || !*q) {
        printf("One or both of the values are zero!\n");
        return;
    }    

    printf("Original values: x = %d, y = %d\n", *p, *q);

    // bitwise operation (XOR)
    *p = *p ^ *q;  
    *q = *p ^ *q;
    *p = *p ^ *q;

    printf("After interchange: x = %d, y = %d\n", *p, *q);
}

int main() {
    int x = 1, y = 2;
    int *p = &x, *q = &y;

    interchange(p, q);

    return 0;
}
```
This code defines a function `interchange` which will take in pointers to two integers. This function first checks if either of the integers are 0. If so, it will print a message and stop the working. If neither is zero, it will perform bitwise interchange (XOR operation). The main function demonstrates the use of this `interchange` function with integers `x = 1` and `y = 2`.

Note that XOR is a bitwise operation that uses the binary representations of the integers. It compares each bit of the first number with the corresponding bit of the second number. If the bits are both zeroes or both ones, it gives 0. If one bit is 0 and the other bit is 1, it returns 1.

The XOR operation is used here three times to swap the values of `*p` and `*q` without using a temporary variable. It's a common trick in lower-level programming.

