It uses two colors and fonts for the different states, but you can customize them as you wish.

```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

// Initial theme state stored in browser's local storage
const initialThemeState = window.localStorage.getItem('themeToggle') === 'true' ? true : false;

function DynamicThemeToggleSwitchComponent() {
  // State of the switch
  const [isToggledOn, setIsToggledOn] = useState(initialThemeState);

  // Color and font theme state based on the current state of the switch
  const theme = isToggledOn ? { backgroundColor: 'blue', fontFamily: 'Arial' } : { backgroundColor: 'red', fontFamily: 'Times New Roman' }

  // Toggle the switch state
  const toggle = () => setIsToggledOn(!isToggledOn);

  // Save the state of the switch in local storage whenever the state changes
  useEffect(() => {
    window.localStorage.setItem('themeToggle', isToggledOn);
  }, [isToggledOn]);

  return (
    <div style={theme}>
      <button onClick={toggle}>
        {isToggledOn ? 'ON' : 'OFF'}
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default DynamicThemeToggleSwitchComponent;
```

With this component, every time you click the button, the state of the switch is toggled between ON and OFF and the theme is correspondingly varied. Additionally, the state of the switch is saved in the browser's local storage, so if you refresh the page, the switch will remain in the same state.

