Luhn algorithm is a simple checksum formula used to validate a variety of identification numbers, most notably credit card numbers. It checks the credit card number with simple multiplication and adding operations.

Here is the java function that validates a credit card number implementing the Luhn algorithm:

```java
public class CreditCardValidator {
    public static boolean validate(String cardNo) {
        int[] ints = new int[cardNo.length()];
        for (int i = 0; i < cardNo.length(); i++) {
            ints[i] = Integer.parseInt(cardNo.substring(i, i + 1));
        }
        for (int i = ints.length - 2; i >= 0; i = i - 2) {
            int j = ints[i];
            j = j * 2;
            if (j > 9) {
                j = j % 10 + 1;
            }
            ints[i] = j;
        }
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < ints.length; i++) {
            sum += ints[i];
        }
        if (sum % 10 == 0) {
            System.out.println(cardNo + " is a valid credit card number");
            return true;
        } else {
            System.out.println(cardNo + " is an invalid credit card number");
            return false;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        validate("4532015112830366"); //example credit card number
    }
}
```
