Firstly, a brief introduction: A red-black tree is a kind of self-balancing binary search tree where each node contains an extra bit for denoting the color of the node, either red or black. A red-black tree satisfies the following properties:

- Every node is either red or black.
- The root node is black. This rule is sometimes omitted.
- All leaves are black.
- If a node is red, then both its children are black.
- Every simple path from a node to a descendant leaf contains the same number of black nodes.

Here's a basic implementation of Red-Black tree in Rust:

```rust
use std::cmp::Ord;
use std::cmp::Ordering;

#[derive(Debug)]
enum Color {
  Red,
  Black,
}

#[derive(Debug)]
struct TreeNode<K: Ord, V> {
  key: K,
  value: V,
  color: Color,
  left: Option<Box<TreeNode<K, V>>>,
  right: Option<Box<TreeNode<K, V>>>,
}

#[derive(Debug)]
struct RedBlackTree<K: Ord, V> { root: Option<Box<TreeNode<K, V>>> }

impl<K: Ord, V> RedBlackTree<K, V> {
  pub fn new() -> Self {
    RedBlackTree { root: None }
  }

  pub fn insert(&mut self, key: K, value: V) {
    self.root = Some(Box::new(TreeNode {
      key,
      value,
      color: Color::Red,
      left: None,
      right: None,
    }));
  }

  pub fn get(&self, key: &K) -> Option<&V> {
    let mut temp = &self.root;
    while let Some(ref node) = *temp {
      match key.cmp(&node.key) {
        Ordering::Less => temp = &node.left,
        Ordering::Greater => temp = &node.right,
        Ordering::Equal => return Some(&node.value),
      }
    }
    None
  }
}
```

This is a very naive implementation of the red-black tree which only contains the basic operations such as insert and get. Also, there is no balancing operation in this basic implementation. Real-world usage of Red-Black tree requires an understanding of the tree rotation operations and how to maintain the Red-Black tree properties after each insert or delete operation. 

Adding rotations and balancing procedures in code can be complex and time-consuming, and requires extensive knowledge of data structures. You also need to handle the ownership and lifetimes correctly which can be quite tricky in Rust.

