This solution is going to involve creating a stack and an algorithm for processing mathematical expressions in reverse Polish (postfix) notation.

```python
class Stack:
    def __init__(self):
        self.list = []

    def push(self, value):
        self.list.append(value)

    def pop(self):
        return self.list.pop()

    def is_empty(self):
        return len(self.list) == 0


def calculate(expression):
    stack = Stack()
    for token in expression:
        if token in ["*", "/", "+", "-"]:
            num2 = stack.pop()
            num1 = stack.pop()
            if token == "*":
                stack.push(num1 * num2)
            elif token == "/":
                if num2 == 0:
                    raise ValueError("Division by zero.")
                stack.push(num1 / num2)
            elif token == "+":
                stack.push(num1 + num2)
            elif token == "-":
                stack.push(num1 - num2)
        else:
            stack.push(token)
    return stack.pop()
```

This program works on expressions in reverse Polish notation (RPN), also known as postfix notation. RPN is a mathematical notation in which every operator follows all of its operands. It is parenthesis-free as long as the operators have a fixed number of operands.

To use this program, we need to pass in a list of tokens in an expression in RPN. For example, `calculate([5, 3, "*", 2, "/"])` returns 7.5. Note that it applies the BODMAS/PEMDAS rule inherently, because the way RPN works, it naturally respects this rule, based on the order of the operations given in the expression. It doesn't handle parenthesis because the usage of RPN avoids the need for parenthesis altogether.

Please take into account that the aforementioned solution for evaluating mathematical expressions is quite simplified and would need many enhancements for more real-world usage, including handling different input formats, adding support for functions and other mathematical constructs, error reporting and recovery and so on.

Here is a simple PHP recursive function to display Fibonacci series to the nth term.

```php
<?php

function fibonacci($n)
{
    if ($n < 0 || $n > 100) {
        return "Error: n must range from 0 to 100";
    }

    if ($n == 0) {
        return 0;
    }

    if ($n == 1) {
        return 1;
    }

    return fibonacci($n - 1) + fibonacci($n - 2);
}

for ($i = 0; $i <= 10; $i++) {
    echo fibonacci($i) . "\n";
}

?>
```
Note: Recursive implementations of this kind are very inefficient as they perform a lot of redundant calculations. Using other methods like memoization or simply using an iterative approach would be much more efficient.

Please, be aware of that the given solution prints Fibonacci numbers until nth term, but considering a number range 0 - 100 as a position in the Fibonacci sequence, not as a limit or something else. It means that it will return the 100th Fibonacci number and not the Fibonacci numbers up to or less than 100.

