Here is a simple way to implement the insertion sort algorithm in Java:

```java
public class InsertionSort {

    public static void main(String a[]){    
        int[] myArray  = {10,5,25,3,1,9};
        System.out.println("Before Insertion Sort");    
        printArray(myArray);

        insertionSort(myArray);          
        System.out.println("After Insertion Sort");    
        printArray(myArray);   
    }    

    //method to sort array using insertion sort
    public static void insertionSort(int array[]) {  
        int n = array.length;  
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {  
            int key = array[i];  
            int j = i-1;  

            while ( (j > -1) && ( array[j] > key ) ) {  
                array[j+1] = array[j];  
                j--;  
            }  
            array[j+1] = key;  
        }  
    } 

    //method to print the array
    public static void printArray(int[] array) {  
        for(int i=0; i < array.length; i++){  
            System.out.print(array[i] + " ");  
        } 
        System.out.println();  
    }  
}  
```

This program sorts an array of integers using the insertion sort algorithm. The array is sorted in place, meaning that no additional space is used to perform the sort. The sorting part of the program works by iterating over the array, and for each element, shifting all of the larger elements to the right to make space, and then inserting the element into the correct position. At the end of each iteration, all elements to the left of the current element are sorted. At the end of the loop, the entire array is sorted.

