You can use the array method indexOf() to find the index of a specific element in JavaScript. Here we assume that all elements in the array are unique.

Here's a simple implementation:

```javascript
function findArrayIndex(array, target) {
  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
    if (array[i] === target) {
      return i;
    }
  }
  return -1;
}

console.log(findArrayIndex([5,7,10,15,20], 15)); // outputs: 3
```

This is a linear approach with a worst-case scenario (when the target is the last element in the array or not present at all) needing to iterate over all the elements. So the time complexity is O(n).

But if you have an array of significant length with a mixture of data types and you're looking for the most efficient way, you may want to index the array elements first, and create a dictionary or map of elements to their indices. This is particularly suitable for when you have repetitive read operations. Here's an implementation using a Map object in JavaScript:

```javascript
function createIndexMap(array) {
  let indexMap = new Map();
  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
    indexMap.set(array[i], i);
  }
  return indexMap;
}

let myArray = [5, "hello", true, 15, {name: "test"}, 20];
let idxMap = createIndexMap(myArray);

console.log(idxMap.get(true)); // outputs: 2
```

This approach requires extra space (O(n)), plus an extra pass over the array initially (O(n)), but each search afterward operates in constant time (O(1)), making it very scalable for large arrays and repeated look-ups. Please bear in mind that JavaScript Map keys are compared by value and not by reference, this can lead to unexpected behaviours when complex types (Objects) are used as keys.

