To solve this problem, we are going to use a combination of techniques involving a Hash table and a doubly linked list which will allow us to establish both LRU and frequency count in the same data structure.

We will need two data structures to implement the `LRUCache`.

1. A `Doubly Linked List` with nodes that contain `Key`,`Value` and `Count`.
2. A `HashMap` of `Keys` and `Nodes` of the list.

A doubly linked list is chosen because the removal operation is `O(1)` for a doubly linked list. We can remove the node directly without iterating through the list.

However, looking up a node in a singly linked list is `O(n)`. To overcome this, we do not `get` or `lookup` in the list directly. We keep a hashmap of keys of nodes in the doubly linked list. The value will be the actual node in the linked list.

The least recently used node is always at the last of the linked list. When a node is accessed (through `get` or `put`), we will move it to the front of the list.

The frequency count of each key is stored as a `Count` in the node. Each time a node is accessed (through `get` or `put`), we will increment the count.

Here is the pseudocode for the same:

```text
class Node {
    int key
    int value
    int frequencyCount = 1
    Node next
    Node prev
}

class LRUCache {
    int capacity
    HashMap<Integer, Node> map
    Node head
    Node tail

    void put(int key, int value) {
        if (map.containsKey(key)) {
            Node node = map.get(key)
            node.value = value
            node.frequencyCount += 1
            moveToFront(node)
        } else {
            if (map.size() == capacity) {
                removeNode(tail)
                map.remove(tail.key)
            }
            Node newNode = new Node(key, value)
            addToFront(newNode)
            map.put(key, newNode)
        }
    }

    int get(int key) {
        if (map.containsKey(key)) {
            Node node = map.get(key)
            node.frequencyCount += 1
            moveToFront(node)
            return node.value
        } else {
            return -1
        }
    }

    int getFrequency(int key) {
        if (map.containsKey(key)) {
            Node node = map.get(key)
            return node.frequencyCount
        } else {
            return -1
        }
    }

    void removeNode(Node node) {
        if (node.prev != null) {
            node.prev.next = node.next
        } else {
            head = node.next
        }

        if (node.next != null) {
            node.next.prev = node.prev
        } else {
            tail = node.prev
        }
    }

    void addToFront(Node node) {
        node.next = head
        node.prev = null
        if (head != null) {
            head.prev = node
        }
        head = node
        if (tail == null) {
            tail = node
        }
    }

    void moveToFront(Node node) {
        removeNode(node)
        addToFront(node)
    }
}
```

By using both a doubly linked list and a hash map, we achieve `O(1)` time complexity for `get`, `put` and `getFrequency` operations.

