The following C++ solution uses the `std::find` function from the `<algorithm>` library. It checks if a given substring is present in the main string. If it is, it then also checks if any instances of the substring overlap.

```C++
#include<stdio.h>
#include<string>
#include<algorithm> // Include the algorithm library
using namespace std;

bool is_enclosed_by(string str, string substring){
    size_t found = str.find(substring);  // find substring in str
    if(found != string::npos){
        // Check for overlapping instances
        while (found != string::npos)
        {
            found = str.find(substring, found+1); 
            if(found != string::npos and found < (str.length() - substring.length() + 1)){
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;  // return true if no overlapping instance is found
    } 
    else 
        return false;  // return false if substring is not present in str
}
```

This function returns true if the substring is present within the string and false otherwise. If the substrings overlap, the program returns false. 

The `std::find` function returns the position of the first occurrence of the substring in the string. If it doesn't find the substring, it returns `string::npos`.

In `found = str.find(substring, found+1);` line, `found+1` is used as the second argument to function `find()` in order to begin the next search from the character following the beginning of the current match for `substring`.

The `if(found != string::npos and found < (str.length() - substring.length() + 1))` line checks whether the substring overlaps with itself in the main string.

Importantly, this function will only return true if:
- The substring is present within the string, and
- It does not overlap with itself in any instances. 
Otherwise, it will return false.

