```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>

int main(){
    double a, b, c, discriminant, realPart, imaginaryPart;

    printf("Enter coefficients a, b and c: ");
    scanf("%lf %lf %lf", &a, &b, &c);

    discriminant = b * b - 4 * a * c;

    // condition for real and different roots
    if (discriminant > 0)
    {
        // sqrt() function returns the square root
        realPart = (-b + sqrt(discriminant)) / (2 * a);
        imaginaryPart = (-b - sqrt(discriminant)) / (2 * a);
        printf("Roots are %.2lf and %.2lf", realPart, imaginaryPart);
    }

    //condition for real and equal roots
    else if (discriminant == 0)
    {
        realPart = -b / (2 * a);
        printf("Roots are %.2lf and %.2lf", realPart, realPart);
    }

    // if roots are not real 
    else
    {
        realPart = -b / (2 * a);
        imaginaryPart = sqrt(-discriminant) / (2 * a);
        printf("Roots are %.2lf+%.2lfi and %.2lf-%.2lfi", realPart, imaginaryPart, realPart, imaginaryPart);
    }

    return 0;
}  
```
This program takes in three values (coefficients for the quadratic equation) as inputs and then calculates the discriminant. 

Based on the value of the discriminant, we classify the roots into three types:
1. Real and different roots if discriminant > 0
2. Real and same roots if discriminant = 0
3. Complex roots if discriminant < 0

And then, calculate the roots accordingly. The sqrt() function is used to find the square root of the discriminant. 

The resultant roots (either real or complex) are printed out as the solution for the given quadratic equation.

