Considering the size and complexity of developing a comprehensive testing framework, this will only be a brief overview of how to design a behavior-driven development (BDD) testing framework using Protractor and Jasmine for a cryptocurrency trading platform. The key elements will be described, but the actual full implementation requires much more detailed coding, which goes beyond the scope of this platform.

### 1. Initialize Project

Assuming you have Node.js already installed, install Protractor globally on your machine using npm:

```sh
npm install -g protractor
```

Also, make sure you have the Jasmine package install as well:

```sh
npm install -g jasmine
```

### 2. Set up Protractor Configuration

Create a file named `protractor.conf.js` which will hold the configuration for the protractor. Here's a basic configuration:

```js
exports.config = {
  directConnect: true,
  framework: 'jasmine',
  specs: ['specs/*.js'],
  capabilities: {
    browserName: 'chrome'
  }
};
```

### 3. Write BDD Tests

Under a directory named `specs`, write your tests in separate JavaScript files. Here's a sample of a BDD test in Jasmine for testing a login feature:

```js
describe('Cryptocurrency Trading Platform', function() {
  var usernameInput = element(by.model('user.username'));
  var passwordInput = element(by.model('user.password'));
  var loginButton = element(by.id('login-button'));

  beforeEach(function() {
    browser.get('https://example.com/login');
  });

  it('should log in with correct credentials', function() {
    usernameInput.sendKeys('valid_username');
    passwordInput.sendKeys('valid_password');
    loginButton.click();

    expect(element(by.css('.welcome-message')).getText())
      .toEqual('Welcome, valid_username');
  });

  it('should fail to log in with wrong credentials', function() {
    usernameInput.sendKeys('invalid_username');
    passwordInput.sendKeys('invalid_password');
    loginButton.click();

    expect(element(by.css('.error-message')).isPresent()).toBe(true);
  });
});
```

This test suite contains two expectations. The first one expects successful login when supplying correct credentials and the second one is checking for an error message presence when supplying invalid credentials.

Similar test suites would be set up for other core features like a trading feature, a portfolio feature, deposit and withdrawal features, etc.

### 4. Run the Tests
Finally, run the test suite with the `protractor` command:

```sh
protractor protractor.conf.js
``` 

If everything is set up correctly, Protractor will run all tests described in `specs/*.js` files and provide a summary of the test run.

Remember to write tests separately for various features and scenarios to ensure comprehensive coverage of the application.

