Creating a React/Redux application that fetches color data from a web API involves a several steps. Below is a rough sample of how it could be accomplished:

1. First, you will need to set up your React application. You can use `create-react-app` for quick setup.

In your terminal, run:

```
npx create-react-app random-color-fetcher
```

2. Install Redux and React-Redux:

```
npm install redux react-redux
```

Also, you'll likely be making asynchronous API calls, so also install redux-thunk:

```
npm install redux-thunk
```

3. Set up reducers in Redux.

Here's a simple example to get you started:
```jsx
// reducers/colorReducer.js

const colorReducer = (state = [], action) => {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'FETCH_COLORS':
      return action.payload;
    default:
      return state;
  }
};

export default colorReducer;
```

4. Set up your Actions:

Here's a simple action that fetches data from a placeholder API:

```jsx
// actions/fetchColors.js

import axios from 'axios';

export const fetchColors = () => async dispatch => {
  const response = await axios.get('https://www.mockapi.com/colors/'); // Use your API endpoint here

  dispatch({ type: 'FETCH_COLORS', payload: response.data });
};
```

5. Now setup the store:

```jsx
//store.js

import { createStore, applyMiddleware } from 'redux';
import thunk from 'redux-thunk';
import colorReducer from './reducers/colorReducer.js';

const store = createStore(
  colorReducer,
  applyMiddleware(thunk)
);

export default store;
```

6. Now let's set up the React components:

```jsx
// components/ColorDisplay.js

import React from 'react';
import { connect } from 'react-redux';
import { fetchColors } from '../actions/fetchColors';

class ColorDisplay extends React.Component {
  componentDidMount() {
    this.props.fetchColors();
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        {this.props.colors.map(color => (<div key={color.id} style={{backgroundColor: `#${color.hexcode}`}}></div>))}
      </div>
    );
  }
}

const mapStateToProps = state => {
  return { colors: state };
};

export default connect(
  mapStateToProps,
  { fetchColors }
)(ColorDisplay);
```

7. Wrap your App with the provider:

```jsx
// App.js

import React from 'react';
import { Provider } from 'react-redux';
import store from './store';
import ColorDisplay from './components/ColorDisplay';

function App() {
  return (
    <Provider store={store}>
      <div>
        <ColorDisplay />
      </div>
    </Provider>
  );
}

export default App;
```

8. This application will fetch color data from the given API (make sure it returns an array of objects containing id and color hex code) when `ColorDisplay` component is mounted and displays these colors by assigning them as background colors of div elements.

9. For simplicity, this application is storing the colors data directly in the state without normalizing it. For a larger application, you would want to normalize your state shape. Make sure to adjust the reducer and mapStatetoProps accordingly.

Please replace the domain 'https://www.mockapi.com/colors/' with your actual API.

This is a simplified example; based on your requirements, your real-world application may be more complex.
